\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the integer lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,2,3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(P_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,y=2,y=3\\) cover all six points.  \n* \\(k=2\\): take the sunny line \\(L_1:\\;y=2x-1\\) (which contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\) \u2013 the latter is not in \\(P_3\\) but the line also contains \\((2,1)\\)), together with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=3\\). Every point of \\(P_3\\) lies on at least one of these three lines.  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+2,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=x\n  \\]\n  contain respectively the point sets \\(\\{(1,1),(2,3)\\},\\{(2,1),(4,0)\\},\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\}\\); their union covers \\(P_3\\).\n\nIt remains to show that \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  Suppose we have one sunny line \\(S\\) and two non\u2011sunny lines \\(N_1,N_2\\).  Each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(P_3\\) (all points with a fixed \\(x\\), a fixed \\(y\\) or a fixed \\(x+y\\)).  Hence the three lines together contain at most \\(3+3+2=8\\) incidences, but a single sunny line can meet at most two points of \\(P_3\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  Consequently the total number of distinct points covered is at most \\(3+3+2-1=7\\), which is insufficient for the six points of \\(P_3\\).  A direct inspection shows that no choice of one sunny line can cover three points of \\(P_3\\); therefore at most five points can be covered, contradicting the requirement.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen for \\(n+1\\) the admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{n+1}=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **From \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) to \\(\\mathcal K_{n+1}\\) with the same \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) be given.  Add the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\;y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The new line is non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  \n   The points that are not already covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and each of them lies on \\(H\\).  Hence we obtain a valid configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n2. **Increasing the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\ge 2\\) and suppose a configuration for \\((n,k-1)\\) exists.  \n   Choose a slope \\(\\sigma\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of all existing sunny lines (there are only finitely many such slopes, so this is possible).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\; y-\\,(n+1)=\\sigma\\bigl(x-1\\bigr)\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   be the line of slope \\(\\sigma\\) passing through the new point \\((1,n+1)\\).  \n   Add \\(S\\) to the configuration of step\u202f1.  The line \\(S\\) is sunny, and it covers the point \\((1,n+1)\\) which was not on the horizontal line \\(H\\); all other new points are already on \\(H\\).  Thus we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=1\\) for \\(n+1\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration for \\((n+1,1)\\) existed.  Then we would have one sunny line \\(S\\) and \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  The non\u2011sunny lines are parallel to one of the three directions \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\ge 2\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   of them share the same direction; assume they are horizontal (the other cases are analogous).  All points of \\(P_{n+1}\\) with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate lie on a single horizontal line, so the \\(m\\) horizontal lines can cover at most \\(m\\) distinct \\(y\\)-values.  Since the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of possible \\(y\\)-coordinates has size \\(n+1>m\\), at least one value \\(b_0\\) is not represented by any horizontal line.  Consequently the points \\((a,b_0)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+2-b_0\\) must be covered by either the sunny line \\(S\\) or by vertical/diagonal lines.  A vertical line can contain at most one such point, and a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most one as well (because the \\(y\\)-coordinates differ).  Hence at most two of the required points with \\(y=b_0\\) can be covered, leaving at least one uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nCombining 1\u20133 we obtain (3). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe statement is true for \\(n=3\\) by Lemma\u202f1.  Lemma\u202f2 shows that if it holds for some \\(n\\) then it holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction the claim follows for all \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll values \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable except \\(k=1\\); i.e. the admissible values are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad 2\\le k\\le n .\n\\]"
