(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines, each parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle at least \\(\\lceil n/3\\rceil\\ge 2\\) of them share the same direction; assume they are horizontal (the other cases are symmetric).  Horizontal lines can cover at most one point for each distinct \\(y\\)-coordinate.  Since the set of possible \\(y\\)-coordinates in \\(P_{n+1}\\) is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(n+1\\) and we have at most \\(\\lceil n/3\\rceil\\) horizontal lines, at least one value \\(b_0\\) is not represented by any horizontal line.  All points with \\(y=b_0\\) must therefore be covered by the sunny line \\(S\\) or by vertical/diagonal lines.  A vertical line can contain at most one such point, and a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can also contain at most one because the \\(y\\)-coordinates of points on a fixed diagonal are all distinct.  Hence at most two of the required points with \\(y=b_0\\) can be covered, leaving at least one uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=1\\) is impossible for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe induction establishes the claimed set of admissible \\(k\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe required condition is that every point of \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) given lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base case \\(n=3\\))\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) for \\(n=3\\) are exactly \\(0,2,3\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(P_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  \nThe three horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad y=2,\\qquad y=3\n\\]\n\ncover all six points, and none of them is sunny.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=2x-1\n\\]\n\n(which contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); the latter is not in \\(P_3\\) but the line also contains \\((2,1)\\)).  \nAdd the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(y=2\\) and \\(y=3\\).  One checks directly that every point of (1) lies on at least one of these three lines.  Exactly two lines (\\(L_1\\) and one of the horizontals) are sunny.\n\n*Construction for \\(k=3\\).*  \nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=2x-1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+2,\\\\\nL_3 &: y=x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir point sets intersect \\(P_3\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Line} & \\text{Points of }P_3\\text{ on it}\\\\ \\hline\nL_1 & (1,1),\\;(2,1)\\\\\nL_2 & (2,1),\\;(1,2)\\\\\nL_3 & (1,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,3)\\ (\\text{only }(1,1),(2,2)\\in P_3)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nTogether they cover all six points of (1).  All three lines are sunny.\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=1\\).*  \nAssume there is a configuration with exactly one sunny line \\(S\\) and two non\u2011sunny lines \\(N_1,N_2\\).  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the directions \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line can contain at most three points of \\(P_3\\) (all points sharing the same \\(x\\), the same \\(y\\) or the same sum \\(x+y\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|N_1\\cap P_3|\\le 3,\\qquad |N_2\\cap P_3|\\le 3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to any of those three directions, therefore it can contain **at most two** points of \\(P_3\\); otherwise two of its points would have equal \\(x\\), equal \\(y\\) or equal \\(x+y\\), forcing the slope to be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\n|S\\cap P_3|\\le 2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf the three lines were all distinct, the total number of points of \\(P_3\\) covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n3+3+2=8 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the three lines may intersect, the actual number of *distinct* points covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n3+3+2-1=7 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|P_3|=6\\); the only way to cover all six points would be to have exactly six distinct incidences, which would require \\(|S\\cap P_3|=2\\) and the two non\u2011sunny lines to be disjoint on \\(P_3\\).  A direct inspection of all possibilities shows that any sunny line meeting two points of \\(P_3\\) must have slope either \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the only pairs of points in \\(P_3\\) sharing a common \\(x\\), \\(y\\) or \\(x+y\\) are \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\), \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\)), contradicting the definition of sunny.  Hence a sunny line can meet **at most one** point of \\(P_3\\), and the total number of covered points is at most \\(3+3+1=7\\) with at least one overlap, i.e. at most six distinct points, but then one of the six points of \\(P_3\\) remains uncovered.  Therefore no configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for \\(n=3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Inductive step)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThen for \\(n+1\\) the admissible set is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{n+1}=\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nLet a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) be given, i.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(i) Preserving the number of sunny lines.**  \n\nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) is non\u2011sunny and is not parallel to any existing line because none of the existing lines is horizontal (otherwise it would already have been counted among the non\u2011sunny lines, but adding another horizontal line does not create a parallelism conflict).  The new points that appear in \\