s exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### Verification of the covering condition\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S\\). By definition \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(b=1\\).  \n  Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \n  Moreover \\(a\\ge 1\\).  \n\n  *If \\(a=1\\):* then \\(b\\le n\\) and the equality \\(a+b=n+1\\) holds exactly when \\(b=n\\).  \n  Hence \\((1,n)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n  *If \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\):* the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) because its \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfy both required conditions, and none of them is sunny. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis always attainable for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{, the value }k=0\\text{ can be realized.}}\n\\]\n\n*The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\) remains unresolved by the present work.*"
