\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_{i}\\supseteq S\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three directions\n   \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Construction with \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following family of lines:\n\n* **Vertical lines** (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis):\n  \\[\n  V_{j}:\\;x=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n  \\]\n  There are \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines.\n\n* **Horizontal line** (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis):\n  \\[\n  H:\\;y=1 .\n  \\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{V_{j}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{H\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L|=(n-1)+1=n\\); all lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence **none** of them is sunny, i.e. the configuration has \\(k=0\\).\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in S\\). By definition \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\), the integer \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (which has equation \\(x=a\\)).\n\nThus every \\((a,b)\\in S\\) is covered, and the required configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Non\u2011existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line. Let \\(L\\) be such a sunny line. By definition, the slope of \\(L\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \