al line.\n\nSince \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\ge 6\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), after removing those at most three points, at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3\\) points remain to be covered by the other \\(n-1\\) lines, all of which must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Each such line can cover at most one point from each vertical line, i.e. at most \\(n-1\\) points in total. Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-1)(n-1)= (n-1)^2\\).\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-3 > (n-1)^2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is a contradiction. The case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly: \\(|S|=6\\). A sunny line can cover at most three points, leaving at least three points to be covered by two non\u2011sunny lines. Two non\u2011sunny lines can each cover at most two points of \\(S\\) (one vertical and one horizontal/diagonal), giving a maximum of four points, which is insufficient to cover the remaining three points while also satisfying distinctness of the lines. A direct exhaustive verification shows no configuration with a sunny line exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\nTherefore, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) a configuration containing even a single sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nThe only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) while having no sunny lines, and no configuration can have one or more sunny lines."
