ontributed no line, then every point with the same \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate would have to be covered by a distinct line (either from \\(\\mathcal V\\) or sunny). Since there are \\(n\\) distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates among the points \\(P_i\\), this would require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving no room for the remaining points of the triangle \u2013 a contradiction. The same reasoning applies to \\(\\mathcal V\\) and \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\n6. **Deriving a contradiction from the existence of a sunny line.**  \n   Because at least one sunny line is present, at most two of the three families can be used. Suppose, without loss of generality, that the families \\(\\mathcal H\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\) are the only non\u2011sunny families employed (the case with \\(\\mathcal D\\) is analogous).  \n\n   *Each horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most one of the points \\(P_i\\)* (they have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates), and similarly each vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most one of the \\(P_i\\). Hence to cover the \\(n\\) points \\(P_i\\) we need at least \\(n\\) lines from \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\{\\text{sunny lines}\\}\\).  \n\n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines in total and at least one of them is sunny, the number of lines taken from \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\) is at most \\(n-1\\). Consequently at most \\(n-1\\) of the points \\(P_i\\) can be covered, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 contradicting the covering requirement.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The assumption that a sunny line exists leads to a contradiction; therefore no sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration. Hence \\(k\\) must be \\(0\\).\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on the given family of lines.\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}\n\\]\n\nthe three families of *non\u2011sunny* lines (parallel respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\nA line is called **sunny** if it belongs to none of the three families.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal D\\); hence they are non\u2011sunny.\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\).\nThus the \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) cover every point of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of any sunny line (\\(k\\ge 1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least one sunny line.\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nPartition \\(\\mathcal L\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal H'\\cup\\mathcal V'\\cup\\mathcal D'\\cup\\mathcal S,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal H'=\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal H\\), \\(\\mathcal V'=\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal V\\), \\(\\mathcal D'=\\mathcal L\\cap\\mathcal D\\) and \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the set of sunny lines.\nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal V'|+|\\mathcal D'|+|\\mathcal S|=n,\\qquad |\\mathcal S|=k\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### The \u201cborder\u201d points\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll of them belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\).\nMoreover they lie on the single line \\(x+y=n+1\\