cal D\\).\nTherefore **at most one** of the families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) can be missing from the configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Deriving the contradiction\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\ge 1\\), at least one family must be missing; otherwise we would have at most three non\u2011sunny families and could use all three, leaving no room for a sunny line (the total would be at least \\(n+1\\) lines, contradicting the given number \\(n\\)).\nConsequently exactly one of the families is absent.\nWithout loss of generality assume that \\(\\mathcal D'\\) is empty, i.e. no line of slope \\(-1\\) is used.\n\nFrom the discussion of the points \\(P_i\\) we know that each of them must be covered by a horizontal line, a vertical line, or a sunny line.\nBecause the \\(y\\)-coordinates of the \\(P_i\\) are all distinct, a horizontal line can contain at most one of them; similarly a vertical line can contain at most one.\nA sunny line also contains at most one of the \\(P_i\\) (different slopes cannot pass through two points whose coordinates sum to the same constant).\n\nHence covering the \\(n\\) points \\(P_i\\) requires at least \\(n\\) distinct lines taken from \\(\\mathcal H'\\cup\\mathcal V'\\cup\\mathcal S\\).\nBut by (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal V'|+|\\mathcal S| = n-|\\mathcal D'| = n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal S|\\ge 1\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal V'| \\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the total number of lines available to cover the \\(P_i\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n(|\\mathcal H'|+|\\mathcal V'|)+|\\mathcal S|\\le (n-1)+|\\mathcal S| = n-1+ k < n ,\n\\]\n\nbecause each sunny line can cover **only one** of the points \\(P_i\\).\nConsequently at most \\(n-1\\) of the points \\(P_i\\) can be covered, leaving at least one point of \\(T_n\\) uncovered\u2014a contradiction to the hypothesis that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThe contradiction stems from the assumption that a sunny line exists.\nTherefore no sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration; i.e. \\(k\\) must be zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nThe only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThe construction with the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\) shows that this value is indeed attainable. No configuration containing a sunny line can meet the covering requirement, as proved above."
