ts must lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) that is either vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n  A vertical line can contain at most one of the points \\((i,1)\\) (because the \\(x\\)-coordinates are distinct), while a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one as well (its equation is \\(x+y=c\\) and the sums \\(i+1\\) are distinct).  \n\n  Hence each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) can cover **at most one** point of the set \\(\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).  \n  By the pigeon\u2011hole principle, to cover all \\(n\\) points we need at least \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \n  Therefore  \n  \\[\n  n-k\\ge n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Step\u202f3 \u2013 Consequences.**  \n  Lemma\u202f1 shows that the lower bound \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \n  Lemma\u202f2 shows that any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n  The gap between these two results (the existence of families with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)) has not been resolved in the present work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*Statement.* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, such that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\; x+y=c .\n\\]\nAll the lines \\(\\ell_c\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently they are **not** sunny.  \nThe set of points \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a+b=c\\) is precisely the intersection of the integer lattice with \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nSince the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to the existence of an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) such that \\(a+b=c\\), each admissible lattice point belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThus the collection  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers all required points. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*Statement.* Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). If \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, then the point \\((1,1)\\) cannot be covered; consequently any admissible family must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) where \\(\\mathcal S\\) is the set of sunny lines and \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy hypothesis \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\ge n-1\\), so \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\le 1\\).\n\nA sunny line is, by definition, **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\n* horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)),\n* vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)),\n* slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\nHence a sunny line can intersect the bottom row of the triangle,\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\nin at most one point, because any two distinct points of \\(R\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different sums \\(i+1\\); a line that is not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\) cannot pass through two such points.\n\nConsequently each sunny line covers **at most one** element of \\(R\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|\\le 1\\), then the total number of points of \\(R\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S|+|\\mathcal N|\\le (n-1)+1=n .\n\\]\nBut \\(|R|=n\\); to cover **all** points of \\(R\\) we would need exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each covering a different point of \\(R\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal N|=0\\) (i.e. all lines are sunny), then each line covers at most one point of \\(R\\), so we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines. This would force \\(|\\mathcal S|=n\\), contradicting the assumption \\(|\\mathcal S|\\ge n-1\\) together with \