,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_c\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThe line \\(\\ell_c\\) contains precisely the lattice points with \\(a+b=c\\).  \nSince every point \\((a,b)\\in P\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), it lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nHence the family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) satisfies the required covering property and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be fixed with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Choice of abscissae for sunny lines.**  \n   Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) contains at least \\(k\\) elements.  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   s_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Definition of the sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) pick a slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha_i:=\\frac{1}{i+1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i\\,(x-s_i).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((s_i,1)\\) and, by the choice of \\(\\alpha_i\\), is sunny.  \n   Distinct pairs \\((s_i,\\alpha_i)\\) give distinct lines, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines are pairwise different.\n\n3. **Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines for the remaining points of the bottom row.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because the \\(s_i\\) lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), the set \\(T\\) has exactly \\(n-k\\) elements.  \n   For each \\(t\\in T\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_t:\\;x=t .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_t\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and the lines \\(\\{V_t\\mid t\\in T\\}\\) are distinct.\n\n4. **Verification that all points of \\(P\\) are covered.**\n\n   *Bottom row \\(y=1\\).*  \n   By construction, the point \\((s_