11sunny.  Using the above bounds we obtain an upper bound for the\n  total number of covered points:\n  \\[\n  \\underbrace{k\\cdot\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n  \\;+\\;\n  \\underbrace{(n-k)\\cdot n}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}}\n  \\;\\ge\\; N=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n* Solving inequality (1) for \\(k\\) yields\n  \\[\n  k\\le n-2.\n  \\]\n\nThus any configuration satisfying the two requirements must have at most\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe converse direction (showing that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be\nrealised) remains open; it would require an explicit construction of the lines for\neach admissible \\(k\\).  This construction is not provided here.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on the given family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|S|= \\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction vector is not parallel to any of the\nvectors \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, it has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  The\ncondition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus the admissible values of\n\\(a\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) of them.  The same argument works\nfor a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  If the line has slope \\(-1\\) its equation is\n\\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 2\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(c\\le n+1\\), and the integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) of \\(a+b=c\\) are\n\\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), again at most \\(n\\) points.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and\n\\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  Suppose \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on the line; then\n\\(b=ma+b\\).  If \\((a',b')\\in S\\) is another lattice point on the same line,\nsubtracting the two equations gives\n\n\\[\nb'-b=m(a'-a).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\) is not an integer, the right\u2011hand side cannot be an integer unless\n\\(a'-a\\) is a multiple of the denominator of \\(m\\) when written in lowest terms.\nConsequently the difference \\(|a'-a|\\) is at least \\(2\\).  The same holds for the\ndifference in the \\(b\\)-coordinates.  Hence along the line the lattice points are\nspaced by at least two units in the \\(a\\)-direction (or equivalently in the\n\\(b\\)-direction).  The projection of the triangle onto the \\(a\\)-axis has length\n\\(n\\); therefore at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) lattice points of \\(S\\) can lie on\nthe line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nIf among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, then\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(k\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S\\) by\nLemma\u202f2, and let the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points by Lemma\u202f1.  Since every point of \\(S\\) must be covered, we have\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{(by (2)).}\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\\;\\le\\;n-2.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge 2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we deduce that for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) it is impossible to have a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with\n\\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  Hence any admissible value of \\(k\\) must satisfy\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations attaining every integer \\(k\\) in this interval\nhas not been established in the present argument.  Consequently the set of all\npossible \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \u220e"
