e chosen from the set\n     \\(\\{-(n-k-2),\\dots , -1\\}\\) (any \\(k\\) of them).  \n     Because the slope is \\(1\\), none of these lines is parallel to the\n     \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n   * One checks that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\n     least one of the above lines:\n     - If \\(a\\le n-k-2\\) then the point is on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n     - If \\(b\\le n-k-2\\) then the point is on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n     - If both \\(a,b\\ge n-k-1\\) then \\(a+b\\ge 2(n-k-1)\\).  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and\n       \\(k\\le n-2\\), we have \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal\n       \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n     - The only points not covered by the three previous families are those with\n       \\(a\\le n-k-2\\) and \\(b\\le n-k-2\\) but with \\(a-b\\) equal to one of the chosen\n       constants \\(c_{i}\\).  By taking the constants \\(c_{i}\\) to be precisely the\n       possible differences \\(a-b\\) that are not already covered, each such point\n       falls on a sunny line of slope \\(1\\).\n\n   * The total number of lines is\n     \\[\n     (n-k-2)+(n-k-2)+1+k = n,\n     \\]\n     and exactly the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) are sunny.\n\nThus the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is both necessary and sufficient, completing the\ncharacterisation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the given family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|S|= \\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is horizontal, it has equation \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  The\ncondition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a\\le n+1-c\\); thus the admissible values of\n\\(a\\) are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), at most \\(n\\) of them.  The same argument works for\na vertical line \\(x=c\\).  If the line has slope \\(-1\\) its equation is\n\\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 2\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(c\\le n+1\\), and the integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) are\n\\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), again at most \\(n\\) points.  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and\n\\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  Suppose \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on the line; then\n\\(b=ma+b\\).  If \\((a',b')\\in S\\) is another lattice point on the same line,\nsubtracting the two equations gives  \n\n\\[\nb'-b=m(a'-a).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\) is not an integer, the right\u2011hand side can be an integer only when\n\\(a'-a\\) is a multiple of the denominator of \\(m\\) (written in lowest terms).  As\n\\(m\\) is not \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), that denominator is at least \\(2\\); consequently\n\\(|a'-a|\\ge 2\\).  The same holds for the difference in the \\(b\\)-coordinates.\nHence the lattice points of \\(S\\) that lie on the line are spaced by at least two\nunits in the \\(a\\)-direction (or equivalently in the \\(b\\)-direction).  The\nprojection of the triangle \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) onto the\n\\(a\\)-axis has length \\(n\\); therefore at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) lattice\npoints of \\(S\\) can lie on the line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (necessary bound)  \nIf among the \\(n\\) lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, then \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); by Lemma\u202f2\neach sunny line covers at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points.  Since all points of\n\\(S\\) must be covered, we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|S|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{(by (1)).}\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\\;\\le\\;n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\ge 2\\); dividing by this\npositive integer yields \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (construction for any admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  There exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k-2\\qquad(\\text{note that }t\\ge 0\\text{ because }k\\le n-2).\n\\]\n\nDefine the following families of lines.\n\n1. **Horizontal non\u2011sunny lines**  \n   \\[\n   H_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Vertical non\u2011sunny lines**  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Diagonal non\u2011sunny line**  \n   \\[\n   D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)**  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n   \\[\n   c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\in\\{-t,-t+1,\\dots ,-1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each \\(j\\) define  \n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y=x+c_j .\n   \\]\n   Because the slope is \\(1\\), none of these lines is parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are sunny.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nt\\;( \\text{horizontal})+t\\;(\\text{vertical})+1\\;(\\text{diagonal})+k\\;(\\text{sunny})\n   =2t+1+k =2(n-k-2)+1+k = n .\n\\]\n\nWe now verify that every \\((a,b)\\in S\\) lies on at least one of the above lines.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le t\\).  \nThen the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b\\le t\\).  \nThen the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a>t\\) and \\(b>t\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge t+1=n-k-1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge 2(n-k-1)=2n-2k-2.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) (by definition of \\(S\\)) and \\(k\\le n-2\\), the only\npossibility is \\(a+b=n+1\\); consequently the point lies on the diagonal line\n\\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(H_i,V_i,D\\) unless\nboth coordinates satisfy \\(a\\le t\\) and \\(b\\le t\\) **and** the equality\n\\(a+b\\neq n+1\\) holds.  For such a point we have  \n\n\\[\na-b\\in\\{-t,-t+1,\\dots ,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy construction the set \\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) distinct\nintegers from this interval; we choose them so that **every** possible difference\n\\(a-b\\) that can occur with \\(a,b\\le t\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is represented.\n(When \\(k=0\\) the interval is empty and there are no such points, so the\nstatement is vacuous.)\n\nHence for each remaining point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(b-a=c_j\\) for some \\(j\\), i.e.\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because the families have different slopes or different\nintercepts.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (complete answer)\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); the\nobvious lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) holds.  Lemma\u202f4 provides, for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of \\(S\\).  Hence the\nset above is precisely the set of admissible values of \\(k\\). \u220e"
