nts must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nEach non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n+1-c\\rfloor\\) points of the set, because the\npossible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \nSimilarly a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most \\(\\lfloor n+1-c\\rfloor\\) points, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(\\lfloor c-1\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nSumming the maximal possible contributions of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines yields a total that is\nstrictly less than \\(T-k\\) unless at least two of the lines are non\u2011sunny. A short calculation\n(using the fact that the sum of the first \\(n\\) positive integers is \\(T\\)) shows that with only one\nnon\u2011sunny line we could cover at most \\(n\\) points, while \\(T-k\\ge T-(n-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+1>n\\) for\n\\(n\\ge3\\). Therefore at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \n*For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). Construct the following family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n1. **Vertical lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2-k\\) include the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; x=i .\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n2. **Lines of slope \\(-1\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) include the line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; x+y=n-j .\n   \\]\n\n   These are also non\u2011sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n3. **Two additional lines.**  \n   Add the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y=1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   and the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(H\\) is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis); the line \\(D\\) is non\u2011sunny as well.\n\nThus far we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-k) \\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{slope }-1)\\;+\\;2 = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines. All of them are non\u2011sunny, so at this stage \\(k=0\\).  \nTo obtain exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines we now **replace** the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(M_j\\) (the slope \\(-1\\) lines) by \\(k\\) sunny lines of distinct slopes, for instance\n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = \\bigl(j+2\\bigr)x + 1 \\qquad (0\\le j\\le k-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(j+2\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and is therefore sunny.  \nBecause the intercept is \\(1\\), the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on every \\(S_j\\); consequently the point\n\\((1,1)\\) is still covered. Moreover, for any integer \\(a\\ge 2\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the\nhorizontal line \\(H\\), and for any integer \\(b\\ge 2\\) the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) (which is present when \\(n-2-k\\ge 1\\); if \\(n-2-k=0\\) we use the line \\(x=2\\) together\nwith the sunny line \\(S_0\\) to cover \\((2,1)\\)).  \n\nFinally, the line \\(D: x+y=n+1\\) covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nAll remaining points satisfy either \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) and are therefore covered by the vertical\nor horizontal line already present. Hence **every** lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of the family.\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_j\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines,\nso it meets the required conditions. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are ruled out by Lemma\u202f1 and remain impossible. \u220e"
