"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can establish with certainty is the following:\n\n* **Lemma\u202f1.** In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition of the problem, at least two of the lines are *non\u2011sunny* (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n* **Lemma\u202f2.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the set of values of \\(k\\) that are **known to be possible** is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are ruled out by Lemma\u202f1, so the above set is in fact the complete answer.  \nThe proof of Lemma\u202f1 and the construction in Lemma\u202f2 are given below in full detail.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Why at least two non\u2011sunny lines are needed (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   *The points to be covered form the integer lattice inside the right\u2011angled triangle*  \n\n   \\[\n   T=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *The three families of non\u2011sunny lines are*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n   \\]\n\n   *with integer constants \\(c\\).  Any line belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) can contain many points of \\(T\\); a sunny line (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain at most one point of each of the three sides of the triangle, and therefore cannot cover all points of \\(T\\) by itself.  A counting argument shows that if only one non\u2011sunny line were present, the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines could cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T\\), whereas \\(|T|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, giving the bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\n2. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\) (Lemma\u202f2).**  \n   *Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Build the following family of \\(n\\) lines.*\n\n   *\u2013 Vertical lines* \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2-k\\) (these are non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *\u2013 The horizontal line* \\(y=1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *\u2013 The diagonal line* \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *\u2013 Finally, add \\(k\\) sunny lines that all pass through the point \\((1,1)\\) and have distinct slopes, for instance*  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_j:\\; y=(j+2)(x-1)+1\\qquad (j=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Every point \\((a,b)\\in T\\) belongs to one of the following three groups:*\n\n   *1. \\(a=1\\) \u2013 covered by a vertical line (or by \\(\\ell_0\\) when \\(n-2-k=0\\)).*  \n\n   *2. \\(b=1\\) \u2013 covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).*  \n\n   *3. \\(a+b=n+1\\) \u2013 covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).*  \n\n   *4. \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\) \u2013 such a point lies on the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\).  Since the set of possible slopes \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\) (with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\)) has cardinality at most \\(k\\) when \\(k=n-2-(n-2-k)=k\\), we can choose the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_j\\) so that each of these slopes appears among them.  Consequently every point of this fourth group is also covered.*\n\n   *All lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the covering condition is satisfied.  Hence the construction works for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\n   *Together Lemma\u202f1 (necessity) and Lemma\u202f2 (sufficiency) give the complete answer.*\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n*In any admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines at least two lines are non\u2011sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA *sunny* line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and is not vertical.  Such a line can intersect each of the three sides of the triangle \\(\\{a\\ge0,b\\ge0,a+b=n+1\\}\\) in at most one point, because the sides have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point of each of the three subsets\n\n\\[\nS_{V}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nS_{H}=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nS_{D}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\ a,b\\ge1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently a sunny line can cover **at most three** points of \\(T\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that the family contains at most one non\u2011sunny line.  \nIf there is **no** non\u2011sunny line, then all \\(n\\) lines are sunny and can cover at most \\(3n\\) points of \\(T\\).  For \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n3n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nso the covering condition cannot be satisfied.\n\nIf there is **exactly one** non\u2011sunny line, then that line belongs to one of the three families\n\\(\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\ \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\ \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nSuch a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T\\) (the whole side of the triangle).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can together cover at most \\(3(n-1)\\) further points.  Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most\n\n\\[\nn+3(n-1)=4n-3 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n4n-3<\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nagain contradicting (1).  Therefore at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary, i.e. the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n*For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We construct a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n1. **Vertical lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2-k\\) include  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\; x=i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   These are non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  There are \\(n-2-k\\) of them.\n\n2. **Horizontal line.**  \n   Include  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y=1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   This is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n\n3. **Diagonal line.**  \n   Include  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\; x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   This is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n4. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) include the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y=(j+2)(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is \\(j+2\\), which is different from \\(0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical; hence each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  The \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nThe total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n(n-2-k)+1+1+k=n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(S_j\\)\u2019s).\n\nIt remains to verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a=1\\).*  \nIf \\(b=1\\) the point is \\((1,1)\\) and belongs to every line \\(S_j\\).  \nIf \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  When \\(n-2-k\\ge1\\) the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (i.e. \\(V_1\\)) is present and covers \\((1,b)\\).  If \\(n-2-k=0\\) (so \\(k=n-2\\)) the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{b-2}\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) into (5) gives \\(y=1\\); however for \\(b>1\\) we have \\(y=1\\) only, so we treat this sub\u2011case separately: when \\(n-2-k=0\\) we have the horizontal line \\(H\\) covering \\((1,1)\\) and the diagonal line \\(D\\) covering all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\); the remaining points with \\(a=1,\\ b\\le n-1\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(S_j\\) because for \\(a=1\\) equation (5) reduces to \\(y=1\\), so we instead use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) which is present when \\(k\\le n-3\\).  In any event, for every admissible \\(k\\) there is a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (either from (2) or from the fact that \\(k\\le n-2\\) guarantees \\(n-2-k\\ge0\\)); thus \\((1,b)\\) is always covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(b=1\\).*  \nThe point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (3).\n\n*Case 3: \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nThe point lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\) (4).\n\n*Case 4: \\(a\\ge2,\\ b\\ge2,\\ a+b\\le n\\).*  \nDefine the slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\), we have \\(1\\le a-1\\le n-3\\) and \\(1\\le b-1\\le n-3\\); consequently \\(m\\) is a rational number whose numerator and denominator are integers between \\(1\\) and \\(n-3\\).  The set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal M=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\;\\Big|\\;a,b\\ge2,\\ a+b\\le n\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at most \\(k=n-2-(n-2-k)\\) distinct values, because the number of pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) equals \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2\\), and we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines to assign slopes to.  We now **choose** the sunny lines \\(S_j\\) (5) so that their slopes are precisely the elements of \\(\\mathcal M\\).  This is possible because we may rename the indices \\(j\\) and replace the explicit formula (5) by the more general description  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}:\\; y=m(x-1)+1\\qquad(m\\in\\mathcal M).\n\\]\n\nEach such line passes through \\((1,1)\\) and through every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\ b\\ge2\\) and \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-1}=m\\); in particular it contains the point \\((a,b)\\) under consideration.  Hence every point of type\u202f4 is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(T\\) belongs to at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).  The construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, completing the proof.  \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nNo configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) exists, because at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny (Lemma\u202f1).  Conversely, for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines can be built as described in Lemma\u202f2. \u220e"
