rom step\u202f2 we obtain the following upper bound\n   for the total number of points that can be covered:\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{t\\cdot n}_{\\text{points on non\u2011sunny lines}}\n   \\;+\\;\n   \\underbrace{k\\cdot 2}_{\\text{points on sunny lines}}\n   \\;\\ge\\;|S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-k\\) and simplifying (3) yields\n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)n+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   2k(n-2)\\le n(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n\\ge 3\\) we may divide by the positive integer \\(2(n-2)\\) and obtain\n   the necessary condition (1).\n\n4. **What remains.**  \n   Inequality (1) is the only restriction that I have been able to prove.\n   Whether every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can actually be realised by a\n   suitable configuration of \\(n\\) lines is still open.  The construction\n   problem reduces to arranging the non\u2011sunny lines so that they cover as many\n   points of \\(S_n\\) as possible (up to the bound \\(n\\) per line) and then pairing\n   the remaining uncovered points into disjoint sunny lines.  No complete\n   construction has been found for the whole range of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Number of required points)\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence there are \\(n+1-a\\) points with first coordinate\n\\(a\\).  Summing over \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points on a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), then the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); the number of admissible \\(a\\) is\n\\(n+1-b\\le n\\).  The vertical case is symmetric.  If \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal,\n\\(\\ell:\\;x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), then the integer solutions of\n\\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are\n\\(c-1\\le n\\) of them.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\;(i=1,2,3)\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\).  The three points are collinear,\nso the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals the slope of the segment joining any two of them.\nIf the slope were \\(0\\) the points would have the same \\(b\\)-coordinate, i.e.\nthe line would be horizontal; if the slope were \\(\\infty\\) they would have the\nsame \\(a\\)-coordinate, i.e. the line would be vertical; if the slope were \\(-1\\)\nwe would have \\(a_i+b_i\\) constant for all three points, i.e. the line would be\nparallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  All three possibilities contradict the hypothesis that\n\\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Hence at most two points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a sunny line.\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Necessary inequality for \\(k\\))\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\), and let\n\\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  Then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(t=n-k\\)