"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to obtain a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the **necessary inequality**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhich any feasible configuration must satisfy.  \nNo matching construction (showing that every integer \\(k\\) obeying (1) can actually be realised) is known at present; therefore the answer remains partial.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Counting the required points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(\\displaystyle |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points (Lemma\u202f1).\n\n2. **How many points can a single line contain?**  \n   *Non\u2011sunny lines* (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  \n   *Sunny lines* (any other slope) can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma\u202f3), because three collinear points would force the line to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions.\n\n3. **Deriving a global bound.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Using the point\u2011capacity estimates from step\u202f2 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\cdot n + k\\cdot 2 \\;\\ge\\; |S_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-k\\) and simplifying yields exactly inequality (1) (Lemma\u202f4).\n\n4. **What remains.**  \n   Inequality (1) is the only restriction proved.  Whether every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) can be achieved by an explicit arrangement of \\(n\\) distinct lines is still open.  The construction problem reduces to arranging the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines so that they cover as many points of \\(S_n\\) as possible (up to the bound \\(n\\) per line) and then pairing the uncovered points into disjoint sunny lines.  No complete construction covering the whole range of admissible \\(k\\) has been found.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Number of required points)\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); hence there are \\(n+1-a\\) points with first coordinate\n\\(a\\).  Summing over \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points on a non\u2011sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal case.*  Write \\(\\ell:\\;y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); the number of admissible\n\\(a\\) is \\(n+1-b\\le n\\).\n\n*Vertical case.*  Symmetric: \\(\\ell:\\;x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) yields at most\n\\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal case.*  Write \\(\\ell:\\;x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nInteger solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) are\n\\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Points on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap S_n\\,|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that three distinct points\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\;(i=1,2,3)\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\).  The three points are collinear,\nso the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals the slope of the segment joining any two of them.\nIf the slope were \\(0\\) the points would share the same \\(b\\)-coordinate,\nhence \\(\\ell\\) would be horizontal; if the slope were \\(\\infty\\) they would\nshare the same \\(a\\)-coordinate, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) would be vertical; if the slope\nwere \\(-1\\) we would have \\(a_i+b_i\\) constant for all three points, i.e. \\(\\ell\\)\nwould be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  All three possibilities contradict the\ndefinition of a sunny line.  Hence at most two points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a\nsunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\), and let\n\\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  Then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(t=n-k\\) for the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny\nline contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); by Lemma\u202f3 each sunny line\ncontains at most \\(2\\) points.  Consequently the total number of points that can\nbe covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\cdot n + k\\cdot 2 \\;\\ge\\; |S_n|\n   = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{(Lemma\u202f1)} .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)n + 2k \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n2k(n-2) \\le n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the factor \\(2(n-2)\\) is positive, and division yields  \n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the claimed bound (5). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence\n\nAny admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two\nrequirements of the problem must have a number \\(k\\) of sunny lines that\nfulfils inequality (5).  In other words,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on completeness\n\nThe inequality (5) is **necessary**; it follows solely from elementary\ncounting arguments and the geometric observation that a sunny line can meet\nthe prescribed triangular lattice in at most two points.  Whether the bound is\nalso **sufficient**\u2014i.e. whether for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) one can\nexhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny among them covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\)\u2014remains open.  Constructing such configurations would require\na delicate arrangement of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines so that they each attain\nthe maximal possible coverage (up to \\(n\\) points) and the remaining uncovered\npoints can be paired off and placed on sunny lines.  No general construction\ncovering the whole range of admissible \\(k\\) has been found.\n\nThus the problem is reduced to the following two tasks:\n\n1. **Tighten the necessary bound** (if possible) by a more refined analysis of\n   how the non\u2011sunny lines can intersect the set \\(S_n\\);\n2. **Provide explicit constructions** for each integer \\(k\\) that meets the\n   bound, thereby proving its sufficiency.\n\nUntil both tasks are accomplished, the answer to the original question remains\npartial. \u220e"
