s not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\). Such a line can intersect the triangular lattice \\(S_n\\) in three or more points.  \n  Example: Take \\(n=7\\). The line \\(\\ell: y = x + 1\\) (slope \\(1\\), thus sunny) contains the points  \n  \\[\n  (1,2),\\;(2,3),\\;(3,4)\n  \\]\n  all of which satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le8\\). Hence \\(|\u2113\u2229S_n|\\ge3\\).  \n  Therefore the bound \u201cat most\u202f2\u201d is incorrect, and the proof given (by contradiction using slopes) does not rule out slopes other than \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). The argument mistakenly assumes that any three collinear points from \\(S_n\\) would force the line to have one of the forbidden slopes, which is not true.\n\n- **Consequence:** Since Lemma\u202f3 is false, any later result that relies on it is unsound.\n\n### Lemma\u202f4 (Necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines)\n- **Step (quoted):** Uses Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 to obtain  \n  \\[\n  t\\cdot n + k\\cdot 2 \\ge |S_n|\n  \\]\n  where \\(t=n-k\\) is the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n- **Assessment:** **Critical Error.**  \n  The inequality assumes each sunny line contributes at most 2 points, which is false per Lemma\u202f3. Consequently the derived inequality  \n  \\[\n  k \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)}\n  \\]\n  is not a valid necessary condition. The algebraic manipulation after the (incorrect) inequality is correct, but the premise is invalid.\n\n### \u201cConsequence\u201d and \u201cRemarks on completeness\u201d\n- **Step:** States that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are those satisfying the bound (5) and admits that sufficiency is not proved.\n- **Assessment:** **Justification Gap.**  \n  Even if Lemma\u202f3 were correct, the solution would still be incomplete because the problem asks for *all* non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists. Providing only a necessary condition without demonstrating that every integer satisfying it is attainable leaves the answer unfinished.\n\n