eed to cover the remaining \\(n-1\\ge 2\\) points of \\(R\\).  \n\n   Therefore **no** sunny line can appear in a feasible configuration; i.e. \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_c\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non\u2011sunny.  \n   The line \\(\\ell_c\\) contains precisely the lattice points with \\(a+b=c\\).  \n   Since every point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has a sum \\(a+b\\) belonging to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), it lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \n   Thus the family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) covers the whole required set and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility argument shows that any admissible family must have \\(k=0\\); the explicit construction above shows that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(P\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nthe \\(n\\) points on the bottom side of this triangle.\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in P\\;\\exists\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\text{