al or of slope \\(-1\\). In either case they would be parallel, contradicting the requirement that all lines be distinct.  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot be present, and we must have  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is non\u2011sunny.  \nThe line \\(\\ell_c\\) contains exactly the lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b=c\\).  \n\nSince every point \\((a,b)\\in P\\) satisfies \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\), it lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{\\ell_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and satisfies condition (1).  \nBecause all its members are non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\), in accordance with (2).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe impossibility argument shows that any admissible configuration must contain **no** sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  \nThe explicit construction above demonstrates that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}}.\n\\] \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
