,\\;x+y=n+1\\)).  \n   Since \\(|P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), inequality (1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no** configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(P\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Take the following \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_{1}: x=1,\\\\\n   &\\ell_{2}: y=1,\\\\\n   &\\ell_{3}: x+y=n+1,\\\\\n   &\\ell_{4}: x=2,\\;\n   \\ell_{5}: y=2,\\;\n   \\ell_{6}: x+y=n,\\\\\n   &\\dots\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   More precisely, for each integer \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) we include the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=t,\\qquad y=t,\\qquad x+y=n+2-t .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The collection of all lines appearing in (3) consists of exactly \\(3(n-2)\\) lines, but many of them coincide when \\(t\\) varies.  \n   Keeping only **one** line of each of the three families for each \\(t\\) we obtain precisely \\(n-2\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines; for instance we may keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n   These \\(n-2\\) vertical lines together with the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2\\) already give \\(n\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny, and they already cover every point of \\(P\\).  \n   Hence for \\(k=0\\) the construction is trivial.\n\n   *Adding sunny lines.*  \n   To obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k>0\\) sunny lines we replace \\(k\\) of the non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones while preserving the covering property.\n\n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x + 1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(s_i\\) has slope \\(i-1\\neq 0,-1\\) and therefore is sunny.  \n   It passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\), both belonging to \\(P\\).  \n   Consequently the removal of the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which originally covered all points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\)) does **not** destroy the covering of any point of \\(P\\): every point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) is still covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which we keep among the non\u2011sunny lines), while the point \\((i,1)\\) is now covered by the sunny line \\(s_i\\).  \n\n   After performing this replacement for all \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain a family consisting of  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\);  \n   * the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the vertical lines \\(x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) together with the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\)).  \n\n   All lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(P\\) is still covered:  \n\n   * points with \\(b=1\\) are covered either by a sunny line \\(s_i\\) (if \\(a\\le k\\)) or by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (if \\(a>k\\));  \n   * points with \\(b=2\\) are covered by the horizontal line \\(y=2\\);  \n   * points with \\(b\\ge 3\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is present because we removed only the vertical lines with \\(a\\le k\\)).  \n\n   Thus the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (2) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n   Hence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nP:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in P\\).  \nIf two of the three points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the line would be vertical; if two have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, the line would be horizontal; if two have the same sum \\(a+b\\), the line would have slope \\(-1\\). In each case the line would be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. Hence no two of the three points can share an \\(x\\)-coordinate, a \\(y\\)-coordinate, or a sum, which is impossible for three points lying on a single straight line. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(P\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the largest possible number is obtained for \\(c=1\\) and equals \\(n\\). However the point \\((1,1)\\) also lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), so a vertical line can be assumed to be used together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); consequently we may count at most \\(n-1\\) *new* points contributed by a vertical line. The same reasoning applies to a horizontal line and to a line of slope \\(-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(P\\).  \nWrite \\(k:=|\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid\\ell\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\\) and \\(n-k\\) for the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points of \\(P\\); by Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n|P|\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n\\]\n\nA short algebraic manipulation gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus **no** covering family can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Existence of families with any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_{1}: x=1,\\\\\n&\\ell_{2}: x=2,\\\\\n&\\dots\\\\\n&\\ell_{n-2}: x=n-2,\\\\\n&\\ell_{n-1}: y=1,\\\\\n&\\ell_{n}: y=2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach point \\((a,b)\\in P\\) is covered:\n\n* if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\);\n* if \\(b=2\\) it lies on \\(\\ell_{n}\\);\n* if \\(b\\ge 3\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nAll lines are vertical or horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny, and we have \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStarting from the family described above, replace the vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=k\\) by the sunny lines\n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=(i-1)x+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(s_i\\) has slope \\(i-1\\neq 0,-1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nIt passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\), both belonging to \\(P\\).\n\nAfter the replacement we have the following collection of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\);\n* the remaining vertical lines \\(x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) (there are \\(n-2-k\\) of them);\n* the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and we verify that every point of \\(P\\) is still covered:\n\n* Points with \\(b=1\\):  \n  \u2013 if \\(a\\le k\\) they lie on the sunny line \\(s_a\\);  \n  \u2013 if \\(a>k\\) they lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n* Points with \\(b=2\\): they lie on the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).\n\n* Points with \\(b\\ge 3\\): then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a\\le k\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) **which has been removed**, but it is also covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (note that \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\), so the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is among the lines \\(y=1\\) or \\(y=2\\) when \\(b=1,2\\); for \\(b\\ge 3\\) we use the fact that the vertical line \\(x=a\\) was *not* removed when \\(a>k\\)).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a>k\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\nThus every point of \\(P\\) belongs to at least one line of the family. The construction works for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the upper bound (7) with the explicit constructions of Section\u202f3, we obtain that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

