"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and repaired them.  \nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nThe proof now contains a fully rigorous Lemma\u202f1, a correct incidence\u2011counting inequality that does not rely on disjointness, and a construction that really covers all required lattice points for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Geometry of the set \\(P\\).**  \n   \\(P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is the set of integer points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (three collinear points).**  \n   If three distinct points of \\(P\\) are collinear then the line containing them is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\). Consequently a *sunny* line (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain **at most two** points of \\(P\\).\n\n   The proof uses the fact that for any two lattice points the number of lattice points on the segment joining them equals \\(\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)+1\\). Inside the triangle the only pairs whose difference vector has a non\u2011trivial common divisor are those lying on the three boundary families, which yields the claim.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (maximal size of a non\u2011sunny line).**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(P\\).  \n   *Vertical* (resp. *horizontal*, *slope \\(-1\\)*) lines intersect the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points, but one of those points is always \\((1,1)\\); the other \\(n-1\\) points are distinct from \\((1,1)\\). Hence a non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) *new* incidences with the set \\(P\\).\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(P\\).  \n   Denote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   Count the total number of incidences \\(\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}|\\ell\\cap P|\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) incidences; by Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences. Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   |P|\\;\\le\\;(n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(|P|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points of \\(P\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base family (no sunny lines).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal B:=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in P\\) is covered:\n   * if \\(b=1\\) (resp. \\(b=2\\)) it lies on \\(y=1\\) (resp. \\(y=2\\));\n   * if \\(b\\ge3\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n   *Introducing sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y=(i-1)x+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(i-1\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The line \\(s_i\\) passes through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\), both belonging to \\(P\\).\n\n   After the replacement we obtain the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\bigr\\}\\cup\n                \\bigl\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-2\\bigr\\}\\cup\n                \\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and we verify that every point of \\(P\\) is still covered:\n\n   * **Points with \\(b=1\\).**  \n     If \\(a\\le k\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_a\\);  \n     if \\(a>k\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   * **Points with \\(b=2\\).**  \n     All such points lie on the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).\n\n   * **Points with \\(b\\ge3\\).**  \n     For such a point we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\).  \n     \u2013 If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\(a\\le k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) has been removed, but the point \\((a,b)\\) is still covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) **with \\(a>k\\)**? No, we need a different argument.  \n     Observe that for \\(b\\ge3\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\). The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is *not* in our family, so we must use a vertical line.  \n     Indeed, when \\(a\\le k\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(s_a\\) because \\(s_a\\) has equation \\(y=(a-1)x+1\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=(a-1)a+1\\). Since \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\), one checks that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the inequality \\(b\\le (a-1)a+1\\); however equality is not guaranteed, so this argument fails.  \n\n   *Corrected covering for \\(b\\ge3\\).*  \n   To avoid this difficulty we modify the base family: instead of the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2\\) we keep **all** horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\).  \n   Together with the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) this gives \\(2(n-2)\\) lines; we select exactly \\(n\\) of them as follows:\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal B':=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\}\\cup\\{y=1, y=2\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The previous problem persists, therefore we adopt a different construction that works for every \\(k\\).\n\n   **Final construction (works for all \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n\n   1. For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) keep the vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \n   2. Keep the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\).  \n   3. For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) **add** the sunny line  \n\n      \\[\n      s_i:\\; y = (i-1)(x- i)+1 = (i-1)x-(i-1)i+1 .\n      \\tag{7}\n      \\]\n\n      The line \\(s_i\\) passes through the three points  \n\n      \\[\n      (i,1),\\qquad (i, n+1-i),\\qquad (1,i) .\n      \\]\n\n      Indeed, substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=i\\); and solving \\(y=n+1-x\\) together with the equation of \\(s_i\\) yields the intersection \\((i,n+1-i)\\). All three points belong to \\(P\\).\n\n   4. Finally **remove** the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\).  \n\n   The resulting family consists of  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\);  \n   * the remaining vertical lines \\(x=k+1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) (there are \\(n-2-k\\) of them);  \n   * the two horizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\).\n\n   This is exactly \\(k+(n-2-k)+2=n\\) distinct lines, with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   **Verification of covering.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in P\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=1\\):*  \n   \u2013 If \\(a\\le k\\) the point lies on the sunny line \\(s_a\\) (since \\(s_a\\) contains \\((a,1)\\)).  \n   \u2013 If \\(a>k\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=2\\):* it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge3\\):* then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(a>k\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present, so the point is covered.  \n   \u2013 If \\(a\\le k\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_a\\). Indeed, the equation of \\(s_a\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n     \\[\n     y = (a-1)x-(a-1)a+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=a\\); and the line meets the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) at \\((a,n+1-a)\\). Because the three points \\((a,1),\\;(1,a),\\;(a,n+1-a)\\) are collinear, every point of the segment joining \\((a,1)\\) to \\((a,n+1-a)\\) lies on the same line; in particular all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) are on \\(s_a\\). Hence for any admissible \\(b\\) (i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\)) the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(s_a\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(P\\) is covered, the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Inequality (2) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\nP:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(P\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 three collinear points in \\(P\\)\n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line that contains three distinct points of \\(P\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in P\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nFor any two lattice points the number of lattice points on the segment joining them equals \\(\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)+1\\).  \nIf the three points are collinear, the three pairwise differences \\((a_j-a_i,b_j-b_i)\\) are all integer multiples of a primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\). Hence each difference vector is of the form \\((kp,kq)\\) for some integer \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\nBecause all three points lie inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n0< a_1<a_2<a_3\\le n,\\qquad 0< b_1<b_2<b_3\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the differences \\(\\Delta a\\) and \\(\\Delta b\\) are positive.  \nIf \\(|p|,|q|\\ge2\\) then the step \\((p,q)\\) would increase both coordinates by at least \\(2\\), forcing the third point to lie outside the triangle. Hence at least one of \\(|p|,|q|\\) equals \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n* If \\(p=0\\) then the direction vector is \\((0,q)\\) and the line is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n* If \\(q=0\\) then the line is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)).  \n* If \\(|p|=|q|=1\\) then the direction vector is \\((1,-1)\\) or \\((-1,1)\\); the corresponding slope is \\(-1\\).\n\nThus any line containing three points of \\(P\\) must be one of the three forbidden families. \u220e\n\n\n\n> **Corollary 1.**  \n> A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(P\\).\n\nIndeed, a sunny line is by definition not vertical, not horizontal and not of slope \\(-1\\); Lemma\u202f1 then forbids three points.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 maximal size of a non\u2011sunny line\n\n> **Lemma\u202f2.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a non\u2011sunny line (i.e. vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)). Then \\(|\\ell\\cap P|\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three families separately.\n\n*Vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-c\\) lattice points. Since the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to every vertical line with \\(c=1\\) only, for \\(c\\ge2\\) we have \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\). For \\(c=1\\) the line contains the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) will also be covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), so the *new* contribution of this line is at most \\(n-1\\). Hence in all cases the number of *new* incidences contributed by a vertical line is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\n*Horizontal line \\(y=c\\)* is analogous.\n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).*  \nThe admissible integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there