n* If \\(b\\ge3\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\); consequently the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nThus the case \\(k=0\\) is realized.\n\n#### 5.2  Sunny lines that replace some verticals  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=(i-1)x-(i-1)i+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(i-1\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\(s_i\\) passes through the three points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\qquad (1,i),\\qquad (i,n+1-i),\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(P\\). Consequently **every** lattice point of the vertical segment\n\\(\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\) lies on \\(s_i\\); in particular all points of the column \\(x=i\\) that belong to \\(P\\) are covered by \\(s_i\\).\n\nNow form the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\bigr\\}\n            \\cup\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-2\\bigr\\}\n            \\cup\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct, there are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;+\\;2\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\nof them, and precisely the first \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 5.3  Verification of the covering property  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in P\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\).*  \nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_a\\) (by (13)).  \nIf \\(a>k\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n*If \\(b=2\\).*  \nThe point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge3\\).*  \nThen \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\).  \n\n\u2013 If \\(a\\le k\\) the whole column \\(\\{(a,b')\\mid 1\\le b'\\le n+1-a\\}\\) is contained in the sunny line \\(s_a\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.  \n\n\u2013 If \\(a>k\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), so \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(P\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nInequality (10) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed in Section\u202f5 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed realizable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"

