            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points to be covered  \n\n\\[\nP:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(P\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 three collinear points in \\(P\\)\n\n> **Lemma\u202f1.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line that contains three distinct points of \\(P\\). Then \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in P\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).  \nFor any two lattice points the number of lattice points on the segment joining them equals \\(\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)+1\\).  \nIf the three points are collinear, the three pairwise differences \\((a_j-a_i,b_j-b_i)\\) are all integer multiples of a primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\). Hence each difference vector is of the form \\((kp,kq)\\) for some integer \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\nBecause all three points lie inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n0< a_1<a_2<a_3\\le n,\\qquad 0< b_1<b_2<b_3\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the differences \\(\\Delta a\\) and \\(\\Delta b\\) are positive.  \nIf \\(|p|,|q|\\ge2\\) then the step \\((p,q)\\) would increase both coordinates by at least \\(2\\), forcing the third point to lie outside the triangle. Hence at least one of \\(|p|,|q|\\) equals \\(0\\) or \\(1\\).\n\n* If \\(p=0\\) then the direction vector is \\((0,q)\\) and the line is vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n* If \\(q=0\\) then the line is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)).  \n* If \\(|p|=|q|=1\\) then the direction vector is \\((1,-1)\\) or \\((-1,1)\\); the corresponding slope is \\(-1\\).\n\nThus any line containing three points of \\(P\\) must be one of the three forbidden families. \u220e\n\n\n\n> **Corollary 1.**  \n> A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(P\\).\n\nIndeed, a sunny line is by definition not vertical, not horizontal and not of slope \\(-1\\); Lemma\u202f1 then forbids three points.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 maximal size of a non\u2011sunny line\n\n> **Lemma\u202f2.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a non\u2011sunny line (i.e. vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)). Then \\(|\\ell\\cap P|\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three families separately.\n\n*Vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-c\\) lattice points. Since the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to every vertical line with \\(c=1\\) only, for \\(c\\ge2\\) we have \\(n+1-c\\le n-1\\). For \\(c=1\\) the line contains the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) will also be covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), so the *new* contribution of this line is at most \\(n-1\\). Hence in all cases the number of *new* incidences contributed by a vertical line is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\n*Horizontal line \\(y=c\\)* is analogous.\n\n*Line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).*  \nThe admissible integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\) points. Since \\(c\\le n+1\\), we have \\(c-1\\le n\\). The point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to the line \\(x+y=2\\); again that point is already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), so the *new* contribution is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line can add at most \\(n-1\\) new incidences with the set \\(P\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(P\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid\\ell\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nn-k\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nCount the total number of incidences  \n\n\\[\nI:=\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}|\\ell\\cap P|.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(P\\) is counted at least once, so \\(I\\ge|P|\\).  \nBy Corollary\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) incidences; by Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) incidences. Hence  \n\n\\[\nI\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n\\]\n\nCombining the two inequalities we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|P|\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|P|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (8) and simplifying (9) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)(n-1)+2k\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore **no** configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 5.1  The basic non\u2011sunny family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2,\\;y=1,\\;y=2\\,\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal B\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in P\\) is covered:\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) (resp. \\(b=2\\)) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (resp. \\(y=2\\)).\n* If \\(b\\ge3\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\); consequently the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nThus the case \\(k=0\\) is realized.\n\n#### 5.2  Sunny lines that replace some verticals  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=(i-1)x-(i-1)i+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(i-1\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\(s_i\\) passes through the three points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\qquad (1,i),\\qquad (i,n+1-i),\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(P\\). Consequently **every** lattice point of the vertical segment\n\\(\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\) lies on \\(s_i\\); in particular all points of the column \\(x=i\\) that belong to \\(P\\) are covered by \\(s_i\\).\n\nNow form the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\bigr\\}\n            \\cup\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-2\\bigr\\}\n            \\cup\\{y=1,\\;y=2\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe lines are distinct, there are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;(n-2-k)\\;+\\;2\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\nof them, and precisely the first \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 5.3  Verification of the covering property  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in P\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\).*  \nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(s_a\\) (by (13)).  \nIf \\(a>k\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n*If \\(b=2\\).*  \nThe point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge3\\).*  \nThen \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-2\\).  \n\n\u2013 If \\(a\\le k\\) the whole column \\(\\{(a,b')\\mid 1\\le b'\\le n+1-a\\}\\) is contained in the sunny line \\(s_a\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) is covered.  \n\n\u2013 If \\(a>k\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), so \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nThus every point of \\(P\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nInequality (10) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed in Section\u202f5 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed realizable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
