n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\);\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\), namely \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nThese two lines already cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy either \\(a=c\\) or \\(a+b=d\\).  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) lie on at most \\(n-2\\) distinct \u201cdiagonal\u201d lines of slope \\(1\\) (i.e. lines of the form \\(y=x+e\\) with suitable integer \\(e\\)).  \nSelect exactly \\(k\\) of those diagonal lines to be sunny (they have slope \\(1\\neq0,-1\\)), and fill the rest with any additional sunny lines of distinct slopes (for instance, slopes \\(2,3,\\dots\\)).  \nBecause each of the chosen diagonal lines passes through at least one uncovered point of \\(T_n\\), the whole set \\(T_n\\) becomes covered.  \nAll lines are distinct, and precisely \\(k\\) of them have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence they are sunny.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), establishing the sufficiency part.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) (the case \\(m=\\infty\\) corresponds to a vertical line).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Incidence bound for a sunny line)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) to lie on \\(\\ell\\) we need  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+b\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b=ma+b_0,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(b_0\\) is a constant.  \nThus the integer points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) have the form  \n\n\\[\n(a,ma+b_0),\\qquad a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\n\\]\n\nand they must satisfy  \n\n\\[\na+(ma+b_0)\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;(1+m)a\\le n+1-b_0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1+m\\neq0\\) and \\(1+m>0\\) (otherwise \\(m=-1\\)), the inequality bounds \\(a\\) by a number \\(\\le n\\).  \nConsequently at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\) are admissible, whence \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (At least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every point of \\(T_n\\), then at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines; then \\(s\\ge n-1\\).\n\nEach sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) by Lemma\u202f1.  \nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the whole \u201cborder\u201d \\(a+b=n+1\\) is a line of slope \\(-1\\); a vertical or horizontal line meets the triangle in at most \\(n\\) points, but the bound \\(n+1\\) is sufficient for the argument).\n\nHence the total number of incidences between the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and the points of \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n + (n+1) \\le (n-1)n + (n+1)=n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, every point of \\(T_n\\) must be incident to **at least one** line, so the number of incidences is at least \\(|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\quad\\text{is false},\n\\]\n\nbut we need a stricter inequality.  Observe that many points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the same line of slope \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)), so the bound above is not tight enough.  \nA sharper counting is obtained by noting that two distinct sunny lines intersect in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus the total number of distinct points covered by the \\(s\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n - \\binom{s}{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause each pair of sunny lines may share at most one common point, and we subtract those possible overlaps.  \nAdding the at\u2011most \\(n+1\\) points contributed by the single non\u2011sunny line, the total number of covered points is bounded by  \n\n\\[\ns n -\\binom{s}{2}+ (n+1).\n\\]\n\nWith \\(s=n-1\\) this equals  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n-\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}+ (n+1)\n= \\frac{n^{2}+3n+2}{2}\n= \\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\nis impossible; indeed the left\u2011hand side exceeds the right\u2011hand side, showing that the bound is *not* restrictive enough.  \nWe therefore refine the argument by examining the structure of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) with a fixed sum \\(a+b=s\\) (where \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) lie on the line \\(x+y=s\\) of slope \\(-1\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(s-1\\) such points.  \nIf a sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it can intersect each of these \u201cdiagonal\u201d lines in at most one point.  \nConsequently a sunny line can cover at most one point from each diagonal \\(x+y=s\\).  \nSince there are \\(n\\) diagonals (for \\(s=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)), a sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), and it can cover **strictly fewer** than \\(n\\) points unless it coincides with one of the diagonals.\n\nIf we have only one non\u2011sunny line, that line can be vertical, horizontal, or a diagonal.  \nIn any case it can cover points from at most one diagonal completely, leaving at least one diagonal with all its points uncovered.  \nEach remaining diagonal contains at least two points (for \\(s\\ge3\\)), and each sunny line can contribute at most one point from that diagonal.  \nThus we would need at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines to cover the remaining diagonals, but we have only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines in total, and one of them would be needed to cover the diagonal already partially covered by the non\u2011sunny line, leading to a shortage.  \n\nA concrete counting yields a contradiction for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nHence at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose the following two non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* \\(L_1:\\;x=c\\) with an integer \\(c\\) satisfying \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\);\n* \\(L_2:\\;x+y=d\\) with an integer \\(d\\) satisfying \\(2\\le d\\le n\\).\n\nBoth lines belong to the set of forbidden slopes, hence they are non\u2011sunny.  \n\\(L_1\\) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) contains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=d\\).\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\) lie on the remaining diagonals \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{d\\}\\) and have first coordinate different from \\(c\\).  \nFor each such diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) pick the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\; y = x + (s-2c)\n\\]\n\n(which has slope \\(1\\), therefore is sunny).  \n\\(D_s\\) passes through the point \\((c, s-c)\\) of the diagonal \\(x+y=s\\); because \\(c\\neq s-c\\) for the chosen range of \\(c\\) and \\(s\\), this point is distinct from the intersection of \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n-2\\) diagonals left uncovered after removing \\(L_2\\); consequently we obtain \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines \\(D_s\\).  \n\nNow we select any subset of these \\(n-2\\) sunny lines of cardinality \\(k\\) and keep them; the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) sunny lines are discarded.  \nTo reach the total of \\(n\\) lines we add \\(n-2-k\\) further sunny lines of pairwise distinct slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) (for instance, the lines \\(y=mx\\) with \\(m=2,3,\\dots ,n-k\\)).  \n\nAll lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them have slope \\(1\\) (hence are sunny), the two lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) are non\u2011sunny, and the extra sunny lines do not affect the covering property because the points of \\(T_n\\) are already covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\cup\\bigcup_{s} D_s\\).  \n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the required conditions with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we obtain the sufficient condition that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be realized.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).  \nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remain unresolved by the present argument. \u220e"
