inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sufficiency (realising every \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n   *Construction.*  \n   Choose two non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n   * a vertical line \\(L_1:\\;x=c\\) with an integer \\(c\\) satisfying \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\);\n   * a diagonal line \\(L_2:\\;x+y=d\\) with an integer \\(d\\) satisfying \\(2\\le d\\le n\\).\n\n   \\(L_1\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\);  \n   \\(L_2\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the diagonal \\(\\Delta_d\\).\n\n   After removing the points already covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\), the only uncovered points are those that lie on the remaining diagonals \\(\\Delta_s\\) (\\(s\\neq d\\)) **and** whose first coordinate is different from \\(c\\).  \n   For each such diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\; y = x + (s-2c) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D_s\\) passes through the lattice point \\((c,\\,s-c)\\) which belongs to \\(\\Delta_s\\) and is not on \\(L_1\\) (because its second coordinate is \\(s-c\\neq c\\)).  \n   Hence \\(D_s\\) covers at least one previously uncovered point of \\(\\Delta_s\\).  \n   The collection \\(\\{D_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{d\\}\\}\\) consists of exactly \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines (all have slope \\(1\\)).\n\n   To obtain a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we proceed as follows:\n\n   * keep any \\(k\\) of the lines \\(D_s\\);\n   * discard the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) of them;\n   * add \\(n-2-k\\) further sunny lines of pairwise distinct slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty,1\\) (for instance the lines \\(y=mx\\) with \\(m=2,3,\\dots ,n-k\\)).\n\n   The final set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L =\\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{\\text{the chosen }k\\text{ lines }D_s\\}\\cup\\{\\text{the extra }n-2-k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the uncovered points are taken care of by the retained \\(D_s\\) lines; the extra sunny lines are irrelevant for covering).  \n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\nCombining the two parts we obtain the full answer: the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and elementary facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) consists of the lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the **diagonal**\n\n\\[\n\\Delta_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\Delta_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThere are precisely \\(n\\) such diagonals.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\) (vertical).  \n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Lemma 1 \u2013 a sunny line meets each diagonal in at most one point  \n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, written as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nIntersecting \\(\\ell\\) with \\(\\Delta_s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx+(mx+b)=s\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)x = s-b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1+m\\neq0\\), the equation has at most one solution \\(x\\); consequently\n\\(\\ell\\cap\\Delta_s\\) contains at most one point. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lemma 2 \u2013 at least two lines must be non\u2011sunny  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that **at most one** line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nLet the (possible) non\u2011sunny line be \\(\\ell_0\\); all other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(\\ell_0\\) is a diagonal \\(\\Delta_{s_0}\\).*  \nThen \\(\\ell_0\\) covers **all** points of the diagonal \\(\\Delta_{s_0}\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonals each contain at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (because for \\(s\\ge 3\\) we have \\(s-1\\ge2\\)).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contribute at most one point from each of those diagonals, so to cover the remaining points we would need at least \\(2(n-1)\\) sunny lines, contradicting the fact that only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are available.\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(\\ell_0\\) is vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)).*  \nSuch a line meets each diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) in exactly one point, so after using \\(\\ell_0\\) there remain \\(n\\) diagonals, each still containing at least one uncovered point (the point of the diagonal not lying on \\(\\ell_0\\)).  \nAgain Lemma\u202f1 shows that a sunny line can supply at most one point per diagonal, therefore we would need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines, but we have only \\(n-1\\).  \n\nBoth cases lead to a contradiction; hence the assumption is false.  \nConsequently **at least two** lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Lemma 3 \u2013 construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Two non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Choose integers \\(c,d\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le c\\le n-1,\\qquad 2\\le d\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=c\\qquad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad   \n   L_2:\\;x+y=d\\qquad(\\text{diagonal }-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines that finish the covering.**  \n   For every diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{d\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\; y = x + (s-2c) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D_s\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and passes through the lattice point\n   \\((c,\\,s-c)\\in\\Delta_s\\).  \n   Because \\(c\\neq s-c\\) for the chosen ranges, this point is not on \\(L_1\\); also \\(s\\neq d\\), so it is not on \\(L_2\\).  \n   Thus each \\(D_s\\) covers at least one point of \\(\\Delta_s\\) that is not already covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\).\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such diagonals, so we obtain \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines \\(D_s\\).\n\n3. **Selecting exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Keep any \\(k\\) of the lines \\(D_s\\); discard the other \\(n-2-k\\) of them.  \n   To reach a total of \\(n\\) lines we add \\(n-2-k\\) further sunny lines with pairwise distinct slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty,1\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = jx \\qquad (j=2,3,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   These extra lines do not affect the covering property because every point of \\(T_n\\) is already covered by \\(L_1\\cup L_2\\cup\\bigcup_{s} D_s\\).\n\n4. **Resulting family.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\n   \\{\\text{the chosen }k\\text{ lines }D_s\\}\\cup\n   \\{\\text{the }n-2-k\\text{ extra sunny lines }S_j\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the retained \\(D_s\\) lines, all of slope \\(1\\)).  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Final conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that this condition is also sufficient.  \n\nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) lines exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
