e of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202f3 (verification).**  \n     The union of the \\(m\\) vertical lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines covers **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n     The total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) we write \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb N\\) and require \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}= \\{y=0\\},\\qquad \n\\ell_{y}= \\{x=0\\},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}= \\{x+y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line may have any slope except \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessity\n\nIf a configuration of lines satisfies the statement of the problem, it uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nHence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nNo other restriction follows from the hypotheses.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sufficiency\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }0\\le m\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x=i .\n\\]\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\) are distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts.\n\nEvery point