#\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}= \\frac{(n+1)n}{2}.\n\\]\nThus the assumption leads to a contradiction, establishing that at least two sunny lines are necessary.\n\nThe detailed counting is carried out in the next section.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and suppose, for the sake of contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing at most one sunny line.  \nRecall that a line is *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_{x}=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf d_{y}=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf d_{d}= (1,1).\n\\]\n\nConsequently a *non\u2011sunny* line is parallel to exactly one of these three directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Structure of a configuration with \\(\\le 1\\) sunny line\n\nIf two distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) were parallel to the same forbidden direction, then both would be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the hypothesis that at most one line is sunny.  \nHence **at most one** line of each forbidden direction can belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain at most the following four lines:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(L_{x}: y=c_{x}\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf d_{x}\\));\n* a vertical line \\(L_{y}: x=c_{y}\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf d_{y}\\));\n* a diagonal line \\(L_{d}: x+y=c_{d}\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf d_{d}\\));\n* possibly one sunny line \\(L_{s}\\) (not parallel to any of the three directions).\n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\ge 3\\), the only admissible values of \\(n\\) under the present assumption are\n\n\\[\nn\\in\\{3,4\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for \\(n\\ge 5\\) we would need at least five distinct lines, but we have shown that at most four distinct lines can appear when the number of sunny lines is \\(\\le 1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Counting the lattice points that can be covered\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_{n}=|\\mathcal P_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) must lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nWe now bound the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that can lie on the four possible lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.1  Points on a horizontal line\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c_{x}\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) precisely for those integers \\(a\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-c_{x},\\qquad\\text{provided }1\\le c_{x}\\le n.\n\\]\n\nHence the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) on \\(L_{x}\\) is at most \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  Points on a vertical line\n\nAnalogously, a vertical line \\(x=c_{y}\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  Points on a diagonal line\n\nA line of the form \\(x+y=c_{d}\\) intersects \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) in the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c_{d},\\;1\\le a,b,\\;c_{d}\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus it contains exactly \\(c_{d}-1\\) points, which is at most \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.4  Points on a sunny line\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; consequently its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nSuch a line can intersect each of the three families of lines described above in at most one point, because two distinct lines of different slopes intersect in a unique point.\n\nTherefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that does not already belong to one of the three non\u2011sunny lines. (If a point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on the sunny line and also on, say, the horizontal line, it has already been counted in the horizontal line.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Upper bound for the total number of covered points\n\nSumming the contributions we obtain the following maximal possible cardinality of the union of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} (L\\cap\\mathcal P_{n})\\bigr|\n&\\le\n\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n+\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{vertical}}\n+\\underbrace{n}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n+\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{sunny (if present)}}\\\\[2mm]\n&=\n3n+1\\quad\\text{if a sunny line is present},\\\\\n&=3n\\quad\\text{if no sunny line is present}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Comparison with the required number \\(N_{n}\\)\n\nRecall that \\(N_{n}=n(n+1)/2\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n\n\\[\nN_{n}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} > 3n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - (3n+1)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-6n-2}{2}\n=\\frac{n^{2}-5n-2}{2}>0\n\\]\nbecause the quadratic \\(n^{2}-5n-2\\) has its positive root at\n\\(\\displaystyle n=\\frac{5+\\sqrt{33}}{2}\\approx 5.37\\), and the inequality is already true for \\(n=3,4,5\\); for larger \\(n\\) the left\u2011hand side grows quadratically while the right\u2011hand side grows only linearly.\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} (L\\cap\\mathcal P_{n})\\bigr|\n\\;<\\; N_{n},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Conclusion of the contradiction\n\nThe contradiction arose from the assumption that a configuration with at most one sunny line exists.  \nHence such a configuration cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore any admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) must contain **at least two sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Final statement\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) (the number of sunny lines) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe proof that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) is indeed realizable remains open; the argument above only establishes the necessary condition \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \u220e\n"
