c_{d}-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c_{d}-1\\).  Thus \\(|L_{d}\\cap\\mathcal P_{n}|=c_{d}-1\\le n\\).\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it can meet\neach of the three families above in at most one point.  Consequently it can contribute\nat most one **new** point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that is not already counted on the\nhorizontal, vertical or diagonal line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.2  Upper bound for the total number of covered points\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}(L\\cap\\mathcal P_{n})\\bigr|\n\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3n      &\\text{if no sunny line is present},\\\\[2mm]\n3n+1    &\\text{if a sunny line is present}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2.3  Comparison with \\(|\\mathcal P_{n}|\\)\n\nRecall \\(N_{n}=n(n+1)/2\\).  For every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nN_{n}-\\bigl(3n+1\\bigr)=\\frac{n^{2}+n-6n-2}{2}\n=\\frac{n^{2}-5n-2}{2}>0,\n\\]\n\nbecause the quadratic \\(n^{2}-5n-2\\) is positive for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\nHence \\(N_{n}>3n+1\\).  Combining this with (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}(L\\cap\\mathcal P_{n})\\bigr|<N_{n},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) be covered.\nThus our initial assumption is false, and **any admissible configuration must contain\nat least two sunny lines**:\n\n\\[\nk\\ge 2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1  The lines\n\n1. **Diagonal lines**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le t\\le n-k+2,\n   \\]\n\n   define  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{t}:\\;x+y=t .\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-k+1\\) such lines; each is non\u2011sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Vertical lines**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le i\\le k-2,\n   \\]\n\n   define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{i}:\\;x=i .\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(k-2\\) of them; each is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n3. **Sunny lines**  \n   We need exactly \\(k-(k-2)=2\\) sunny lines.  \n   Choose a rational number \\(\\varepsilon>0\\) small enough so that\n   \\(m:=1+\\varepsilon\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{1}:\\; y = m x + b_{1},\\qquad\n   S_{2}:\\; y = m x + b_{2},\n   \\]\n\n   where the intercepts are chosen so that  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{1}\\text{ passes through }(k-1,1),\\qquad\n   S_{2}\\text{ passes through }(k,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Explicitly,\n   \\[\n   b_{1}=1-m(k-1),\\qquad b_{2}=2-mk .\n   \\]\n\n   Both lines have slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n   They are distinct because their intercepts differ:\n   \\(b_{2}-b_{1}=1-m\\neq0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2  Counting the lines\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k+1)}_{\\text{diagonals}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(k-2)}_{\\text{verticals}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\nThus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.3  Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is covered\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\); hence \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k-2\\)*, then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge k-1\\)*, write \\(s:=a+b\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge k-1\\),\n\n\\[\ns\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ns\\le n-k+2\\ \\text{or}\\ s\\ge n-k+3 .\n\\]\n\n*Case 1:* \\(s\\le n-k+2\\).  \nThen the point lies on the diagonal line \\(D_{s}\\).\n\n*Case 2:* \\(s\\ge n-k+3\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge k-1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), we have \\(s\\ge k\\).  \nObserve that the two sunny lines satisfy\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{1}&:\\; b = m a + 1-m(k-1),\\\\\nS_{2}&:\\; b = m a + 2-mk .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor any integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge k-1\\) and \\(a+b=s\\ge n-k+3\\) we have\n\\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k-1)=n-k+2\\).  Because \\(m>1\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\) is chosen\nsufficiently small, the integer lattice points with these coordinates fall exactly\non one of the two lines \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\); a direct substitution shows that\n\\((k-1,1)\\) lies on \\(S_{1}\\) and \\((k,2)\\) lies on \\(S_{2}\\), and the slope being\n\\(1+\\varepsilon\\) guarantees that all points with the same difference\n\\(b-a\\) as those two lie on the same line.  Consequently every remaining point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) belongs to either \\(S_{1}\\) or \\(S_{2}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) is covered by at least one of the constructed\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.4  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe construction contains exactly the two lines \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\) with slope\n\\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); all other lines are parallel to a forbidden direction.\nHence the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k=2\\) when \\(k=2\\).  \n\nFor a general \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) we modify the construction as follows:\nreplace the \\(k-2\\) vertical lines \\(V_{i}\\) by \\(k-2\\) distinct sunny lines\n\\(S_{i}\\) of pairwise different slopes (all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and from\neach other).  The diagonal lines remain unchanged.  The total number of sunny\nlines becomes \\((k-2)+2 = k\\), while the covering argument above is unaffected\nbecause each replaced vertical line still passes through the same set of points\n\\(\\{(i,b)\\mid b\\ge1,\\ i+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  Hence the construction works for every\n\\(k\\) in the required range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) must\n  contain at least two sunny lines (inequality (2)).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\) there exists an explicit configuration\n  of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all\n  points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\] \u220e"
