exists an integer\n\\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that (3) holds, i.e.\n\\[\nk=n-s\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{(n-2s)(n+1)+s(s+1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Counting the points.**  \n   The set  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(T=n(n+1)/2\\) points.\n\n2. **Maximum coverage by non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three special directions.  \n   * A vertical line \\(x=c\\) (with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   * A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains the same number of points.  \n   * A diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) (with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)) contains \\(d-1\\) points.  \n\n   By taking the \\(s\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\) we obtain the\n   maximal possible number of covered points, namely (1). This yields the\n   expression (2) for the uncovered points.\n\n3. **Pigeonhole bound for sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three special directions.\n   In the triangular lattice \\(\\mathcal P\\) any three distinct points are never collinear\n   unless they lie on one of the three special directions. Hence a sunny line can\n   contain at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n   Therefore at least \\(\\lceil R(s)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines are necessary; this is the\n   pigeonhole inequality (3).\n\n4. **Sufficiency.**  \n   If (3) holds for some \\(s\\), we explicitly construct a configuration:\n   * Use the \\(s\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\) (they are non\u2011sunny and cover\n     exactly \\(C(s)\\) points).  \n   * The remaining \\(R(s)\\) points are paired arbitrarily; each pair determines a\n     unique line that is not parallel to any of the three special directions,\n     because the two points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates,\n     and distinct sums \\(x+y\\).  \n   * If \\(R(s)\\) is odd, one point is left alone; a line through that single point\n     (again not parallel to the special directions) is added.  \n   The total number of sunny lines used is exactly \\(\\lceil R(s)/2\\rceil\\), which\n   equals \\(k=n-s\\) by (3). All lines are distinct, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n   lies on at least one of them.\n\n5. **Characterisation of admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Inequality (4) is the final condition. Solving it for each \\(n\\) gives the\n   complete list of admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nT=|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Maximum number of points covered by non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\),\n\n* the vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\);  \n  hence it contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\);\n\n* the horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains the same number of points;\n\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) (\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)) contains \\(d-1\\) points.\n\nIf we are allowed to use \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the largest possible number of\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that can be covered is obtained by taking the \\(s\\) vertical\nlines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\).  Their total contribution is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(s)&=\\sum_{c=1}^{s}(n+1-c)\n      =s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNo other choice of \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover more points, because any\nhorizontal or diagonal line contains at most as many points as the\ncorresponding vertical line with the same index.\n\nConsequently the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that remain uncovered after\nusing \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(s)&=T-C(s)\\\\\n    &=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\Bigl(s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2\\Bigr)\\\\[2mm]\n    &=\\frac{(n-2s)(n+1)+s(s+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   A pigeonhole bound for sunny lines\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three special directions.\nIn the set \\(\\mathcal P\\) any three distinct points are never collinear unless\nthey lie on a line of one of those three directions.  Hence a sunny line can\ncontain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\nTherefore, if after the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines there remain \\(R(s)\\) uncovered\npoints, at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{R(s)}2\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines are necessary.  Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), we must\nhave\n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{R(s)}2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInequality (9) is the **necessary condition** for the existence of a required\nconfiguration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Sufficiency of the condition\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the inequality (9) holds.\nSet \\(k=n-s\\).\n\n1. **Construction of the non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,s).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non\u2011sunny, and by (6) they cover exactly \\(C(s)\\) points\n   of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n2. **Construction of the sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal Q\\) be the set of the remaining \\(R(s)\\) points.\n   Pair the points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) arbitrarily; if \\(R(s)\\) is odd, leave one\n   point unpaired.  \n   For each pair \\(\\{P,Q\\}\\subset\\mathcal Q\\) consider the line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\)\n   through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\).  Because \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) have different\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different sums\n   \\(x+y\\), the line \\(\\ell_{PQ}\\) is not parallel to any of the three special\n   directions; hence it is sunny.  \n   If a single point \\(P\\) is left over, draw any line through \\(P\\) that is\n   not parallel to the special directions (for instance, the line of slope\n   \\(2\\) through \\(P\\)).  This line is also sunny.\n\n   The number of sunny lines constructed is exactly\n   \\(\\lceil R(s)/2\\rceil\\), which equals \\(k\\) by (9).  All these lines are\n   distinct because each contains a different pair (or a different single\n   point).\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines\n   (if it belongs to the first \\(s\\) columns) or on one of the sunny lines\n   (by construction).  Hence the covering requirement is satisfied, and the\n   total number of lines is \\(s+k=n\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus, whenever (9) holds for some \\(s\\), a configuration meeting all the\nrequirements exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Final characterisation\n\nCombining the necessary part (9) with the construction above, we obtain the\ncomplete description:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge3\\).  A non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) is admissible (i.e. there exist\n> \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem with\n> exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines) **iff** there exists an integer\n> \\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that \\(k=n-s\\) and\n> \\[\n> n-s\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{(n-2s)(n+1)+s(s+1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n> \\tag{11}\n> \\]\n> Equivalently, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those for which\n> the inequality\n> \\[\n> k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{(n-2(n-k))(n+1)+(n-k)(n-k+1)}{4}\\Bigr\\rceil\n> \\]\n> holds.\n\nFor each concrete \\(n\\) the set of admissible \\(k\\) can be obtained by\nchecking the finitely many possibilities