"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI was unable to complete a fully rigorous classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nThe main obstacle is the claim that a sunny line can contain at most two points of the set  \n\\(\\displaystyle \\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \nThat claim is false (for example the sunny line \\(y=x\\) contains many points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) when \\(n\\) is large). Consequently the pigeon\u2011hole bound derived from it is not valid, and the resulting condition (4) does **not** characterize the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\nWhat I can still prove rigorously is a **necessary** inequality that any admissible pair \\((n,k)\\) must satisfy, obtained by a correct pigeon\u2011hole argument that uses the true maximal number of points a sunny line can cover. The inequality is weaker than the one claimed in the first attempt, and I have not been able to show that it is also sufficient.\n\n> **Lemma (necessary condition).**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \n> Denote by \\(s=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines. Then\n> \\[\n> k\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{T-C_{\\max }(s)}{M}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n> \\tag{\u2605}\n> \\]\n> where  \n> \\[\n> T=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\text{(total number of points of }\\mathcal P\\text{)},\n> \\]\n> \\[\n> C_{\\max }(s)=s\\,(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2\n> \\quad\\text{(the maximal number of points that can be covered by }s\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines)},\n>\\]\n> and \\(M\\) is the **largest possible number of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that can lie on a single sunny line**.  \n> The exact value of \\(M\\) depends on \\(n\\); a universal bound is \\(M\\le n\\).\n\nThe inequality (\u2605) follows from the pigeon\u2011hole principle: after the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines have covered at most \\(C_{\\max }(s)\\) points, at least \\(T-C_{\\max }(s)\\) points remain, and each sunny line can cover at most \\(M\\) of them.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Count the points.**  \n   \\(\\displaystyle \\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains  \n   \\[\n   T=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n   \\]\n   points.\n\n2. **Maximum coverage by non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n   \\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \n   By taking the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\) we obtain the maximal possible\n   coverage\n   \\[\n   C_{\\max }(s)=s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bounding the capacity of a sunny line.**  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   For any such slope the intersection of the line with the integer lattice inside the triangle can contain at most \\(M\\) points, where a simple geometric argument shows \\(M\\le n\\) (the line can intersect at most one point in each column, and there are at most \\(n\\) columns).  \n   This yields the pigeon\u2011hole inequality (\u2605).\n\n4. **Partial conclusion.**  \n   Any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (\u2605) for some integer \\(s\\in[0,n]\\).  \n   Determining the exact value of \\(M\\) (the maximal collinearity of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) on a sunny line) and proving that the bound is also sufficient remain open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nT=|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Points covered by non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) the admissible points are\n\\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence it contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\nThe same count holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).\nA diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) (\\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\)) contains \\(d-1\\) points.\n\nIf we are allowed to use \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the maximal number of points that can be covered is achieved by taking the \\(s\\) vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\). Their total contribution is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\max }(s)&=\\sum_{c=1}^{s}(n+1-c)\n            =s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNo other selection of \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover more points, because any\nhorizontal or diagonal line contains at most as many points as the vertical\nline with the same index.\n\nThus after the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines at most \\(C_{\\max }(s)\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered, and at least\n\n\\[\nR(s)=T-C_{\\max }(s)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints remain uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   How many points can a sunny line contain?\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(\\alpha\\) satisfies\n\\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\nConsider the projection of \\(\\mathcal P\\) onto the \\(x\\)-axis.\nIn each column \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) there are at most \\(n+1-c\\) points,\nand any two distinct points of the same column have different \\(y\\)-coordinates.\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, it meets each column in at most one point.\nHence a sunny line can contain at most one point from each column, giving the crude bound\n\n\\[\nM\\le n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA sharper bound can be obtained by also observing that \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel\nto the diagonal direction \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\); consequently it cannot contain\ntwo points whose sums \\(x+y\\) are equal. This restriction does not improve the\nasymptotic bound, and for the purpose of a necessary condition we retain the\nsimple estimate (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Pigeon\u2011hole inequality\n\nThe \\(R(s)\\) uncovered points must be covered by the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines.\nSince each sunny line can cover at most \\(M\\) points, the pigeon\u2011hole principle\nimplies\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{R(s)}{M}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (3) for \\(R(s)\\) and using the universal bound \\(M\\le n\\) yields the\nnecessary condition\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{T-C_{\\max }(s)}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n\\qquad\ns=n-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nExplicitly,\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\n\\frac{\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\n\\Bigl(s(n+1)-\\frac{s(s+1)}2\\Bigr)}{n}\n\\Bigr\\rceil\n=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\n\\frac{(n-2s)(n+1)+s(s+1)}{2n}\n\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nInequality (7) must hold for some integer \\(s\\in[0,n]\\); equivalently, any\nadmissible \\(k\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\n\\frac{(n-2(n-k))(n+1)+(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2n}\n\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Remarks on sufficiency\n\nThe bound (8) is **necessary**.  \nTo prove that it is also **sufficient** one would need to determine the exact\nmaximum \\(M\\) of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that can lie on a single sunny line,\nand then exhibit a construction that attains the bound for every admissible\npair \\((n,k)\\).  The simple construction used in the first attempt fails because\nsunny lines can contain many points (e.g. the line \\(y=x\\) contains\n\\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points), so the estimate \u201cat most two points per sunny\nline\u2019\u2019 is invalid.  Finding a tight value of \\(M\\) and a matching construction\nremains an open problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor a given \\(n\\ge3\\) any admissible number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy the\nnecessary inequality (8).  Determining whether this condition is also sufficient,\nand thus obtaining a complete classification of all possible \\(k\\), is left as\nfuture work. \u220e"
