ecisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are admissible, and no other value can occur because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(a\\le a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\), which is one of the \\(L_i\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor any integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x-(i-1)\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,1),(i+1,2),\\dots ,(n,n+1-i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWriting the equation as \\(y = x-(i-1)\\) gives \\(y = x-i+1\\).  \nIf \\((x,y)=(i+t,\\,t+1)\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\) then \\(y = (i+t)-(i-1)=t+1\\), so the point lies on \\(S_i\\).  \nConversely, any integer solution of \\(y = x-i+1\\) with \\(x\\ge i\\) can be written in that form.  \nThe condition \\(x\\le n\\) forces \\(t\\le n-i\\), i.e. \\(y\\le n+1-i\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nEach line \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(+1\\). Since \\(+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\). Hence it is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\]\n\nDefine the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines as follows:\n\n* for each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\) include the vertical line \\(L_i : x=i\\);\n* for each \\(i\\in\\{m+1,m+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) include the sunny line \\(S_i : y = x-(i-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification\n\n1. **Number of sunny lines.**  \n   By Lemma\u202f3 each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and there are exactly \\(k=n-m\\) of them.  \n   The vertical lines are not sunny. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n2. **Coverage of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   *Points in the first \\(m\\) columns.*  \n   If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le m\\), then \\(a\\) equals some \\(i\\le m\\); by Lemma\u202f1 the point lies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\in\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   *Points in the remaining columns.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f2 the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(S_a\\) (because \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) is exactly the condition appearing in Lemma\u202f2).  \n   Since \\(S_a\\in\\mathcal L_k\\), the point is covered.\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n3. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   The vertical lines have distinct abscissae, and the sunny lines have distinct intercepts \\(-(i-1)\\); consequently no two lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) coincide.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nBecause we have only \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\), and it cannot be negative. Therefore the only possible values of \\(k\\) are the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\). The construction above shows that each of these values is indeed attainable.