nt* sunny line. Choose any point \\(P\\) on \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) and any direction vector not parallel to \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\); the line through \\(P\\) with that direction is sunny; call it \\(S_2\\). Do the same for a point on \\(D_2^{(m)}\\) to obtain a sunny line \\(S_3\\). (If one of the diagonals is empty, the corresponding line is omitted.)\n\n   The number of sunny lines introduced is therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{1}_{\\text{cover }B_m}\n   \\;+\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\mathbf{1}_{D_1^{(m)}\\neq\\varnothing}}_{\\text{if }m\\ge 1}\n   \\;+\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\mathbf{1}_{D_2^{(m)}\\neq\\varnothing}}_{\\text{if }m\\ge 2}\n   \\;=\\;k .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, a direct count shows that the three contributions sum to \\(k=n-m\\). (When \\(m=n\\) we have \\(k=0\\) and no sunny line is added; when \\(m=0\\) we have \\(k=n\\) and the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines obtained by applying Lemma\u202f2 repeatedly to the successive \u201cborder\u201d layers of the triangle.)\n\n4. **Verification of the count.**  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{m}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}} = n .\n   \\]\n\n   All lines are distinct by construction (the sunny lines are chosen with directions different from the vertical ones, and the three possible sunny lines are taken with distinct direction vectors).  \n\n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the vertical lines, to \\(B_m\\), or to one of the two diagonals, hence is covered.\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, satisfying the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nConversely, any admissible configuration must contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines (there are only \\(n\\) lines in total) and at least \\(0\\) s