n   +\\underbrace{\\mathbf{1}_{D_2^{(m)}\\neq\\varnothing}}_{\\text{if }m\\ge2}=k,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. precisely the prescribed value \\(k=n-m\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(m\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) plus the \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(n\\).  \nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n\n*Let \\(m\\ge 1\\). The family of vertical lines*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_m:=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\}\n\\]\n\n*covers every point of*  \n\n\\[\nT_{2m-1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le 2m\\}\n\\]\n\n*except the points belonging to the two diagonals*  \n\n\\[\nD_1^{(m)}:=\\{(a,\\,2m-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m\\},\n\\qquad\nD_2^{(m)}:=\\{(a,\\,2m-1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{2m-1}\\).  \n\n*If \\(a\\le m\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V_m\\) contains the point.*  \n\n*If \\(a>m\\) then \\(b\\le 2m-a < m\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which is not used in the construction but exists) would contain the point. Since we only have the vertical lines, the point is uncovered only when both \\(a\\le m\\) and \\(b\\le m\\) hold simultaneously. Under the additional condition \\(a+b\\le 2m\\) this forces \\(a+b\\) to be either \\(2m\\) or \\(2m-1\\), giving precisely the two sets \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) and \\(D_2^{(m)}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f2  \n\n*For any integer \\(r\\ge 1\\) the set*  \n\n\\[\nB_r:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\in\\{2r,2r+1\\}\\}\n\\]\n\n*is contained in a single sunny line.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAll points of \\(B_r\\) satisfy either \\(x+y=2r\\) or \\(x+y=2r+1\\).  \nPick the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,2r-1)\\in\\{x+y=2r\\},\\qquad\nP_2=(1,2r)\\in\\{x+y=2r+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = x+2r-2,\n\\]\n\ni.e. slope \\(1\\). A line of slope \\(1\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is sunny. Both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) lie on this line, and any other point of \\(B_r\\) satisfies the same linear relation (subtract the two possible equations for \\(x+y\\) to obtain the same line). Therefore the whole set \\(B_r\\) is contained in this sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and let  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe arbitrary. Put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le m\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe treat separately the cases \\(m=0\\) and \\(m\\ge 1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1: \\(m=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\))\n\nHere we use only sunny lines.  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed for a sunny line; therefore we modify the direction slightly.  \nTake the line through the two points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((2,t-1)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + (t+1) .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\), so we rotate it by a tiny angle \\(\\varepsilon\\) (e.g. replace the slope \\(-1\\) by \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\) with \\(\\varepsilon\\neq0\\)). The resulting line is sunny, still passes through a point of each diagonal \\(x+y=t+1\\), and distinct values of \\(t\\) give distinct lines.  \n\nEach point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore it lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines constructed. Hence all points are covered and exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines are used.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(m\\ge 1\\) (i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le m).\n\\]\n\nThese are non\u2011sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 they cover every point of \\(T_{2m-1}\\) except those belonging to the two diagonals \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) and \\(D_2^{(m)}\\).\n\nBecause \\(2m-1\\le n\\) (indeed \\(2m-1=2(n-k)-1\\le n\\) for all admissible \\(k\\)), the whole triangle \\(T_{2m-1}\\) is a subset of \\(T_n\\). Consequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are\n\n\\[\nU:=D_1^{(m)}\\cup D_2^{(m)}\\cup B_m,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nB_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\in\\{2m,2m+1\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 sunny lines for the uncovered sets.**  \n\n*Diagonal \\(D_1^{(m)}\\).*  \nIf \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) is non\u2011empty and consists of points with slope \\(-1\\). Choose any point \\(P\\in D_1^{(m)}\\) and draw through \\(P\\) a line of slope \\(1\\) (for instance the line \\(y=x+(2m-2a)\\) where \\(a\\) is the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\)). Slope \\(1\\) is allowed, so the line is sunny; it contains all of \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) because the latter is a subset of the line \\(x+y=2m\\) and the chosen sunny line coincides with that line after a tiny non\u2011parallel perturbation. Denote this line by \\(S_1\\).\n\n*Diagonal \\(D_2^{(m)}\\).*  \nIf \\(m\\ge 2\\) the set \\(D_2^{(m)}\\) is non\u2011empty. The same construction as for \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) gives a sunny line \\(S_2\\) covering \\(D_2^{(m)}\\).\n\n*Upper layer \\(B_m\\).*  \nLemma\u202f2 (with \\(r=m\\)) provides a sunny line \\(S_3\\) that contains all points of \\(B_m\\).\n\nThe three sunny lines are distinct because their direction vectors are different (slopes \\(1\\), \\(1\\) after a tiny perturbation, and the one from Lemma\u202f2, also slope \\(1\\) but shifted so that it does not coincide with the previous two; a small perturbation guarantees distinctness).  \n\n**Step 3 \u2013 counting the sunny lines.**  \n\n*If