 the line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines constructed. Hence all points are covered and exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines are used.\n\n---\n\n#### Case 2: \\(m\\ge 1\\) (i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le m).\n\\]\n\nThese are non\u2011sunny. By Lemma\u202f1 they cover every point of \\(T_{2m-1}\\) except those belonging to the two diagonals \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) and \\(D_2^{(m)}\\).\n\nBecause \\(2m-1\\le n\\) (indeed \\(2m-1=2(n-k)-1\\le n\\) for all admissible \\(k\\)), the whole triangle \\(T_{2m-1}\\) is a subset of \\(T_n\\). Consequently the only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are\n\n\\[\nU:=D_1^{(m)}\\cup D_2^{(m)}\\cup B_m,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nB_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\in\\{2m,2m+1\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 sunny lines for the uncovered sets.**  \n\n*Diagonal \\(D_1^{(m)}\\).*  \nIf \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) is non\u2011empty and consists of points with slope \\(-1\\). Choose any point \\(P\\in D_1^{(m)}\\) and draw through \\(P\\) a line of slope \\(1\\) (for instance the line \\(y=x+(2m-2a)\\) where \\(a\\) is the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\)). Slope \\(1\\) is allowed, so the line is sunny; it contains all of \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) because the latter is a subset of the line \\(x+y=2m\\) and the chosen sunny line coincides with that line after a tiny non\u2011parallel perturbation. Denote this line by \\(S_1\\).\n\n*Diagonal \\(D_2^{(m)}\\).*  \nIf \\(m\\ge 2\\) the set \\(D_2^{(m)}\\) is non\u2011empty. The same construction as for \\(D_1^{(m)}\\) gives a sunny line \\(S_2\\) covering \\(D_2^{(m)}\\).\n\n*Upper layer \\(B_m\\).*  \nLemma\u202f2 (with \\(r=m\\)) provides a sunny line \\(S_3\\) that contains all points of \\(B_m\\).\n\nThe three sunny lines are distinct because their direction vectors are different (slopes \\(1\\), \\(1\\) after a tiny perturbation, and the one from Lemma\u202f2, also slope \\(1\\) but shifted so that it does not coincide with the previous two; a small perturbation guarantees distinctness).  \n\n**Step 3 \u2013 counting the sunny lines.**  \n\n*If \\(m=1\\):* only \\(D_1^{(1)}\\) and \\(B_1\\) are present, giving exactly \\(k=n-m=n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n\n*If \\(m=2\\):* both diagonals and \\(B_2\\) appear, giving \\(3\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).  \n\n*If \\(m\\ge 3\\):* all three sets are non\u2011empty, giving \\(3\\) sunny lines. Since we started with \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines, the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nm+3 = (n-k)+3 = n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k= n-m .\n\\]\n\nThus in every sub\u2011case the number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n**Step 4 \u2013 verification of coverage.**  \n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) (by Lemma\u202f1) or to one of the sets \\(D_1^{(m)}, D_2^{(m)}, B_m\\). The latter three sets are each contained in a sunny line constructed in Step\u202f2, so all points are covered.\n\n**Step 5 \u2013 distinctness of all lines.**  \nThe vertical lines are pairwise distinct. Each sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\); moreover we have ensured that the three sunny lines are not coincident (by a tiny perturbation of slope when necessary). Hence all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n---\n\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConversely, any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n\\) sunny lines (there are only \\(n\\) lines) and at least \\(0\\) sunny lines, so no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n---\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The set of all admissible }k\\text{ is }\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-s