ical line \\(x=1\\) when \\(t=2\\), or by the diagonal line\n\\(x+y=2\\) when \\(t=2\\).  By a careful choice of the indices \\(t\\) that are removed (for\ninstance, remove the lines corresponding to the largest values of \\(t\\)), we can\ndecrease the number of sunny lines from \\(n-3\\) down to the minimal value \\(k_{0}\\).\n\nConversely, to obtain **more** sunny lines, we start from the minimal configuration\nwith exactly \\(k_{0}\\) sunny lines (obtained by keeping only the necessary non\u2011sunny lines)\nand then **replace** one of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines by a new sunny line of a fresh\nslope that does not intersect any previously used sunny line at a lattice point of\n\\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).  Such a replacement adds one sunny line while still covering all\nrequired points, because the new sunny line can be chosen to pass through a point that\nwas previously covered by the removed non\u2011sunny line.  Repeating this operation we can\nincrease the number of sunny lines by one at each step, up to the trivial extreme where\nall \\(n\\) lines are sunny (simply discard the three non\u2011sunny lines and keep the \\(n\\) sunny\nlines already present in (6)).\n\nHence for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{k_{0},k_{0}+1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \\(\\qquad\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\text{ is admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \u220e"
