l P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause there are only three forbidden directions, \\(s\\le3\\).  Substituting the maximal\npossible value \\(s=3\\) into (9) gives\n\n\\[\n3n+kn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\,}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (sufficient constructions)\n\nDefine the three non\u2011sunny lines (4).  For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le n+1\\) choose a\nslope \\(m_t\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) distinct from all previously chosen slopes and set the line\n\\(L_t\\) as in (5).  The line \\(L_t\\) contains precisely the points \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with\n\\(a+b=t\\).\n\nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=\\{L_h,L_v,L_d\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas \\(n+2\\) members, but \\(L_3\\) coincides with \\(L_d\\); after deleting this duplicate we obtain\nexactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covering all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  In this family the three\nlines (4) are non\u2011sunny and the remaining \\(n-3\\) lines are sunny, so we have realised\n\\(k=n-3\\).\n\nStarting from this configuration we can **remove** sunny lines one by one and **replace**\nthem by the non\u2011sunny lines (4).  After removing \\(r\\) sunny lines we are left with\n\\(k=n-3-r\\) sunny lines.  The removal process can be continued until the number of sunny\nlines reaches the lower bound (10); indeed, when \\(k\\) reaches \\(\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) the\nremaining non\u2011sunny lines are still sufficient to cover the points whose sums equal the\nsmallest few integers, and the already present sunny lines cover the rest.  Hence a\nconfiguration with the minimal admissible number  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nexists.\n\nConversely, beginning with the minimal configuration we may **replace*