"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** non\u2011negative integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds by the **extremal principle**.  \nWe first exhibit a configuration with **no** sunny line (\\(k=0\\)): the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nNext we show that, starting from this configuration, we can **replace** any chosen non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property, provided we keep at least one non\u2011sunny line of each of the three forbidden directions (vertical, horizontal, and slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThe extremal argument is: assume we have a configuration with the **maximum possible** number of sunny lines. If fewer than \\(n\\) sunny lines are present, we prove that one of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines can be rotated (keeping it distinct from the others) to become sunny without losing any covered lattice point. This contradicts maximality, forcing the number of sunny lines to be \\(n\\).  \n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can construct a configuration containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines: start from the all\u2011non\u2011sunny configuration (\\(k=0\\)) and successively replace \\(n-k\\) of its lines by sunny ones using the replacement lemma proved by the extremal argument.  \n\nThus all values \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\) are attainable.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n\\(|T_n|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf e_{-1}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base configuration, \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}L_c\\supseteq T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacement Lemma)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume that among \\(\\mathcal L\\) there is at least one line parallel to each of the three forbidden directions \\(\\mathbf e_x,\\mathbf e_y,\\mathbf e_{-1}\\).  \nThen there exists a line \\(L\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\) that can be replaced by a **sunny** line \\(L'\\) (different from all other lines) such that the new family still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof (extremal principle).*  \nChoose a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) with the **maximum possible** number of sunny lines under the hypothesis that each forbidden direction is represented at least once.  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are already sunny we are done.  \nOtherwise pick a non\u2011sunny line, say \\(L_0\\), parallel to \\(\\mathbf e_x\\) (the vertical case; the horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) cases are analogous).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\in L_0\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered **only** by \\(L_0\\).  \nIf \\(S=\\varnothing\\) we may delete \\(L_0\\) and replace it by any sunny line through a point of \\(T_n\\) (for instance the line through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(2\\)), contradicting maximality.  \nHence \\(S\\neq\\varnothing\\).\n\nPick \\((a_0,b_0)\\in S\\). Because \\(L_0\\) is vertical, \\(a_0\\) is fixed for all points of \\(S\\).  \nConsider the family of all sunny lines passing through \\((a_0,b_0)\\); they are precisely the lines  \n\n\\[\ny-(b_0)=m\\,(x-a_0),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nOnly finitely many of these lines can coincide with an existing line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (at most \\(n-1\\) of them).  \nConsequently we can choose a slope \\(m\\) avoiding those finitely many forbidden slopes; the resulting line \\(L'\\) is sunny, distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), and passes through \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\nNow replace \\(L_0\\) by \\(L'\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) that were covered by \\(L_0\\) other than \\((a_0,b_0)\\) remain covered because each such point also lies on some other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (otherwise it would belong to \\(S\\)).  \nThe point \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is now covered by \\(L'\\).  \nThus the new family still covers \\(T_n\\) and has **one more** sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\), contradicting the maximality of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence the assumption that a non\u2011sunny line must remain is impossible; a replacement as claimed always exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the base configuration of Lemma\u202f1, which has \\(k=0\\).  \nIf \\(k=n\\) we apply Lemma\u202f2 repeatedly: at each step we replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property.  \nBecause Lemma\u202f2 guarantees that a replacement is always possible as long as at least one line of each forbidden direction remains, we can perform the replacement until **all** lines are sunny, obtaining a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\nFor an interme