nes in total, we cannot replace more than one vertical line by a sunny line without leaving some points uncovered.\n\n   This observation shows that the simple replacement strategy used before cannot be extended to obtain \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion of the partial result.**  \n   The construction with the \\(n\\) vertical lines proves that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n   The failure of the previous construction and the geometric obstruction described above indicate that a different, more sophisticated method would be required to achieve \\(k\\ge 1\\). At present I have not succeeded in finding such a method, so the problem remains open for \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by vertical lines)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) because \\(a\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\), which is one of the \\(L_i\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Points on the line \\(S_i\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nS_i\\cap\\mathbb Z^2=\\{(i,1),(i+1,2),\\dots ,(n,n+1-i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWriting the equation as \\(y = x-i+1\\) shows that for any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\) the point \\((i+t,\\,t+1)\\) satisfies the equation, and conversely any integer solution must be of this form. The condition \\(i+t\\le n\\) forces \\(t\\le n-i\\), i.e. \\(y\\le n+1-i\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------