"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with **no** sunny line, and also with **exactly one** sunny line; but it is impossible to satisfy the conditions with two or more sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The requirement is that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen \\(n\\) lines.\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   are all non\u2011sunny and together cover \\(T_n\\) (each point has its first coordinate equal to some \\(i\\)).  \n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Keep the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   Replace the vertical line \\(x=n\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\;y=x-(n-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S\\) passes through the unique point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Hence the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{1}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   *Key observation.* A sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently it meets each vertical line \\(x=j\\) in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   In the set \\(T_n\\) the two right\u2011most columns contain more than one point:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{column }x=n-1:\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n   &\\text{column }x=n:\\;(n,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   If a column is not covered by a vertical line, the points of that column must be supplied by sunny lines. Because each sunny line can contribute at most one point to a given column, covering the two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) would require **two distinct sunny lines** if the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) were omitted.  \n   Since we are allowed at most \\(n\\) lines in total, omitting **both** vertical lines \\(x=n-1\\) and \\(x=n\\) would force us to use at least three sunny lines (two for column \\(n-1\\) and one for column \\(n\\)), contradicting the assumption that the total number of sunny lines is \\(k\\ge 2\\) while the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).  \n\n   Therefore at least one of the two right\u2011most columns must be covered by a vertical line. If exactly one of them is covered vertically, the other column still contains two points (the column \\(x=n-1\\) when \\(x=n\\) is vertical, or vice\u2011versa) and would need at least two sunny lines, giving \\(k\\ge 2\\) but also requiring more than \\(n\\) lines in total. Hence **it is impossible to have \\(k\\ge 2\\)**.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); the impossibility argument shows that no larger \\(k\\) can occur. Thus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}=\\{L_i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad L_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(L_i\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.*  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n\\) and, of course, \\(a\\ge 1\\). 