]\n\nwhich is neither \\(0\\) nor undefined nor \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently the line  \n\n\\[\nL^{\\ast}:\\; y = -2(x-c)+(n+2-c)= -2x + (2c+n+2-c)= -2x + (c+n+2)\n\\]\n\nis sunny.\n\nObserve that \\(P_1\\) lies on the original vertical line \\(x=c\\); thus after removing \\(L\\) we lose coverage of \\(P_1\\), but we regain it via \\(L^{\\ast}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) are either on \\(L^{\\ast}\\) (namely \\(P_2\\)) or already belong to \\(T_n\\) and are covered by the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\}\\).  \n\nFinally, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'' = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L\\})\\cup\\{L^{\\ast},\\,L_{n+1}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(L_{n+1}\\) is the vertical line supplied by Lemma\u202f1, consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\), and covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  Take the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Each of the three points lies on at least one of them, and none of the lines is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Use the sunny line \\(x+y=3\\) (which passes through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)), together with the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\) (covering \\((1,1)\\)).  \n  Add any third line distinct from the previous two, for instance \\(y=3\\); it does not affect the covering property.  \n  Exactly one of the three lines is sunny.\n\n* \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n  Any sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line can contain at most one of the three points of \\(T_3\\).  \n  Hence with two sunny lines we could cover at most two points, leaving the third uncovered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive construction\n\nAssume the theorem holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\); i.e. for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Construction of a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).*  \nApply Lemma\u202f1 to \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\).  \nWe obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k)}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Construction of a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) (provided \\(k\\le n-2\\)).*  \nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), the family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines.  \nPick one non\u2011sunny line and apply Lemma\u202f2.  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k+1)}\\) has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(T_{n+1}\\), and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus, assuming the statement for \\(n\\), we have produced configurations for \\(n+1\\) with every admissible number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\((n+1)-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies configurations for \\(k=0,1\\), i.e. for all integers \\(0\\le k\\le 3-2\\).  \nThe inductive step shows that if the claim holds for a given \\(n\\), then it holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nTherefore, by mathematical induction, the claim holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) contained \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nSince a sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, each sunny line can contain at most one point of the set \\(T_n\\) (otherwise two points of \\(T_n\\) would have the same difference of coordinates, forcing the line to have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)). 