"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof is by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n* **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n  Explicit configurations are given for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\); a configuration with \\(k=2\\) is shown to be impossible because a sunny line can contain at most one of the three required points.\n\n* **Inductive step.**  \n  Assume the statement holds for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n  From a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) we construct:\n\n  1. **Lemma\u202f1 (extension without adding a sunny line).**  \n     Adding a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (which is not sunny) yields a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n  2. **Lemma\u202f2 (extension by adding one sunny line).**  \n     Replacing a non\u2011sunny vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line through the two new points  \n     \\[\n     P_{1}=(c,\\;n+2-c),\\qquad P_{2}=(c+1,\\;n-c)\n     \\]\n     (slope \\(-2\\)) gives a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\).\n\n  Lemma\u202f1 provides the case \\(k\\) for \\(n+1\\); Lemma\u202f2 provides the case \\(k+1\\).  \n  Since the induction hypothesis guarantees configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), the step yields configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2\\).\n\n* **Upper bound.**  \n  Any sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can contain **at most one** point of the set  \n  \\[\n  T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n  \\]\n  Because \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny.  \n\nThus the induction together with the upper bound proves the theorem for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the covering set\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{the line }x+y=0\\ (\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 extending without creating a sunny line\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nThen for any integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{\\,x=c\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly the same number of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new points that appear in \\(T_{n+1}\\) but not in \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n+1}\\) satisfies \\(a=c\\) then it lies on the added vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq c\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and consequently \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); it is already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is covered.  \nThe line \\(x=c\\) is vertical, hence not sunny, so the number of sunny lines does not change. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 extending while increasing the number of sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nAssume \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains at least one non\u2011sunny line.  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(L^{\\ast}\\) such that replacing a suitable non\u2011sunny line by \\(L^{\\ast}\\) yields a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\). Consequently the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) has a non\u2011sunny line, after a possible rotation of the whole picture we may assume that this line is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x=c\\qquad (1\\le c\\le n).\n\\]\n\nConsider the two new points of \\(S_{n+1}\\)\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(c,\\;n+2-c),\\qquad \nP_{2}=(c+1,\\;n-c).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(a+b=n+2\\), hence belong to \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nThe line through \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{(n-c)-(n+2-c)}{(c+1)-c}= \\frac{-2}{1}=-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore this line is sunny.  \nDenote it by \\(L^{\\ast}\\).\n\nNow construct the new family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= (\\mathcal L_n\\setminus\\{L_0\\})\\cup\\{L^{\\ast},\\,x=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(x=c\\) (the same vertical line we removed) is re\u2011added, so the total number of lines is \\(n+1\\) and all are distinct.\n\nCoverage:\n\n* Every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered because we removed only \\(L_0\\) and re\u2011added it.\n* The point \\(P_{1}\\) lay on \\(L_0\\); after the replacement it lies on \\(L^{\\ast}\\).\n* The point \\(P_{2}\\) lies on \\(L^{\\ast}\\) by construction.\n* All other points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) have abscissa \\(a\\neq c\\) and therefore already belong to \\(T_n\\); they remain covered.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).  \nSince \\(L^{\\ast}\\) is sunny and the only new line that is sunny, the number of sunny lines has increased by exactly one. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Each required point lies on at least one of them; none is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the sunny line \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden, so we must avoid it; instead take the sunny line \\(y=-2x+5\\) which passes through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); note that \\((2,1)\\) is required).  \n  Together with the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\) (covering \\((1,1)\\)) and any third line distinct from them (e.g. \\(y=3\\)), we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n  A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line can contain at most one point of \\(T_3\\).  \n  With two sunny lines we could cover at most two of the three points, leaving one uncovered. Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\): the admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\) (i.e. \\(0\\le k\\le 3-2\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Inductive construction\n\nAssume the theorem holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\); that is, for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k)}\\).**  \n  Apply Lemma\u202f1 to \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\).  \n  The resulting family has \\(n+1\\) lines, still covers \\(T_{n+1}\\), and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{(k+1)}\\) (for \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n  Since \\(k\\le n-2\\), the family \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines.  \n  Choose one non\u2011sunny line and apply Lemma\u202f2.  \n  The obtained family has \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers \\(T_{n+1}\\), and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2,\n\\]\n\na suitable configuration for \\(n+1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nIf a line \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\) is sunny, its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nSuppose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the same sunny line.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(a_2\\neq a_1\\) and \\(b_2\\neq b_1\\).  \nMoreover, because both points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1)+(b_2-b_1)= (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\le (n+1)-(1+1)=n-1.\n\\]\n\nIf a sunny line contained two points of \\(T_n\\), the difference of their abscissae would be a non\u2011zero integer \\(d\\) with \\(|d|\\le n-1\\).  \nRepeating this argument for all sunny lines shows that each sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\); otherwise two points would give a slope belonging to the forbidden set (a short direct verification: the only slopes that allow two lattice points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny; otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n\\) points to cover.  \nHence any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Conclusion\n\nThe induction shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe upper\u2011bound argument shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
