f \u201csunny\u201d depends on fixed directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)). Rotating the whole picture would change which lines are considered sunny, so this step lacks justification. **Justification Gap** (the argument assumes we may rotate without affecting the problem\u2019s constraints).\n  2. Definition of points \\(P_{1}=(c, n+2-c)\\) and \\(P_{2}=(c+1, n-c)\\). Both indeed satisfy \\(a+b=n+2\\), so they belong to the new set \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n  3. Computation of the slope of the line through \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\) as \\(-2\\) is correct; this line is sunny.\n  4. Construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}= (\\mathcal L_n\\setminus\\{L_0\\})\\cup\\{L^{\\ast},\\,x=c\\}\\).  \n     - This adds **two** lines after removing one, giving a total of \\(n+1\\) lines, as intended.\n  5. **Coverage claim:**  \n     - \u201cEvery point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered because we removed only \\(L_0\\) and re\u2011added it.\u201d Correct.  \n     - \u201cThe point \\(P_{1}\\) lay on \\(L_0\\); after the replacement it lies on \\(L^{\\ast}\\).\u201d Correct.  \n     - \u201cThe point \\(P_{2}\\) lies on \\(L^{\\ast}\\) by construction.\u201d Correct.  \n     - **Critical Error:** \u201cAll other points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) have abscissa \\(a\\neq c\\) and therefore already belong to \\(T_n\\); they remain covered.\u201d This is false: by definition, every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+2\\), which exceeds the bound \\(n+1\\) required for membership in \\(T_n\\). Hence these points are **not** already covered, and the proof provides no line that contains them. Consequently the constructed family does **not** cover all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\). This invalidates Lemma\u202f2.\n\n- Because Lemma\u202f2 fails, any subsequent inductive step that relies on it is unsound.\n\n#### 2.4 Base case \\(n=3\\)\n- The description for \\(k=0\\) (three non\u2011sunny lines) correctly covers the three points.\n- For \\(k=1\\): the solution claims to use the line \\(x+y=3\\) but then says \