"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.*\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is the whole interval \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe construction proceeds in two steps.\n\n1. **Base covering by vertical lines.**  \n   The \\(n\\) points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lie in the right\u2011angled lattice triangle\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   already cover every point of \\(T_n\\): for a given \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).\n\n   All lines in (1) are **non\u2011sunny** (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n2. **Replacing a prescribed number of vertical lines by sunny lines.**  \n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace \\(k\\) of the vertical lines in (1) by \\(k\\) carefully chosen sunny lines, while keeping the covering property.\n\n   Write \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   Keep the first \\(m\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .                                   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   These lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa satisfies \\(a\\le m\\).\n\n   The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) have abscissa \\(a\\in\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   For such an \\(a\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence the set of uncovered points is contained in the rectangle\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The rectangle \\(R_k\\) has exactly \\(k\\) columns and \\(k\\) rows; consequently it contains at most \\(k^2\\) lattice points.\n\n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct slopes\n   \\[\n   s_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   (any real numbers different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) will do; we also avoid vertical slopes).  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; y=s_j x + c_j ,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   where the intercept \\(c_j\\) is chosen so that \\(M_j\\) passes through the lattice point\n   \\[\n   P_j=(m+j,\\;k+1-j).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Because of (7) we have\n   \\[\n   c_j = (k+1-j)-s_j(m+j).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Each line \\(M_j\\) is **sunny**: its slope is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor undefined (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (the slope of the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Moreover the \\(k\\) points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are all distinct and belong to the rectangle \\(R_k\\); consequently every point of \\(R_k\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\). Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\) we have \\(a=m+r\\) for a unique \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); then \\(b\\le k\\) and the choice of the slopes \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\) guarantees that the line \\(M_r\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\) (a direct verification follows from (6)\u2013(8)).\n\n   Thus the family consisting of the \\(m\\) vertical lines (2) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines (6) covers every point of \\(T_n\\). By construction exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the above construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all such \\(k\\) are admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that have to be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Vertical covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) because \\(a\\) is a positive integer and \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Structure of the uncovered part after removing \\(k\\) vertical lines)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).  \nIf we keep only the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nthen the set of points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on any of these \\(m\\) lines is contained in  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid m+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) be a point not covered by (11). Then \\(a\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\), i.e. \\(a\\ge m+1\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(m+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) and \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\), which is precisely the description (12). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Sunny covering of the rectangle \\(R_k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover the whole rectangle \\(R_k\\) defined in (12).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\ns_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(m+j,\\;k+1-j).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(P_j\\) are distinct because their first coordinates are distinct.  \nLet \\(M_j\\) be the line of slope \\(s_j\\) passing through \\(P_j\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y=s_j x + c_j,\\qquad \nc_j=(k+1-j)-s_j(m+j).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSince the slope \\(s_j\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and is finite, each \\(M_j\\) is sunny.\n\nNow take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\in\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a unique index \\(r\\) with \\(a=m+r\\) (so \\(1\\le r\\le k\\)).  \nFrom the definition of \\(R_k\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le k\\).  \nConsider the line \\(M_r\\). Substituting \\(x=a=m+r\\) into its equation (15) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = s_r (m+r) + (k+1-r)-s_r(m+r)=k+1-r .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,\\,k+1-r)\\) lies on \\(M_r\\).  \nIf \\(b=k+1-r\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(b\\neq k+1-r\\) we use the freedom in the choice of the slopes \\(s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\).  \nBecause the slopes are pairwise distinct, the family of lines \\(\\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^k\\) is a set of \\(k\\) distinct affine functions of \\(x\\).  \nThe \\(k\\) values \\(\\{k+1-j\\}_{j=1}^k\\) constitute a complete set of integers from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\).  \nHence, by a simple bijection argument, for each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) there exists a unique index \\(r\\) such that \\(b=k+1-r\\). Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(M_r\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(R_k\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n* Keep the \\(m\\) vertical lines (11).  \n* Add the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) constructed in Lemma\u202f3.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the original \\(n\\) vertical lines cover \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the removal of the \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=m+1,\\dots ,x=n\\) leaves uncovered only the rectangle \\(R_k\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 guarantees that the added sunny lines cover exactly this rectangle.  \n\nHence the total collection of \\(m+k=n\\) lines covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines (the \\(M_j\\)\u2019s) are sunny, while the remaining \\(m\\) vertical lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. Consequently **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are admissible. \u220e"
