ecisely (8). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Sunny covering of the rectangle \\(R_k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover the whole rectangle \\(R_k\\) defined in (8).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n\\[\ns_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(m+j,\\;k+1-j).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(P_j\\) are distinct because their first coordinates are distinct.  \nLet \\(M_j\\) be the line of slope \\(s_j\\) passing through \\(P_j\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y=s_jx+c_j,\\qquad \nc_j=(k+1-j)-s_j(m+j).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince the slope \\(s_j\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and is finite, each \\(M_j\\) is sunny.\n\nNow take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in R_k\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\in\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a unique index \\(r\\) with \\(a=m+r\\) (\\(1\\le r\\le k\\)).  \nFrom the definition of \\(R_k\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le k\\).  \n\nConsider the line \\(M_r\\). Substituting \\(x=a=m+r\\) into (11) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = s_r(m+r)+(k+1-r)-s_r(m+r)=k+1-r .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((a,\\,k+1-r)\\) lies on \\(M_r\\).  \nIf \\(b=k+1-r\\) we are done.  \n\nIf \\(b\\neq k+1-r\\) we use the other lines.  \nFor the fixed column \\(a=m+r\\) the values \\(\\{s_j a+c_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) are precisely the \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \nIndeed, for \\(j=r\\) the value equals \\(k+1-r\\) by (12); for any \\(j\\neq r\\) the expression\n\\(s_j a+c_j\\) differs from \\(k+1-r\\) because the slopes \\(s_j\\) are distinct, and the resulting numbers fill the remaining integers \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \nConsequently there exists a unique index \\(j\\) such that \\(s_j a+c_j=b\\); the corresponding line \\(M_j\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\nHence every point o