a\\) and \\(b''-b\\) to be integer multiples of \\(a'-a\\) and \\(b'-b\\) respectively, which forces \\(a''\\) or \\(b''\\) to be non\u2011positive or to violate \\(a''+b''\\le n+1\\). Hence no third point can belong to the line.\n\n3. Using Lemma\u202f2, each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since there are \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points to be covered, the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2n\\).\n\n4. For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-2n = \\frac{n(n-3)}{2}\\ge 0\\) with equality only at \\(n=3\\).  \n   Even for \\(n=3\\) the left\u2011hand side equals \\(6\\) while the right\u2011hand side equals \\(6\\); in this borderline case the equality would require every sunny line to contain exactly two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   However, Lemma\u202f2 shows that a sunny line can contain at most two points, and the configuration in which each of the three lines contains exactly two points would force the three lines to be pairwise parallel (they would all have to pass through the same two points), contradicting the requirement that the lines be distinct.\n\n5. Hence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction.  \n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nAssume, for the sake of contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(L_i\\);  \n* each \\(L_i\\) is *sunny*, i.e. its slope \\(m_i\\) satisfies \\(m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a line with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in T_n\\cap L\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\), then \\(a\\) and \\(a'\\) are consecutive integers.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\).  \nFrom \\((a,b)\\in L\\) we have \\(c=b-ma\\); from \\((a',b')\\in L\\) we have \\(c=b'-ma'\\).  \nSubtracting yields  \n\n\\[\nb-b' = m(a-a').\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq 0\\) and \\(a\\neq a'\\), the integer \\(a-a'\\) divides the integer \\(b-b'\\).  \nBecause \\(|a-a'|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|b-b'|\\le n\\), the only possibility compatible with (1) and \\(m\\neq -1\\) is \\(|a-a'|=1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the \\(x\\)-coordinates must be pairwise consecutive, say \\(a_2=a_1+1\\) and \\(a_3=a_2+1\\).  \nApplying (1) to the pairs \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1 = m,\\qquad b_3-b_2 = m .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b_3-b_1 = 2m\\).  \nSince \\(b_i\\) are integers, \\(m\\) must be an integer.  \nIf \\(m= -1\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the sunny condition.  \nIf \\(m=0\\) the line would be horizontal, also forbidden.  \nThus \\(m\\) is a non\u2011zero integer different from \\(-1\\).  \n\nNow \\(a_3+b_3 = (a_1+2)+(b_1+2m) = (a_1+b_1)+2+2m\\).  \nBecause \\((a_1,b_1)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a_1+b_1\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(m\\ge 1\\) or \\(m\\le -2\\), the quantity \\(a_3+b_3\\) exceeds \\(n+1\\), contradicting \\((a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWith \\(n\\) sunny lines the total number of covered points is therefore at most \\(2n\\).\n\nHowever  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 4,\n\\]\n\nand for \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|T_3|=6=2\\cdot3\\).  \nIn the case \\(n=3\\) equality would require every sunny line to contain exactly two points of \\(T_3\\).  \nBut Lemma\u202f2 shows that a sunny line can contain at most two points, and if th