            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (the hypotenuse is the line \\(a+b=n+1\\)).\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny  \n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1 (At most two lattice points per sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\nSuppose that three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\cap L\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\) exist.\nFrom the equation of the line we have  \n\n\\[\nb_i-ma_i=c\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the equalities for \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1). \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nSince the points are distinct, \\(a_2\\neq a_1\\); hence \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a non\u2011zero integer.\nEquation (2.2) shows that \\(m\\) is a rational number whose denominator divides\n\\(a_2-a_1\\).  The same reasoning with the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nb_3-b_2=m(a_3-a_2). \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting (2.2) from (2.3) gives  \n\n\\[\n(b_3-b_2)-(b_2-b_1)=m\\bigl[(a_3-a_2)-(a_2-a_1)\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nb_3-2b_2+b_1=m\\,(a_3-2a_2+a_1). \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nBecause all three points belong to \\(T_n\\), the differences\n\\(a_3-a_2\\) and \\(a_2-a_1\\) are integers of the same sign (otherwise two of the points would lie on a vertical or horizontal line, contradicting the sunny condition).  \nConsequently the second factors in (2.4) are non\u2011zero integers of the same sign, forcing  \n\n\\[\n|m|=\\frac{|b_3-2b_2+b_1|}{|a_3-2a_2+a_1|}\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|m|>1\\) then from (2.2) we obtain \\(|b_2-b_1|>|a_2-a_1|\\ge 1\\), whence\n\\(b_2\\ge b_1+2\\).  Adding the inequalities \\(a_1\\ge 1,\\;b_1\\ge 1\\) and using\n\\(a_2+b_2\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na_1+b_1\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nRepeating the same argument for the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) yields\n\\(a_3+b_3\\le n-1\\), contradicting the fact that the third point must satisfy\n\\(a_3+b_3\\ge a_1+b_1+2\\ge n+1\\).  \n\nHence \\(|m|=1\\).  The case \\(m=1\\) gives a line parallel to \\(y=x\\), which is allowed, but then (2.2) forces \\(a_2-a_1=b_2-b_1\\).  Since the points lie in the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only possibility is \\(a_2-a_1=b_2-b_1=1\\).  Applying the same to the pair \\((a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\) forces \\(a_3-a_2=b_3-b_2=1\\), whence\n\\(a_3+b_3=a_1+b_1+4\\).  Because \\(a_1+b_1\\ge 2\\), we obtain \\(a_3+b_3\\ge 6\\), which exceeds \\(n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Thus a third point cannot exist.\n\nThe case \\(m=-1\\) is excluded by the definition of a sunny line.  Therefore a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Upper bound for the number of covered points)\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most \\(2n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2.1 each line contributes at most two points, and the lines are distinct, so the total is bounded by \\(2n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 2.3 (No configuration with \\(k=n\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is **no** set of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (2.1) we have \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n,\n\\]\n\nand for \\(n=3\\) we have equality \\(\\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6=2\\cdot3\\).\nLemma\u202f2.2 shows that at most \\(2n\\) points can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines,\nhence for \\(n\\ge 4\\) covering all \\(|T_n|\\) points is impossible.\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) equality would require every sunny line to contain exactly two points of \\(T_3\\).\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of them and is not horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\) must be of slope \\(1\\) and pass through a pair of points of the form \\((i,j),(i+1,j+1)\\).\nThere are only three such pairs:\n\\((1,1)-(2,2),\\;(1,2)-(2,3)\\) (the latter is not in \\(T_3\\)), and \\((2,1)-(3,2)\\) (the latter is not in \\(T_3\\)).\nThus at most two distinct sunny lines can be formed, contradicting the requirement of three distinct lines.\nHence the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.1  The basic non\u2011sunny framework  \n\nConsider the following three lines, all of which are **not** sunny:\n\n* \\(L_H:\\; y=1\\)\u2003(horizontal);\n* \\(L_V:\\; x=1\\)\u2003(vertical);\n* \\(L_D:\\; x+y=n+1\\)\u2003(the hypotenuse of the triangle).\n\nThese three lines together contain all points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the\nboundary of the triangle.  The interior points are those with  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2,\\; a+b\\le n .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThe set of interior points is a smaller triangle of the same shape,\ncall it \\(T'_n\\).  Its size is  \n\n\\[\n|T'_n|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.2  Reducing the interior to a thin strip  \n\nChoose an integer  \n\n\\[\nr:=\\max\\{0,\\,k- (n-3)\\}\\qquad (0\\le r\\le k).\n\\]\n\nWe shall cover the interior points by two additional **non\u2011sunny** lines\nand by at most \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* Add the vertical line \\(x=2\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the horizontal line\n  \\(y=2\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n  These two lines together contain all points of \\(T'_n\\) whose\n  coordinates satisfy \\(a=2\\) or \\(b=2\\).  After removing them, the\n  remaining uncovered points form the even smaller triangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T''_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 3,\\;b\\ge 3,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  whose cardinality is  \n\n  \\[\n  |T''_n|=\\frac{(n-4)(n-3)}{2}. \\tag{2.7}\n  \\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge n-3\\) we may stop here: we have used exactly three non\u2011sunny\nlines (\\(L_H,L_V,L_D\\)) plus the two lines \\(x=2\\) and \\(y=2\\), i.e. **five**\nnon\u2011sunny lines.  The remaining \\(n-5\\) lines can be taken arbitrarily\nas sunny lines; they are not needed to cover any further points because\n\\(T''_n\\) is empty when \\(n\\le 5\\) and for larger \\(n\\) we will cover it\nwith sunny lines as described below.  In any case the number of sunny\nlines used does not exceed \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.3  Covering the remaining thin triangle with sunny lines  \n\nAssume now that after the previous step we still have a non\u2011empty set\n\\(T''_n\\).  Observe that every line of slope \\(1\\) (which is **not**\nsunny) would again be parallel to the hypotenuse, so we must use sunny\nlines of a different slope.  Choose the slope  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{1}{2}\\qquad(\\text{any rational number }\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\text{ works}).\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n3\\le t\\le n-3,\n\\]\n\nconsider the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = \\frac12 x + \\bigl(t-\\tfrac12\\cdot 3\\bigr)\n      = \\frac12 x + \\bigl(t-\\tfrac32\\bigr). \\tag{2.8}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope is \\(\\frac12\\), the line passes through at most two\nlattice points of the integer lattice; indeed the points are\n\\((2t-3,\\,t-1)\\) and \\((2t-1,\\,t)\\), both of which satisfy (2.5) and\ntherefore belong to \\(T''_n\\).  Moreover, distinct values of \\(t\\) give\ndistinct lines, and each line \\(S_t\\) is sunny (its slope is neither\n\\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).\n\nThe collection \\(\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n-3\\}\\) contains exactly\n\\(n-5\\) lines, and together they cover **all** points of \\(T''_n\\):\nevery point \\((a,b)\\in T''_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(a,b\\ge 3\\);\nhence \\(b = \\frac12 a + c\\) for a unique integer \\(c\\) with\n\\(3\\le c\\le n-3\\), namely \\(c = b-\\frac12 a\\).  Thus the point lies on the\ncorresponding line \\(S_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3.4  Counting the sunny lines  \n\nSummarising the construction:\n\n| Type of line | Number | Sunny? |\n|--------------|--------|--------|\n| \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) | 1 | No |\n| \\(x=1\\) (vertical)   | 1 | No |\n| \\(x+y=n+1\\) (hypotenuse) | 1 | No |\n| \\(x=2\\) (vertical)   | 1 | No |\n| \\(y=2\\) (horizontal) | 1 | No |\n| Lines \\(S_t\\) (slope \\(1/2\\)) | \\(n-5\\) | Yes |\n\nThus we have used exactly \\(n-5\\) sunny lines.  Since \\(k\\) can be any\ninteger with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), we can adjust the construction as follows:\n\n*If \\(k\\ge n-5\\)*, keep the above family; it already contains \\(n-5\\le k\\)\nsunny lines, and we may add \\(k-(n-5)\\) further sunny lines of arbitrary\nslopes (they will not be needed for covering any point, but they increase\nthe count of sunny lines to exactly \\(k\\)).\n\n*If \\(k< n-5\\)*, we simply omit \\(n-5-k\\) of the lines \\(S_t\\) and replace\neach omitted line by a non\u2011sunny line of one of the three allowed\ndirections (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  Because each omitted\n\\(S_t\\) covered exactly two interior points, we can cover those two points\ninstead by a short horizontal or vertical segment that coincides with an\nalready existing non\u2011sunny line; the distinctness of the lines is\npreserved because we introduce a new line parallel to an existing one but\nshifted by at least one unit, which does not affect the covering property.\nConsequently the total number of sunny lines becomes exactly \\(k\\).\n\nIn every case we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Conclusion  \n\n* Proposition\u202f2.3 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n* The explicit construction above proves that for every integer\n  \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
