e would force the slope to be \\(\\pm1\\), contradicting the definition. The argument is flawed:\n\n1. The deduction that the differences \\(a_3-a_2\\) and \\(a_2-a_1\\) must have the same sign because otherwise two points would be on a vertical or horizontal line is incorrect. Two distinct points can have opposite signs for the \\(a\\)-differences without creating a vertical/horizontal line; the sign only tells us which point is to the right of the other.\n\n2. Even if the sign argument were accepted, the conclusion that \\(|m|\\ge1\\) and then that \\(|m|=1\\) does not follow. For example, the line \\(y=2x-1\\) (slope \\(2\\)) passes through the lattice points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); both belong to \\(T_3\\). Adding a third point \\((3,5)\\) would leave the triangle, but for larger \\(n\\) a line of slope \\(2\\) can contain three points of \\(T_n\\) (e.g. \\((1,1),(2,3),(3,5)\\) are all in \\(T_6\\)). Thus the \u201cat most two points\u201d claim is false.\n\n3. A concrete counter\u2011example: for \\(n=5\\), the line \\(y=x\\) (slope\u202f1, which is sunny) contains the three points \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\) of \\(T_5\\). Hence the lemma is disproved.\n\nBecause the lemma is false, the subsequent Lemma\u202f2.2 and Proposition\u202f2.3, which depend on it, are unsupported.\n\n**Conclusion:** Critical error \u2013 the statement of Lemma\u202f2.1 is false, and its proof contains multiple logical gaps.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2.2  \n> \u201cIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most \\(2n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** This follows directly from Lemma\u202f2.1. Since Lemma\u202f2.1 is invalid, Lemma\u202f2.2 has no foundation. The step itself is correct *if* Lemma\u202f2.1 were true, but because the premise is false, this result is not established.\n\n#### Proposition\u202f2.3  \n> \u201cNo configuration with \\(k=n\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:** The proposition uses the inequality \\(|T_n| > 2n\\) (for \\(n\\g