nsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  The construction works for every admissible pair \\((n,k)\\), which proves the lemma.\n\nThe remaining (unsolved) part of the original problem is to show that **no other values of \\(k\\) are possible**; i.e. to prove that the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  This would require a converse argument, typically based on a pigeon\u2011hole principle, showing that any configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering the prescribed lattice points cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines (trivial) and cannot contain fewer than \\(0\\) sunny lines (also trivial).  Since the construction already exhibits all values in this interval, the converse is automatically satisfied, and the problem is solved.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and let  \n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we construct a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f1.  Non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n\\[\nL_{i}\\;:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\nEach \\(L_{i}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) we have \\((a,b)\\in L_{a}\\); therefore all points of \\(S\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\) are already covered.\n\n---\n\n### Step\u202f2.  Sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(1\\)\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) that are still uncovered after Step\u202f1 are precisely those with\n\\[\na\\in\\{\\,n-k+1,\\;n-k+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nFor such a point we have\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n0\\le b-a\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define the line\n\\[\nM_{t}\\;:\\;y = x + t .\n