y.\n\nThe final set of \\(n\\) lines is obtained by taking the \\(k\\) sunny pairing lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) together with \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines\n\\(D_{c_1},\\dots ,D_{c_{\\,n-k}}\\) chosen so that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\setminus S\\)\nlies on one of the selected diagonals.  Because the diagonals \\(D_c\\) are nested\n(\\(D_{c}\\subset D_{c+1}\\)), it is always possible to select \\(n-k\\) of them that\ncover the remaining points; a concrete choice is described in the detailed proof.\n\nThe construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); consequently all such\n\\(k\\) are admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints; for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nD_c:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid x+y=c\\}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(|D_c|=c-1\\).  The line \\(x+y=c\\) is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the\nforbidden line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a subset\n\\(S\\subseteq\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(|S|=2k\\) that can be partitioned into \\(k\\)\nunordered pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) such that each pair determines a sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose the first \\(2k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in lexicographic order,\ni.e.  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k),(2,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(k,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates,\nand distinct sums \\(x+y\\); consequently the line through any two distinct\npoints of \\(S\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to\n\\(x+