ng the admissibility of \\(k=0\\) and\n\\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\);\notherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non\u2011sunny.  \nThe set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with sum \\(c\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=c\\}=L_c\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nSince the possible sums range from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_0} (L\\cap\\mathcal P_n)=\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nThus the required covering is achieved with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe must produce \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains every point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n1. **Pairing the points.**  \n   List the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in any order.  Because \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is at least \\(n\\) (indeed strictly larger for \\(n\\ge3\\)),\n   we can select \\(2n\\) distinct points from \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Partition these\n   \\(2n\\) points into \\(n\\) unordered pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)).\n   The remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (if any) will be placed on the same\n   lines as the chosen pairs; this does not affect the argument.\n\n2. **Lines through the pairs.**  \n   For each pair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) let \