ny order.  Because \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is at least \\(n\\) (indeed strictly larger for \\(n\\ge3\\)),\n   we can select \\(2n\\) distinct points from \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Partition these\n   \\(2n\\) points into \\(n\\) unordered pairs \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) (\\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)).\n   The remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (if any) will be placed on the same\n   lines as the chosen pairs; this does not affect the argument.\n\n2. **Lines through the pairs.**  \n   For each pair \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the unique line passing through\n   the two points.  Because the two points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates,\n   different \\(y\\)-coordinates and different sums \\(x+y\\), the slope of\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  Consequently each\n   \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n3. **Covering all points.**  \n   By construction each of the \\(2n\\) selected points lies on some \\(\\ell_i\\).\n   Any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) coincides with one of the selected points\n   (the selection was made from \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) itself), or can be added to a\n   pair without destroying the distinct\u2011coordinate property; thus it also lies\n   on one of the \\(\\ell_i\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{i=1}^{n} (\\ell_i\\cap\\mathcal P_n)=\\mathcal P_n .\n   \\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines\nand satisfies the covering requirement, establishing the case \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBoth constructions are explicit, satisfy the required covering condition, and\ncontain exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.  Therefore the values\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are admissible for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  The status of\nthe intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains unresolved. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generat