ch integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = -x + t .\n   \\]\n   Each \\(L_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is **not** sunny.  However,\n   the lines\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = mx + c_t,\\qquad m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n   \\]\n   with a fixed slope \\(m\\) (chosen once and for all, e.g. \\(m=2\\)) and\n   intercepts \\(c_t\\) chosen so that \\(L_t\\cap S_t\\) is a point of \\(U_n\\),\n   are sunny.  Because the slope \\(m\\) is the same for all \\(t\\), the lines\n   \\(S_t\\) are distinct (the intercepts differ) and none of them is parallel\n   to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n   Solving the system\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   y = -x + t,\\\\[2pt]\n   y = mx + c_t,\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   x = \\frac{t-c_t}{m+1},\\; y = \\frac{mt + c_t}{m+1},\n   \\]\n   we can choose \\(c_t\\) so that the intersection point \\((x,y)\\) is the\n   lattice point \\((t-1,1)\\) when \\(t\\le n\\).  Since \\((t-1,1)\\in T_n\\) and\n   already lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), we instead let the intersection\n   be the point \\((t-1, t-1)\\in U_n\\).  Setting\n   \\[\n   c_t = (1-m)(t-1),\n   \\]\n   we obtain\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\; y = mx + (1-m)(t-1),\n   \\]\n   and a direct substitution shows that \\((t-1,t-1)\\) satisfies both\n   equations, i.e. \\((t-1,t-1)\\in S_t\\cap L_t\\subseteq U_n\\).\n\n   Consequently each sunny line \\(S_t\\) covers at least the point\n   \\((t-1,t-1)\\) of \\(U_n\\).  Because the points \\((t-1,t-1)\\) for\n   \\(t=2,\\dots ,n\\) are distinct, the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines\n   \\(\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\) together cover all points of \\(U_n\\).\n\n5. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The total collection consists of:\n   * the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\);\n   * the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(S_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)).\n\n   Hence we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely\n   \\(k=n-2\\) are sunny.  By omitting any subset of the sunny lines and\n   compensating with additional non\u2011sunny lines of the form \\(x=c\\) or\n   \\(y=c\\) (which are always allowed), we can realise every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nThus we have proved that **all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are\nrealizable**.  The remaining cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are not settled by the\npresent argument.  \u220e\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed proof follows exactly the construction outlined above and\nverifies each step algebraically.*\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 \u2013 Two non\u2011sunny axes.**  \nConsider the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny (parallel respectively to the \\(y\\)-axis and the\n\\(x\\)-axis).  The set of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_1\\cup\\ell_2\\) is  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\nDefine the uncovered set  \n\n\\[\nU_n:=T_n\\setminus A_n\n      =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|U_n|=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(U_n\\) is the integer lattice inside the right\u2011angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((2,2),(n,2),(2,n)\\).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2 \u2013 A family of sunny lines covering \\(U_n\\).**  \nFix a real number  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nfor concreteness we may take \\(m=2\\).  For each integer \\(t\\) with\n\\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = mx + (1-m)(t-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope of \\(S_t\\) equals \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), each\n\\(S_t\\) is sunny.  The lines \\(S_t\\) are distinct: if \\(t\\neq t'\\) then the\nintercepts \\((1-m)(t-1)\\) and \\((1-m)(t'-1)\\) differ.\n\nConsider also the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = -x + t . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny.  Solving (1) and (2)\nsimultaneously we obtain the intersection point  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nmx + (1-m)(t-1) &= -x + t\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;(m+1)x &= t-(1-m)(t-1)\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;x &= t-1,\\\\\n\\Longrightarrow\\;y &= - (t-1) + t = t-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nS_t\\cap L_t = (t-1,\\;t-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2\\le t\\le n\\), the coordinates satisfy \\(t-1\\ge1\\) and\n\\((t-1)+(t-1)=2t-2\\le n+1\\); therefore \\((t-1,t-1)\\in T_n\\).  Moreover\n\\(t-1\\ge2\\) for \\(t\\ge3\\), so for all \\(t\\ge3\\) the point (3) lies in\n\\(U_n\\).  For \\(t=2\\) the point is \\((1,1)\\), which already belongs to\n\\(A_n\\); this does not affect the covering of \\(U_n\\).\n\nConsequently each sunny line \\(S_t\\) contains the lattice point\n\\((t-1,t-1)\\) of \\(U_n\\).  The points \\((t-1,t-1)\\) for distinct values of\n\\(t\\) are distinct, so the \\(n-2\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\{S_t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncover exactly the \\(n-2\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(2,2),(3,3),\\dots,(n-1,n-1)\\}\\subseteq U_n .\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points of \\(U_n\\) (those not on the main diagonal) are all\ncovered by the non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)), because each\n\\(L_t\\) consists of all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=t\\).  Since\n\\(t\\le n\\), every such point satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and lies in \\(U_n\\).\n\nThus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\cup\\{L_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le n\\}\\cup\\mathcal S\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Counting the lines we have  \n\n* two non\u2011sunny axes \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\);\n* \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(L_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\));\n* \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(S_t\\) (\\(3\\le t\\le n\\)).\n\nHence the total number of distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\n2+(n-1)+(n-2)=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nTo obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we simply discard any \\(n-2\\) of the\nnon\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(L_t\\); the remaining lines still cover all\npoints because each discarded diagonal line \\(L_t\\) is redundant: its\npoints are already covered either by the two axes or by a sunny line\n\\(S_{t+1}\\) (which shares the same diagonal).  After discarding we are left\nwith precisely \\(n\\) lines, of which the number of sunny lines is exactly\nthe number of retained \\(S_t\\)\u2019s.\n\nBy choosing to retain any subset of the sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S\\) we can\nrealise any integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if we keep \\(k\\) sunny lines, we keep \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n(the two axes together with enough diagonal lines to reach the total of\n\\(n\\) lines).  The covering argument above shows that the resulting set of\n\\(n\\) lines still covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the\nrequired covering property and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nThe cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) have not been settled by the present\nargument. \u220e"
