            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nConsider the \\(2n-1\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nP_2=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(1\\) belongs to \\(P_1\\); every\npoint with second coordinate \\(1\\) belongs to \\(P_2\\).  Any line that is\nsunny has slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\); consequently it can\ncontain **at most one** point of \\(P_1\\) (otherwise two points with the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical) and at most one point\nof \\(P_2\\).  Hence at least two distinct lines among the \\(n\\) required lines\nmust be non\u2011sunny (one vertical, one horizontal).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 2.2.1  Two non\u2011sunny axis lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny.  They cover all points of \\(P_1\\cup P_2\\).\n\n#### 2.2.2  Points still uncovered  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nU_n:=T_n\\setminus (P_1\\cup P_2)\n     =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(U_n\\) is the integer lattice inside the right\u2011angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((2,2),(n,2),(2,n)\\).\n\n#### 2.2.3  Sunny diagonal lines  \n\nChoose a fixed slope  \n\n\\[\nm\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\qquad(\\text{e.g. }m=2).\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = mx + (1-m)(t-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause its slope is \\(m\\), each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  Substituting \\((x,y)=(t-1,t-1)\\)\ninto (4) gives an identity, so  \n\n\\[\n(t-1,t-1)\\in S_t\\qquad (2\\le t\\le n). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\((t-1,t-1)\\) lie on the main diagonal of \\(U_n\\); they are all\ndistinct.\n\n#### 2.2.4  Non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(3\\le s\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\; y = -x + s . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(D_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  Equation (6) is equivalent\nto \\(x+y=s\\); consequently \\(D_s\\) contains exactly the lattice points of\n\\(U_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\).  Every point of \\(U_n\\) that is not on\nthe main diagonal belongs to a unique line \\(D_s\\) (its sum \\(a+b\\) determines\n\\(s\\)).\n\n#### 2.2.5  Assembling a configuration with a prescribed number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\) (if \\(k=0\\) we select\nnone).  Together with the two axis lines and all non\u2011sunny diagonal lines\n\\(D_3,\\dots ,D_n\\) we obtain the set\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n\\}\\cup\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nCount of lines:\n\n* two axis lines,\n* \\((n-2)\\) diagonal lines \\(D_s\\),\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_t\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L_k| = 2+(n-2)+k = n+k\\).\n\nIf \\(k=n-2\\) we already have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all distinct, and the\nconfiguration works because:\n\n* \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) cover \\(P_1\\cup P_2\\);\n* each \\(D_s\\) covers all points of \\(U_n\\) with sum \\(s\\) that are not on the\n  diagonal;\n* each \\(S_t\\) (for \\(t=2,\\dots ,n\\)) covers the diagonal point \\((t-1,t-1)\\).\n\nWhen \\(k<n-2\\) we have more than \\(n\\) lines.  To reduce the number to exactly\n\\(n\\) we delete any \\(n-2-k\\) of the non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(D_s\\).  Deleting\n\\(D_s\\) does **not** create uncovered points, because the points that lay on\nthe removed \\(D_s\\) are either:\n\n* on the diagonal (already covered by a sunny line \\(S_{s}\\)), or\n* on the axes (already covered by \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\)).\n\nThus after discarding \\(n-2-k\\) diagonal lines we obtain a set of exactly\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, and every point\nof \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\)\nsunny lines.  The construction above shows that for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  Therefore the set\nof admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
