1\\cup P_2\\). **Correct.**\n\n##### 3.2 Definition of the uncovered set \\(U_n\\)  \n\\(U_n = T_n\\setminus(P_1\\cup P_2)\\) is correctly described as the integer points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). **Correct.**\n\n##### 3.3 Sunny diagonal lines \\(S_t\\)  \nThe lines \\(S_t: y = mx + (1-m)(t-1)\\) (with a fixed \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) indeed pass through \\((t-1,t-1)\\). Each \\(S_t\\) is sunny because its slope is \\(m\\). **Correct.**\n\n##### 3.4 Non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(D_s\\))  \n\\(D_s: y = -x + s\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non\u2011sunny. It contains exactly the lattice points of \\(U_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\). This description is accurate. **Correct.**\n\n##### 3.5 Assembling the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)  \nThe set  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid 3\\le s\\le n\\}\\cup\\{S_t\\mid 2\\le t\\le k+1\\}\n\\]\ncontains \\(2+(n-2)+k = n+k\\) distinct lines. Up to this point the counting is fine.\n\n##### 3.6 Claim for the case \\(k=n-2\\)  \nWhen \\(k=n-2\\) the total number of lines is \\(n\\). The argument that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered is correct:\n- Points of \\(P_1\\cup P_2\\) are covered by \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\).\n- Points of \\(U_n\\) not on the main diagonal are covered by the appropriate \\(D_s\\).\n- Points on the main diagonal are covered by the sunny lines \\(S_t\\) (which, for \\(k=n-2\\), include all \\(t=2,\\dots,n\\)).  \n\nThus the construction works for \\(k=n-2\\). **Correct.**\n\n##### 3.7 Claim for the case \\(k<n-2\\) (the problematic part)  \nThe solution states:\n\n> \u201cWhen \\(k<n-2\\) we have more than \\(n\\) lines. To reduce the number to exactly \\(n\\) we delete any \\(n-2-k\\) of the non\u2011sunny diagonal lines \\(D_s\\). Deleting \\(D_s\\) does **not** create uncovered points, because the points that lay on the removed \\(D_s\\) are either:\n> * on the diagonal (already covered by a sunny line \\(S_{s}\\)), or\n> * on the axes (already covered by \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\)).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error:**  \n- A line \\(D_s\\) consists of **all** lattice points \\((a,b)\\) in \\(U_n\\) with \\(a+b=s\\). Only **one** of these points, namely \\((s-1,s-1)\\), lies on the main diagonal and is covered by the sunny line \\(S_{s}\\) (provided \\(S_{s}\\) is present).  \n- The remaining points on \\(D_s\\) have \\(a\\neq b\\) and are not on the axes. After removing \\(D_s\\) there is no other line in the constructed family that contains these points, because:\n  * The axis lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) have equations \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) and cannot pass through points with both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\).\n  * The sunny lines \\(S_t\\) each contain only the single diagonal point \\((t-1,t-1)\\).\n  * No other \\(D_{s'}\\) with \\(s'\\neq s\\) contains a point with sum \\(s\\).\n\nConsequently, deleting a diagonal line \\(D_s\\) leaves many points of \\(U_n\\) uncovered. The claim that \u201cdeleting \\(D_s\\) does not create uncovered points\u201d is false. Therefore the construction for \\(k<n-2\\) fails, and the existence of configurations with fewer than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines is not established.\n\nBecause this flaw undermines the core part of the existence proof for all admissible \\(k\\), the solution is invalid. No further steps need to be examined."
