"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that **every** integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\ncan occur as the number of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof consists of two parts.\n\n1. **Existence for any prescribed \\(k\\).**  \n   \u2013 *Non\u2011sunny lines* are those parallel to one of the three forbidden directions  \n     \\(\\ell_{x}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\;\\ell_{y}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\;\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \n   \u2013 For a given \\(k\\) we construct a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and we verify that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   The construction is elementary:\n   * Choose \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\); these are non\u2011sunny.\n   * The remaining \\(k\\) lines are taken to be distinct sunny lines of the form  \n     \\[\n     y = m_i x + c_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n     \\]\n     where the slopes \\(m_i\\) are chosen from \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the intercepts \\(c_i\\) are chosen so that each of the still uncovered points \\((a,b)\\) (those with \\(a>n-k\\)) lies on at least one of the sunny lines.  \n     Because the set of points to be covered after the vertical lines have been placed is a finite set, a simple greedy choice of the pairs \\((m_i,c_i)\\) works: each new sunny line is taken through a yet\u2011uncovered point and with a slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); this line automatically covers all points collinear with it, and the process stops after at most \\(k\\) steps because we are allowed to use exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The construction shows that for **any** \\(k\\) we can produce a suitable family, proving the *existence* part.\n\n2. **Necessity of the interval \\([0,n]\\).**  \n   \u2013 The number of lines is exactly \\(n\\); therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  \n   \u2013 No further restriction is imposed by the geometry: the three forbidden directions are mutually distinct, so a line can be either sunny or non\u2011sunny independently of the other lines. Consequently every integer between the obvious bounds \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is admissible.\n\nCombining the two parts yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf v_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf v_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf v_d=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nThus a line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is parallel to one of the three vectors above; we shall refer to these three families as *vertical*, *horizontal* and *diagonal* lines, respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by vertical lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) the set \\(T_n\\) is covered by the\n\\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t\n\\]\n\ntogether with the points whose abscissa exceeds \\(t\\); i.e. after removing the\nvertical lines the uncovered points are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>t\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(a\\le t\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A single sunny line can be chosen through any prescribed point)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\).  There exists a sunny line passing through \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any slope \\(m\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\) and consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{m}:\\;y=m(x-a)+b .\n\\]\n\nIts direction vector \\((1,m)\\) is not a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or\n\\((1,-1)\\) because \\(m\\neq0\\) and \\(m\\neq-1\\).  Hence \\(\\ell_{m}\\) is sunny and\ncontains \\((a,b)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Greedy covering of a finite set by sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(S\\) be a finite set of points in the plane.  For any integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) there exist\n\\(k\\) sunny lines whose union contains \\(S\\) **iff** \\(|S|\\le k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(|S|\\le k\\) we enumerate the points of \\(S\\) as \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_{|S|}\\).\nFor each \\(i\\) choose, by Lemma\u202f2, a sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) passing through\n\\(P_i\\).  The collection \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{|S|}\\}\\) (and any additional\n\\(k-|S|\\) arbitrary sunny lines) covers \\(S\\).\n\nConversely, a single sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(S\\) that is\nnot collinear with any other point of \\(S\\); therefore at least \\(|S|\\) sunny\nlines are necessary. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSet \\(t=n-k\\) and take the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{vert}}=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the points still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nU_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>t\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge t+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(t+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(U_{t}\\) has its second coordinate \\(b\\) belonging to the\nset \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|U_{t}|\\le k .\n\\]\n\nApply Lemma\u202f3 with the set \\(S=U_{t}\\) and the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines.  We obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) whose union\ncovers \\(U_{t}\\).  (If \\(U_{t}\\) is empty we may choose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny\nlines; they will automatically satisfy the covering condition.)\n\nFinally define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L = L_{\\text{vert}}\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nwhich are sunny, and by construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least\none line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Thus the required configuration exists for the\nchosen \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the interval \\([0,n]\\)\n\nThe definition of a sunny line is binary; a line is either sunny or not.\nSince we are asked for a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of\nsunny lines can never be larger than \\(n\\) nor negative.  No further\nrestriction follows from the geometric conditions, because the construction\nabove works for every admissible value of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines; and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Hence the set of\nall admissible \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
