\dots ,k\\}\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |U_{t}|\\le k .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering the remainder.**  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f3 with the set \\(S=U_{t}\\) and the integer \\(k\\).  \n   We obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) whose union\n   contains \\(U_{t}\\).  (If \\(U_{t}\\) is empty we may choose any \\(k\\) distinct\n   sunny lines; they will automatically satisfy the covering condition.)\n\n4. **Final family of lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = L_{\\text{vert}}\\cup\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, and by construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least\n   one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Necessity of the interval \\([0,n]\\)\n\nThe definition of a sunny line is binary; a line is either sunny or not.\nSince we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines can\nnever exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.  The construction above shows that every\ninteger between these trivial bounds can indeed be realized.  Hence no further\nrestriction exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  Therefore the set\nof admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification 