ion.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on the given family of lines.\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Extremal values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Minimum).  \nThere exists a configuration with \\(k=0\\); i.e. all \\(n\\) lines can be chosen non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll the lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Maximum).  \nThere exists a configuration with \\(k=n\\); i.e. all \\(n\\) lines can be chosen sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha=2\\)).\nConsider the \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\;y=\\alpha (x-i)+ (n+1-i)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the line \\(M_i\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n+1-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,\\,n-i),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(S\\) (the second point is present for \\(i<n\\)).  \nConsequently every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(M_i\\):\nif \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and we may take \\(i=a\\); the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_a\\) because the defining equation of \\(M_a\\) is satisfied by \\((a,b)\\).  \nAll the lines have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2 we obtain the two extremal possibilities\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad(0\\le s\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe shall build a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n\\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines (hence \\(k\\) sunny lines) and covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 1 \u2013 the non\u2011sunny part  \n\nTake the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,s).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(S\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 2 \u2013 the sunny part  \n\nChoose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (again \\(\\alpha=2\\) works).\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(s+1)\\bigr)+(n-s).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(s+1\\le i\\le n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nL\\ni\\bigl(i,\\;n+1-i\\bigr)\\in S .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the single line \\(L\\) already contains all points of \\(S\\) whose abscissa is\ngreater than \\(s\\).\n\nTo obtain the required total of \\(n\\) distinct lines we add \\(k-1=n-s-1\\) further\nsunny lines, for instance the parallel translates  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(s+1)\\bigr)+(n-s)+t\\qquad(t=1,2,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have the same slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 3 \u2013 verification  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) falls into one of the two categories:\n\n* If \\(a\\le s\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a> s\\) then, by (2), \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L\\).\n