e 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the chosen lines.\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Extremal values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1 (Minimum possible \\(k\\)).  \nThere exists a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nAll the lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (Maximum possible \\(k\\)).  \nThere exists a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha=2\\)).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)+(n+1-i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in S\\) satisfies (1), so every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nAll the lines have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2 we obtain the two extremal possibilities  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad(0\\le s\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe shall build a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n\\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines (hence \\(k\\) sunny lines) and covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 1 \u2013 the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(s\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,s).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(S\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 2 \u2013 the sunny lines  \n\nThe points of \\(S\\) whose abscissa is larger than \\(s\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nP_i=\\bigl(s+i,\\;n+1-(s+i)\\bigr)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-s).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-s\\) such points.  \nFor each \\(i\\) choose a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(For instance \\(\\alpha_i=i+2\\) works.)  \nDefine \\(L_i\\) to be the line of slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) passing through \\(P_i\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-\\bigl(n+1-(s+i)\\bigr)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-(s+i)\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is sunny by (3), the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-s}\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes, and by construction each \\(L_i\\) contains the point \\(P_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Step 3 \u2013 verification  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le s\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a> s\\) then \\(a=s+i\\) for a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-s\\}\\); by (2) the point \\((a,b)=(s+i,n+1-(s+i))\\) is exactly \\(P_i\\), which lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of the \\(s\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines and the \\(n-s\\) lines \\(L_i\\), all of which are sunny.  Hence the number of sunny lines equals \\(k=n-s\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nThe vertical lines are distinct by construction.  \nThe sunny lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) (condition (3)).  \nNo vertical line coincides with a sunny line because a vertical line has infinite slope, whereas each \\(\\alpha_i\\) is finite.\n\nTherefore \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfies all requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited:\n\n* a configuration with \\(k=0\\) (Lemma\u202f2.1);\n* a configuration with \\(k=n\\) (Lemma\u202f2.2);\n* for every intermediate integer \\(k\\) (\\(0<k<n\\)) a concrete construction producing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
