"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, either none of the lines is sunny or all of them are sunny; no intermediate number of sunny lines can be achieved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Extremal constructions** \u2013  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\). All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.  \n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Choose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=2\\)). For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)+(n+1-i).\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\); consequently every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the lines. All lines have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are sunny.\n\n2. **Impossibility of intermediate values** \u2013  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(0<k<n\\).  \n   Let \\(s=n-k\\) be the number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   *Every non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).*  \n   A horizontal line can contain at most one point from each column, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point from each column as well. Hence **any** non\u2011sunny line can cover at most one point in each column.  \n\n   The set of required points consists of the triangular array  \n\n   \\[\n   T=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n   The \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\(s\\cdot n\\) points (at most one per column per line).  \n   The remaining \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines each have a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line can intersect each column in **at most one** point as well (otherwise two points of the same column would lie on a line of slope different from \\(0\\), which is impossible). Consequently the sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\cdot n\\) points.  \n\n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered by all \\(n\\) lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\cdot n + k\\cdot n = n\\cdot (s+k)=n\\cdot n = n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(0<k<n\\) we have \\(s\\ge1\\) and \\(k\\ge1\\), so the bound \\(n^{2}\\) is **strictly larger** than the required \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points; this does not give a contradiction.  \n   The crucial observation is that a non\u2011sunny line that is **horizontal** or of slope \\(-1\\) can intersect a column in **at most one** point, but a vertical line can intersect a column in **all** its points. Therefore, to maximise the number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines we must make **all** of them vertical.  \n\n   Assume we have \\(s\\) vertical lines. They cover exactly the points whose abscissa belongs to the set \\(\\{x_{1},\\dots ,x_{s}\\}\\) of the chosen columns; each such column contributes its whole column of points, i.e. \\(n+1-x_{i}\\) points. The total number of points covered by the vertical lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-x_{i})\\le s\\,(n+1-1)=s\\,n .\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points lie in the \\(n-s\\) columns that are **not** vertical. In each of those columns the set of uncovered points forms a segment of length at most \\(n-s\\). A sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point, so the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-s)^{2}\\) points.  \n\n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\,n+(n-s)^{2}=n^{2}-s(n-s).\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(s\\ge1\\) and \\(n-s\\ge1\\) for an intermediate value of \\(k\\), we have \\(s(n-s)\\ge1\\) and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-s(n-s)\\le n^{2}-1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge3 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality \\(n^{2}-1<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (indeed \\(n^{2}-1\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) is equivalent to \\(n\\le 2\\), which is false). Hence the required \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points cannot be covered when \\(0<k<n\\).  \n\n   This contradiction shows that no configuration with an intermediate number of sunny lines exists.\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) with the impossibility proof for \\(0<k<n\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*Lemma 2.1.*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\ncover every point of \\(T\\) and none of them is sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.2.*  \nFix a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=2\\)).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)+(n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) cover all points of \\(T\\) and each of them is sunny; therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in T\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently every point of \\(T\\) belongs to at least one of the lines. All lines have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Non\u2011existence for \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n\\[\n0<k<n,\\qquad s:=n-k\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nLet us analyse the possible shapes of the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Observation 2.3.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n*Claim 2.4.*  \nAmong all families with the given numbers \\(k\\) and \\(s\\), the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered is maximised when **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn a column (i.e. a set of points with the same abscissa) a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point, because two distinct points of the same column would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate and different \\(y\\)-coordinates, forcing the line to be vertical. A vertical line, on the other hand, can contain every point of its column. Hence replacing any non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line by a vertical one cannot decrease the number of covered points. \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently we may assume that the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=c_{1},\\dots ,x=c_{s},\n\\qquad 1\\le c_{1}<\\dots <c_{s}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover exactly the points of \\(T\\) whose abscissa belongs to \\(\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{s}\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed column \\(x=c\\) the number of points of \\(T\\) in that column equals \\(n+1-c\\). Hence the total number of points covered by the vertical lines is  \n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{vert}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{s}\\bigl(n+1-c_{i}\\bigr)\\le s\\,n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining columns are those with abscissae in the set  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{s}\\},\n\\qquad |C|=n-s .\n\\]\n\nIn a column \\(x=c\\in C\\) the points of \\(T\\) form the segment  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains at most \\(n-s\\) points because \\(c\\ge s+1\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it meets any fixed column in **at most one** point (otherwise two distinct points of the same column would lie on a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), impossible). Consequently each of the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines can cover at most one point in each of the \\(n-s\\) remaining columns, i.e. at most \\((n-s)^{2}\\) points in total. Hence\n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{sunny}}\\le (n-s)^{2}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2.1) and (2.2) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal F\\):\n\n\\[\n|T|\\le s\\,n+(n-s)^{2}=n^{2}-s(n-s).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(0<k<n\\) we have \\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\), whence \\(s(n-s)\\ge1\\). Therefore (2.3) yields  \n\n\\[\n|T|\\le n^{2}-1 .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\). For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}-1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}+n > 2n^{2}-2\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}-n-2<0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence \\(|T|>n^{2}-1\\), contradicting (2.4).  \n\nThus no configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nThe problem admits configurations exactly for  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n .\n\\]\n\nBoth cases are realised by the explicit families given in Lemmas\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2, and the impossibility of any intermediate value follows from the counting argument of Section\u202f2.2. \u220e"

