Non\u2011existence for \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n\\[\n0<k<n,\\qquad s:=n-k\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nLet us analyse the possible shapes of the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Observation 2.3.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n*Claim 2.4.*  \nAmong all families with the given numbers \\(k\\) and \\(s\\), the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered is maximised when **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIn a column (i.e. a set of points with the same abscissa) a horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point, because two distinct points of the same column would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate and different \\(y\\)-coordinates, forcing the line to be vertical. A vertical line, on the other hand, can contain every point of its column. Hence replacing any non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line by a vertical one cannot decrease the number of covered points. \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently we may assume that the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=c_{1},\\dots ,x=c_{s},\n\\qquad 1\\le c_{1}<\\dots <c_{s}\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover exactly the points of \\(T\\) whose abscissa belongs to \\(\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{s}\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed column \\(x=c\\) the number of points of \\(T\\) in that column equals \\(n+1-c\\). Hence the total number of points covered by the vertical lines is  \n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{vert}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{s}\\bigl(n+1-c_{i}\\bigr)\\le s\\,n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining columns are those with abscissae in the set  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{s}\\},\n\\qquad |C|=n-s .\n\\]\n\nIn a column \\(x=c\\in C\\) the points of \\(T\\) form the segment  \n\n\\[\n\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains at most \\(n-s\\) points because \\(c\\ge s+1\\).\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it meets any fixed column in **at most one** point (otherwise two distinct points of the same column would lie on a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), impossible). Consequently each of the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines can cover at most one point in each of the \\(n-s\\) remaining columns, i.e. at most \\((n-s)^{2}\\) points in total. Hence\n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{sunny}}\\le (n-s)^{2}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2.1) and (2.2) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(T\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal F\\):\n\n\\[\n|T|\\le s\\,n+(n-s)^{2}=n^{2}-s(n-s).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(0<k<n\\) we have \\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\), whence \\(s(n-s)\\ge1\\). Therefore (2.3) yields  \n\n\\[\n|T|\\le n^{2}-1 .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\). For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}-1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}+n > 2n^{2}-2\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}-n-2<0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is false for \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence \\(|T|>n^{2}-1\\), contradicting (2.4).  \n\nThus no configuration with \\(0<k<n\\) can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Conclusion\n\nThe problem admits configurations exactly for  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n .\n\\]\n\nBoth cases are realised by the explicit families given in Lemmas\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2, and the impossibility of any intermediate value follows from the counting argument of Section\u202f2.2. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
