# 2.  Non\u2011existence for \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where  \n\n\\[\n0<k<n,\\qquad s:=n-k\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Shape of the non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).  \nIf a non\u2011sunny line is not vertical, replace it by a vertical line passing through the same column of points of \\(T\\). This replacement cannot decrease the number of points of \\(T\\) that are covered, because a vertical line contains **all** points of its column whereas a horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line contains at most one point of that column. Repeating this for every non\u2011vertical non\u2011sunny line we obtain a new family (still of size \\(n\\) and with the same numbers \\(k\\) and \\(s\\)) in which **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical. Hence we may assume\n\n\\[\n\\text{the }s\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines are }x=c_{1},\\dots ,x=c_{s},\n\\qquad 1\\le c_{1}<\\dots <c_{s}\\le n .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2  Points covered by the vertical lines  \n\nIn column \\(x=c\\) the set of required points is  \n\n\\[\nC_{c}=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\},\n\\qquad |C_{c}|=n+1-c .\n\\]\n\nThus the vertical lines cover exactly  \n\n\\[\nP_{\\text{vert}}=\\sum_{i=1}^{s}(n+1-c_{i})\\le s\\,n .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality follows because each \\(c_{i}\\ge1\\).)\n\n#### 2.3  Points that must be covered by sunny lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{s}\\},\n\\qquad |R|=n-s .\n\\]\n\nFor a column index \\(r\\in R\\) we have \\(r\\ge s+1\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|C_{r}|=n+1-r\\le n-s .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it meets any fixed column in **at most one** point (otherwise two points of the same column would lie on a line whose slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\