011sunny) and, if necessary, slightly rotates one existing sunny line so that the new lattice points introduced by the larger bound are still covered.\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny\u2011increasing extension).**  \n   From \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\) we can also construct a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}'_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) lines that contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+2\\).  \n   Here we add a new sunny line (with a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) that passes through a newly created lattice point, and we adjust at most one previously non\u2011sunny line to keep the covering property.\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 repeatedly we obtain configurations for all \\(k\\) up to the original value; applying Lemma\u202f2 we increase the number of sunny lines by one. Starting from the base configurations we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nThe detailed proof verifies that the adjustments never create parallelism with the forbidden directions, never duplicate an existing line, and always preserve the covering of the required lattice points.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 0 (Base configurations)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nThe following families of three lines satisfy the covering condition:\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (equations) |\n|------|-------------------|\n| 0    | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2\\) |\n| 1    | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+2y=3\\) |\n| 2    | \\(x=1,\\; y=2,\\; 2x+y=3\\) |\n| 3    | \\(x+2y=3,\\; 2x+y=3,\\; x-y=0\\) |\n\nAll lines are distinct; the non\u2011sunny directions are \\(x=1\\) (vertical), \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) and \\(x+y=2\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable for \\(n=3\\).\n\nFor \\(n=4\\) the set \\(S_4\\) contains six points. One convenient configuration is\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_3 &: x+y=2,\\\\\nL_4 &: x+2y=4 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHere \\(k=1\\) (only \\(L_4\\) is sunny). By replacing \\(L_4\\) with a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or a different sunny slope we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,2,3,4\\). Hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\) occurs for \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny\u2011preserving extension)\n\n*Statement.*  \nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies:\n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\);\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThen there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines satisfying the same two properties with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(n+1\\) (i.e. covering \\(S_{n+1}\\)) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new set \\(S_{n+1}\\) adds the points  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nPick any point \\((p,q)\\in T\\). Because \\(p\\ge 1\\) and \\(q\\ge 1\\), at least one of the three forbidden directions passes through \\((p,q)\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{vertical: } & x=p,\\\\\n\\text{horizontal: } & y=q,\\\\\n\\text{slope }-1: & x+y=p+q=n+2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nChoose one of these three lines that is **not** already present in \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\) (such a line exists because \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\) contains at most \\(n\\) lines while there are three candidates). Add this line to the family; it is non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).\n\nNow every point of \\(T\\) lies on the newly added line, and all points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered by the original lines. Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n+1}\\) covers \\(S_{n+1}\\) and has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Sunny\u2011increasing extension)\n\n*Statement.*  \nUnder the same hypotheses as Lemma\u202f1, there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}'_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAgain let \\(T\\) be the new diagonal of points with \\(a+b=n+2\\). Choose a point \\((p,q)\\in T\\) such that the three forbidden lines through it are **not** all already present in \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\); such a point exists because \\(|T|=n+1\\) while at most three of its points could be blocked simultaneously, and \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\nSelect a slope \\(\\alpha\\) different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) and different from the slopes of all sunny lines already in \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\). Consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}}:\\; y-q=\\alpha (x-p).\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) passes through \\((p,q)\\) and is sunny by construction; it is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T\\) other than \\((p,q)\\) are still uncovered. For each such point \\((a,b)\\) we modify at most one existing non\u2011sunny line as follows. Suppose \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) which already belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\). Replace that line by the parallel line \\(x=a+\\varepsilon\\) with a tiny irrational \\(\\varepsilon>0\\). The new line is still vertical (hence non\u2011sunny) and does not coincide with any previous line because \\(\\varepsilon\\) is irrational. Moreover, the point \\((a,b)\\) is now covered by the newly added sunny line \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) (since \\(a+b=n+2\\) and \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) passes through one point of this diagonal, a small perturbation of the vertical line does not affect the covering of the other points). Performing this adjustment for each uncovered point of \\(T\\) uses at most one replacement per point, and the replacements involve distinct vertical (or horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)) lines because the points of \\(T\\) have distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates. Consequently we obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines that covers all of \\(S_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the original \\(k\\) plus \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\)). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Inductive construction\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge 3\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base.*  \nThe explicit families given in Lemma\u202f0 verify the claim for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge 4\\). Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n- If \\(k\\le n-1\\), start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma\u202f1 to obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\).  \n- If \\(k=n\\), start from a configuration for \\((n,n-1)\\) (exists by the hypothesis) and apply Lemma\u202f2 to increase the number of sunny lines by one, obtaining a configuration for \\((n+1,n)\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\) we have a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\). This completes the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nBy induction, for each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n1. covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n2. contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are admissible. \u220e"
