"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that  \n\n1. covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\);  \n2. contains precisely \\(k\\) *sunny* lines (i.e. lines whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThe proof is a complete induction on \\(n\\). The inductive step is carried out by two lemmas:\n\n* **Lemma\u202f1 (Sunny\u2011preserving extension).** From a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k)\\) by adding a non\u2011sunny line that covers all newly required points.\n* **Lemma\u202f2 (Sunny\u2011increasing extension).** From a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) we obtain a configuration for \\((n+1,k+1)\\) by first adding a sunny line through one new point and then, using Lemma\u202f1, adding a non\u2011sunny line that covers the remaining new points.\n\nBase configurations for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) realise every \\(k\\) in the required range; the lemmas then allow us to reach any larger \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Base step.**  \n   Explicit families of \\(3\\) and \\(4\\) lines are exhibited that cover \\(S_3\\) and \\(S_4\\) and realise every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) (resp. \\(0\\le k\\le4\\)).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f1 (preserve \\(k\\)).**  \n   The new points when passing from \\(S_n\\) to \\(S_{n+1}\\) are the diagonal  \n   \\(T_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}\\).  \n   Choose any point \\((p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\\). One of the three forbidden directions\n   \\(x=p,\\;y=q,\\;x+y=n+2\\) is not already present among the \\(n\\) lines (there are at most three such lines). Adding that missing forbidden line (which is non\u2011sunny) covers the whole set \\(T_{n+1}\\) and does not change the number of sunny lines.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f2 (increase \\(k\\) by one).**  \n   Start from a configuration for \\((n,k)\\).  \n   *Step\u202fa.* Pick a point \\((p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\\) whose three forbidden lines are **not** all already present (such a point exists because \\(|T_{n+1}|=n+1\\) and at most three points can be blocked simultaneously). Add a sunny line \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) of slope \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) passing through \\((p,q)\\); choose \\(\\alpha\\) also different from the slopes of the already sunny lines, so the new line is distinct and sunny.  \n   *Step\u202fb.* After this addition, the only points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that may remain uncovered are the other \\(n\\) points of the diagonal. Apply Lemma\u202f1 to the *current* family (which now has \\(n+1\\) lines) to add one more non\u2011sunny line that covers **all** points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) not already covered. This extra line is non\u2011sunny, so the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k+1\\).  \n   The resulting family has \\(n+2\\) lines; we simply discard one of the original non\u2011sunny lines that is now redundant (its removal does not affect the covering because every point it covered is also covered by either the new non\u2011sunny line or by an existing line). Thus we obtain a configuration of exactly \\(n+1\\) lines with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n5. **Induction.**  \n   Using the base step and repeatedly applying Lemma\u202f1 we can keep the same \\(k\\) while increasing \\(n\\). Using Lemma\u202f2 we can increase \\(k\\) by one while also increasing \\(n\\). Starting from the base families we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Hence the theorem holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Definitions and notation  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the three *forbidden directions* are the horizontal line, the vertical line and the line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of lines we write  \n\n* \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\) = number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L)=S_n\\) means that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nOur aim is to determine, for each \\(n\\ge3\\), the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\exists\\;\\mathcal L\\text{ with }|\\mathcal L|=n,\\;\n\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L)=S_n,\\;\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=k\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Base configurations  \n\n#### \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe following four families each consist of three distinct lines and realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{Lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2\\\\\n1 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+2y=3\\\\\n2 & x=1,\\; y=2,\\; 2x+y=3\\\\\n3 & x+2y=3,\\; 2x+y=3,\\; x-y=0\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe first three lines in each row are clearly distinct; the slopes are respectively\n\\( \\infty,0,-1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the added line has slope \\( -\\tfrac12, -2\\) or \\(1\\), which are sunny.\n\nHence \\(K_3=\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n#### \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(S_4\\) consists of the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nConsider  \n\n\\[\nL_1:x=1,\\qquad L_2:y=1,\\qquad L_3:x+y=2,\\qquad L_4:x+2y=4 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are the three forbidden directions; \\(L_4\\) has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and is sunny.  \nThus the configuration has \\(k=1\\).  \n\nBy replacing \\(L_4\\) with a line of