 and at most three points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) can have **all** three forbidden lines already present (otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines), we can choose a point \\((p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\\) for which at least one of the three forbidden lines is missing from \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n1. **Add a sunny line.**  \n   Choose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   \\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal L_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because there are only finitely many forbidden slopes.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y-q=\\alpha (x-p) .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) passes through \\((p,q)\\), is sunny, and is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L' =\\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{L_{\\text{sun}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Now \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n+1\\) and \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=k+1\\).  \n   The only points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that may still be uncovered are the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus\\{(p,q)\\}\\).\n\n2. **Cover the remaining points without adding sunny lines.**  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f1 to the configuration \\(\\mathcal L'\\). Lemma\u202f1 guarantees the existence of a non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{add}}\\) that together with \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers all of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L'' = \\mathcal L'\\cup\\{L_{\\text{add}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Now \\(|\\mathcal L''|=n+2\\) and \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L'')=k+1\\) (the added line is non\u2011sunny).\n\n3. **Delete a redundant non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   Among the original non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) at most one can become redundant, because the only new covering requirement is the diagonal \\(T_{n+1}\\), which is already covered by \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) (at \\((p,q)\\)) and by \\(L_{\\text{add}}\\) (at all other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\)). Choose any original non\u2011sunny line that does **not** contain a point of \\(S_n\\) that is covered uniquely by it (such a line exists because the set of points uniquely covered by a given line is finite while we have at least two new covering lines). Remove that line; denote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L'_{n+1}\\).\n\n   The removal does not destroy the covering of any point of \\(S_{n+1}\\), and the number of lines drops back to \\(n+1\\). The sunny count remains \\(k+1\\).\n\nThus we have produced a configuration of exactly \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Base.*  \nThe constructions in \u00a72.2 show that the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge4\\); i.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\le n\\), start from \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) and apply Lemma\u202f1. We obtain a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(k=n+1\\), start from \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(n)}\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma\u202f2. This yields a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists for \\(n+1\\). Hence the induction proceeds, and the formula for \\(K_n\\) holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nTherefore **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(k\\le n\\) satisfy the conditions of the problem, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible because a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. \u220e"
