            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Definitions and notation  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n\\[\n0,\\qquad \\infty\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad -1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the three *forbidden directions* are the horizontal line, the vertical line and the line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of lines we write  \n\n* \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)\\) = number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* \\(\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L)=S_n\\) means that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nOur aim is to determine, for each \\(n\\ge3\\), the set  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\exists\\;\\mathcal L\\text{ with }|\\mathcal L|=n,\\;\n\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L)=S_n,\\;\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L)=k\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Base configurations  \n\n#### \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nThe following four families each consist of three distinct lines and realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{Lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=2\\\\\n1 & x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+2y=3\\\\\n2 & x=1,\\; y=2,\\; 2x+y=3\\\\\n3 & x+2y=3,\\; 2x+y=3,\\; x-y=0\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe first three lines in each row are clearly distinct; the slopes are respectively\n\\( \\infty,0,-1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the added line has slope \\( -\\tfrac12, -2\\) or \\(1\\), which are sunny.\n\nHence \\(K_3=\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n#### \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(S_4\\) consists of the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nConsider  \n\n\\[\nL_1:x=1,\\qquad L_2:y=1,\\qquad L_3:x+y=2,\\qquad L_4:x+2y=4 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) are the three forbidden directions; \\(L_4\\) has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and is sunny.  \nThus the configuration has \\(k=1\\).  \n\nBy replacing \\(L_4\\) with a line of slope \\(0\\) (e.g. \\(y=2\\)), with a line of slope \\(-1\\) (e.g. \\(x+y=3\\)), or with a sunny line of a different slope (e.g. \\(2x+y=5\\)), we obtain configurations with \\(k=0,2,3,4\\) respectively. Consequently \\(K_4=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lemma\u202f1 (extension without changing the number of sunny lines)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) be a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* \\(\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L_n)=S_n\\);\n* \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_n)=k\\).\n\nThen there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines with  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L_{n+1})=S_{n+1},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L_{n+1})=k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe new points are  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=S_{n+1}\\setminus S_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b=n+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nPick any \\((p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\\). The three forbidden lines through \\((p,q)\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx=p,\\qquad y=q,\\qquad x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nAt most three of them can already belong to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\); because \\(|\\mathcal L_n|=n\\ge3\\) there is always at least one of the three that is **not** in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\). Add that missing forbidden line to the family; denote the enlarged family by \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\).\n\nThe added line is non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) lies on the added line, and all points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered by the original lines. Hence \\(\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L_{n+1})=S_{n+1}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Lemma\u202f2 (extension increasing the number of sunny lines by one)\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nUnder the hypotheses of Lemma\u202f1, there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L'_{n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{cov}(\\mathcal L'_{n+1})=S_{n+1},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L'_{n+1})=k+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAgain let \\(T_{n+1}\\) be the new diagonal. Because \\(|T_{n+1}|=n+1\\) and at most three points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) can have **all** three forbidden lines already present (otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines), we can choose a point \\((p,q)\\in T_{n+1}\\) for which at least one of the three forbidden lines is missing from \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n1. **Add a sunny line.**  \n   Choose a slope \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   \\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line already in }\\mathcal L_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Such an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because there are only finitely many forbidden slopes.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y-q=\\alpha (x-p) .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) passes through \\((p,q)\\), is sunny, and is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L' =\\mathcal L_n\\cup\\{L_{\\text{sun}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Now \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n+1\\) and \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L')=k+1\\).  \n   The only points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that may still be uncovered are the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus\\{(p,q)\\}\\).\n\n2. **Cover the remaining points without adding sunny lines.**  \n   Apply Lemma\u202f1 to the configuration \\(\\mathcal L'\\). Lemma\u202f1 guarantees the existence of a non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{\\text{add}}\\) that together with \\(\\mathcal L'\\) covers all of \\(S_{n+1}\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L'' = \\mathcal L'\\cup\\{L_{\\text{add}}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Now \\(|\\mathcal L''|=n+2\\) and \\(\\operatorname{sun}(\\mathcal L'')=k+1\\) (the added line is non\u2011sunny).\n\n3. **Delete a redundant non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   Among the original non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) at most one can become redundant, because the only new covering requirement is the diagonal \\(T_{n+1}\\), which is already covered by \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) (at \\((p,q)\\)) and by \\(L_{\\text{add}}\\) (at all other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\)). Choose any original non\u2011sunny line that does **not** contain a point of \\(S_n\\) that is covered uniquely by it (such a line exists because the set of points uniquely covered by a given line is finite while we have at least two new covering lines). Remove that line; denote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L'_{n+1}\\).\n\n   The removal does not destroy the covering of any point of \\(S_{n+1}\\), and the number of lines drops back to \\(n+1\\). The sunny count remains \\(k+1\\).\n\nThus we have produced a configuration of exactly \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Induction on \\(n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that  \n\n\\[\nK_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Base.*  \nThe constructions in \u00a72.2 show that the statement holds for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge4\\); i.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(k\\le n\\), start from \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(k)}\\) and apply Lemma\u202f1. We obtain a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* If \\(k=n+1\\), start from \\(\\mathcal L_n^{(n)}\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis) and apply Lemma\u202f2. This yields a configuration of \\(n+1\\) lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) with \\(n+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists for \\(n+1\\). Hence the induction proceeds, and the formula for \\(K_n\\) holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nTherefore **all** non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(k\\le n\\) satisfy the conditions of the problem, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible because a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
