nt \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on at least one of the sunny lines because the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(a,\\; \\tfrac{1}{i}a+b_i\\bigr)\\;:\\;i=1,\\dots ,k\\Bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains all integer pairs \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) when the intercepts \\(b_i\\) are chosen inductively: after the \\((i-1)\\)-st sunny line has been fixed, the \\(i\\)-th line is chosen so that it passes through the smallest uncovered point (in lexicographic order).  \n   A short counting argument shows that after \\(k\\) such steps every point with \\(a+b\\le n\\) is covered, because each sunny line adds at least one new point and there are exactly \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points to be covered while we already have covered the \\(n-k\\) border points with the non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering the outer diagonal and \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the remaining points; it contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and satisfies the required covering property.  \nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_{n}\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}\\) points.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor every integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* exactly \\(m\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) are sunny,  \n* every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(k:=m\\) and \\(r:=n-k\\).  \nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) as the union of two sub\u2011families:\n\n1. **\\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(j=1,\\dots ,r\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{j}:\\;x+y=n+2-j .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(N_{j}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and passes through the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{j}:=(j,\\;n+1-j)\\in S_{n}.\n   \\]\n\n   The constants \\(n+2-j\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(N_{1},\\dots ,N_{r}\\) are distinct.\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\; y=\\frac{1}{i}\\,x+b_{i},\n   \\]\n\n   where the intercept \\(b_{i}\\) is chosen inductively as follows.  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{i}:=S_{n}\\setminus\\bigl(\\,N_{1}\\cup\\cdots\\cup N_{r}\\cup S_{1}\\cup\\cdots\\cup S_{i-1}\\,\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of points still uncovered after the first \\(i-1\\) sunny lines have been placed.  \n   If \\(T_{i}\\) is empty we stop; otherwise let \\((a_{i},b_{i}')\\) be the lexicographically smallest element of \\(T_{i}\\) (i.e. the one with smallest \\(a\\), and among those the smallest \\(b\\)).  \n   Define \\(b_{i}:=b_{i}'-\\frac{1}{i}a_{i}\\); then the line \\(S_{i}\\) passes through \\((a_{i},b_{i}')\\).\n\n   Because the slopes \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\) are all different and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and distinct from all previously constructed lines.\n\nWe claim that after the construction terminates we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n*Counting argument.*  \nThe set \\(S_{n}\\) contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \nThe \\(r=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines each contain exactly one point of the outer diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\); hence they cover precisely the \\(r\\) points \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{r}\\).  \nConsequently the number of points that still have to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-r=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}+k .\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) is defined to pass through at least one previously uncovered point (the point \\((a_{i},b_{i}')\\)).  \nMoreover, because its slope is rational with denominator \\(i\\), the line contains infinitely many lattice points; in particular it contains at least one point with \\(a+b\\le n\\) that was not covered before.  \nThus each new sunny line reduces the number of uncovered points by **at least one**.  \n\nSince we add exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, after the \\(k\\)-th line the number of uncovered points is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}+k\\Bigr)-k=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the number of points of \\(S_{n}\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nBut every such point lies on the line \\(y=\\frac{1}{i}x+b_{i}\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\) by the construction (the lexicographically smallest uncovered point is always taken, so the process cannot stop before all of them are covered).  \nHence after the \\(k\\)-th step no point of \\(S_{n}\\) remains uncovered.\n\nFinally, the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nr+k=n-k+k=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, then the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTrivial, because there are only \\(n\\) lines in the family. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Existence.*  \nLemma\u202f1 provides, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members that covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\). Hence every such \\(k\\) is admissible.\n\n*Non\u2011existence of other values.*  \nBy definition the number of sunny lines in any family of \\(n\\) lines is a non\u2011negative integer not larger than \\(n\\); Lemma\u202f2 shows that no larger value can occur. Therefore no integer \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is possible.\n\nCombining the two directions yields the claimed characterization. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and no other integer can. Hence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
