ts ,n+1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) in (5) define a line  \n\n\\[\ns_{i}:\\; y=\\frac{1}{i-(n+1-k)}\\,x+b_{i},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere the constant \\(b_{i}\\) is chosen so that \\(s_{i}\\) passes through \\(P_{i}\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nb_{i}= (n+1-i)-\\frac{1}{i-(n+1-k)}\\,i .\n\\]\n\nThe slopes occurring in (6) are  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1},\\frac{1}{2},\\dots ,\\frac{1}{k},\n\\]\n\nhence none of them equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(s_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_{i}\\) are pairwise different.\n\nNow put  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}:=\\mathcal N_{k}\\cup\\{s_{i}\\mid i=n+2-k,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N_{k}|+k=(n-k)+k=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Covering property.*  \n\n* If \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\in\\mathcal N_{k}\\).\n* If \\(a+b\\in\\{n+2-k,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), then \\((a,b)=P_{i}\\) for the unique \\(i\\) given by (5), and by construction \\(P_{i}\\in s_{i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThere are only \\(n\\) lines in the family; a line is either sunny or not, so the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Existence.*  \nLemma\u202f1 provides, for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dot