es of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non\u2011sunny; let their multiset of directions be  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N\\subseteq \\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D ,\\qquad |\\mathcal N|=n-3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each line \\(M\\in\\mathcal N\\) we bound the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that it can contain using Lemma\u202f2.  \nIf \\(M\\) is vertical or horizontal with constant \\(c\\), then \\(|M\\cap S_n|=n+1-c\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(M\\) is diagonal with constant \\(c\\), then \\(|M\\cap S_n|=\\min\\{c-1,n+1-c\\}\\le \\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\rfloor\\).\n\nHence, regardless of the precise distribution of the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines among the three families, the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by all non\u2011sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)\\cdot n . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contributions of the sunny lines (8) and the non\u2011sunny lines (9) we obtain an upper bound for the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n\\[\n|S_n\\cap\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L|\n\\le 3n + (n-3)n = n^2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBut by (2) we have  \n\n\\[\n|S_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} > n^2 \\quad\\text{for every } n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n(The inequality follows from \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - n^2 = \\frac{n}{2}>0\\).)\n\nThus (10) contradicts the covering requirement (5).  \nThe contradiction stems from the assumption that at least three sunny lines exist.  \nTherefore **no** configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (5) can contain three or more sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le 2 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with Lemma\u202f3, which shows that every integer \\(k\\) in this range is indeed attainable, we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2\