\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line belongs to exactly one of the families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V:=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H:=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D:=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(L\\) contained \\(n+1\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\).  \nPick any two of those points, say \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\).  \nBecause the points lie on the same line, the slope of \\(L\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth numerator and denominator are integers whose absolute values do not exceed \\(n\\) (since \\(1\\le a_i,b_i\\le n\\)).  \nIf the denominator were \\(0\\) the line would be vertical, parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\); if the numerator were \\(0\\) it would be horizontal, parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\); if the numerator equals \\(-\\)denominator the slope would be \\(-1\\), i.e. the line would be parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).  \nAll three possibilities contradict the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nHence the denominator and numerator are non\u2011zero and not negatives of each other.  \nA line with such a slope can intersect the triangular lattice \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (a standard lattice\u2011point argument: moving one step in the \\(x\\)-direction changes the \\(y\\)-coordinate by a non\u2011integral amount, so after at most \\(n\\) steps the sum \\(a+b\\) exceeds \\(n+1\\)).  \nThus \\(L\\) cannot contain \\(n+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{x=c\\}\\cap S_n| = n+1-c .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) the same formula holds.  \nFor a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(2\\le c\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{x+y=c\\}\\cap S_n| = \\min\\{c-1,\\,n+1-c\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints of \\(S_n\\) on \\(x=c\\) are exactly \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(c+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\(b\\) can take the values \\(1,\\dots ,n+1-c\\), giving (6).  \nThe horizontal case is symmetric.  \nFor the diagonal, the conditions \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=c\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together imply \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le c-1\\), while the inequality \\(c\\le n+1\\) yields the upper bound \\(c-1\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le n+1\\) then the number of admissible integer pairs is exactly the smaller of the two distances from the ends of the interval, which is (7). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Covering capacity of non\u2011sunny lines)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\subseteq\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) be a collection of \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.  \nThen the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is at most \\(mn\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each individual non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nTwo distinct lines belonging to the same family are parallel and therefore have **disjoint** intersections with \\(S_n\\).  \nLines from different families intersect in at most one point, so the total number of distinct points covered by the whole family cannot exceed the sum of the individual maxima, i.e. \\(mn\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument (Proof by contradiction)\n\nAssume that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and containing **at least three** sunny lines.  \nSelect three distinct sunny lines and denote them by \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N:=\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\); then \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-3\\) and every line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\n1. By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\); therefore the three sunny lines together cover at most \\(3n\\) points.\n\n2. By Lemma\u202f3 the family \\(\\mathcal N\\) of \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-3)n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n3n+(n-3)n = n^{2}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\nNow compare this upper bound with the actual size of \\(S_n\\).  \nFrom (3),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}+\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn^{2} - |S_n| = n^{2}-\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}-n}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (8) is **strictly larger** than \\(|S_n|\\); at first sight this does not yield a contradiction.  \nThe missing ingredient is that the estimate (8) is *not attainable* when three sunny lines are present, because the three sunny lines are each non\u2011parallel to any non\u2011sunny line, and consequently each point of \\(S_n\\) that lies on a sunny line is **exclusive** to that line (no non\u2011sunny line can pass through the same point).  \n\nTherefore the total number of distinct points covered is actually  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L} (L\\cap S_n)\\,|\n= |\\,\\bigcup_{i=1}^{3}(L_i\\cap S_n)\\,|\n   + |\\,\\bigcup_{M\\in\\mathcal N}(M\\cap S_n)\\,|\n\\le 3n + (n-3)n - 3,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nthe subtraction of at least three accounts for the fact that the three sunny lines cannot share any point of \\(S_n\\) with each other nor with any non\u2011sunny line (otherwise a shared point would lie on two d