ction \\(d_{2}\\) (a vertical) in at most one point, and it can intersect a line of direction \\(d_{3}\\) (a diagonal) in at most one point.  \n\n   Consider the \\(n+1\\) distinct lines of direction \\(d_{3}\\) given by the equations\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+2 .\n   \\]\n   (Only the first \\(n\\) of them intersect the set \\(T_{n}\\); the last one, \\(x+y=n+2\\), does not contain any point of \\(T_{n}\\) but will be useful for the counting argument.)  \n\n   Each sunny line can meet at most one of these \\(n+1\\) diagonals, because two distinct intersections with the same family of parallel lines would force the sunny line itself to be parallel to that family, contradicting its definition.  \n   Hence the total number of intersection points between the \\(n\\) sunny lines and the \\(n+1\\) diagonals is at most \\(n\\).\n\n   On the other hand, every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on exactly one of the first \\(n\\) diagonals (the one with sum equal to its coordinates).  \n   Since the family of \\(n\\) lines is required to cover **all** points of \\(T_{n}\\), each of the \\( \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be an intersection of a line from the family with the diagonal that contains it.  \n   Therefore the total number of such intersections is at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n,\n   \\]\n   which contradicts the upper bound obtained from the pigeon\u2011hole principle.  \n\n   Hence the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny is impossible; at least one line must be non\u2011sunny. Consequently\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+2\\) define the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) each contain points of \\(T_{n}\\); \\(\\ell_{n+2}\\) does not intersect \\(T_{n}\\) but will be used in the counting argument.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}:\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad \nd_{2}:\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad \nd_{3}:\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a collection \\(\\mathcal{L}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition of the problem and such that **all** of them are sunny; i.e. \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to the family \\(\\{\\ell_{s}\\}_{s}\\), then \\(L\\) meets each \\(\\ell_{s}\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct intersection points of \\(L\\) with two members of the parallel family \\(\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\\) would lie on a line parallel to that family, forcing \\(L\\) itself to be parallel to the family, contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n---\n\nBecause every \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny, it is not parallel to any \\(\\ell_{s}\\). By Lemma\u202f1 each \\(L_{i}\\) intersects the set \\(\\{\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+2}\\}\\) in at most one point. Hence the total number of intersection points between the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) and the \\(n+1\\) diagonals \\(\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+2}\\) is at most \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{\\,L_{i}\\cap \\ell_{s}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;2\\le s\\le n+2\\,\\}\\bigr|\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, the covering requirement states that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\). Since \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the unique diagonal \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\), each such point is an intersection of a line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) with the diagonal that contains it. Consequently the set of intersections counted in (3) contains at least all points of \\(T_{n