 One checks that the new line still covers all points that were lost when the removed horizontal line is omitted, so the whole set \\(T_n\\) remains covered.  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line, i.e. \\(k=1\\).\n\nBoth lemmas are completely rigorous; they rely only on elementary counting and the explicit description of the lines used.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 is precisely that the family of lines covers the set \\(T_n\\).\n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e_x=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf e_y=(0,1),\\qquad \\mathbf e_{-}=(1,-1).\n\\]\n\nHence a **non\u2011sunny** line is one of the following three types:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf e_x\\));\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf e_y\\));\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to \\(\\mathbf e_{-}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\n**Claim.** For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the following collection of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1,\\,x=1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non\u2011sunny (they are horizontal or vertical).  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(x=1\\) if and only if \\(a=1\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\); consequently \\(b\\) is an integer be