**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(n+1-a\\bigr)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((\\text{slope }0)\\), vertical \\((\\text{slope }\\infty)\\) and the line \\(x+y=0\\) \\((\\text{slope }-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (the case \\(k=0\\))\n\n**Claim.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is vertical, hence not sunny.  The lines are distinct because their\n\\(x\\)-intercepts are distinct.  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  By definition \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) vertical lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (the case \\(k=n\\))\n\n**Claim.**  For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(n+1-i\\ge 1\\), each \\(P_i\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\); moreover the\nset \\(\\{P_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) already satisfies the covering requirement, since every\n\\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) coincides with exactly one of the \\(P_i\\) (namely with \\(i=a\\)).\n\nChoose any real number \\(\\alpha\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\)