 together contain all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nObserve that for any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the point  \n\n\\[\nP_a=(a,\\;n+1-a)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(T_n\\).  Moreover, the collection \\(\\{P_a\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) **already**\ncovers \\(T_n\\): indeed, if \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\), so\n\\(b=n+1-a\\) and consequently \\((a,b)=P_a\\).\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n(Any \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\) will do; for definiteness we may take \\(\\alpha=2\\).)\n\nFor each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\; y=\\alpha x + \\bigl[(n+1-a)-\\alpha a\\bigr].\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(L_a\\) passes through \\(P_a\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\alpha a +\\bigl[(n+1-a)-\\alpha a\\bigr]=n+1-a,\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the \\(y\\)-coordinate of \\(P_a\\).\n\n*Each \\(L_a\\) is sunny.*  \nAll lines have the same slope \\(\\alpha\\), and by construction \\(\\alpha\\neq0\\) (so the line is not horizontal) and \\(\\alpha\\neq-1\\) (so it is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  The lines are not vertical because their slope is finite.  Hence every \\(L_a\\) is sunny.\n\n*The lines are distinct.*  \nIf \\(L_a=L_b\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\), then their intercepts would be equal:\n\\[\n(n+1-a)-\\alpha a=(n+1-b)-\\alpha b.\n\\]\nRearranging yields \\(\\alpha(b-a)=b-a\\), i.e. \\(\\alpha=1\\).  Since we chose \\(\\alpha\\neq1\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=2\\)), this cannot happen; therefore all \\(L_a\\) are distinct.\n\nFinally, because the set \\(\\{P_a\\}\\) equals \\(T_n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}L_a .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe two results above demonstrate that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the extreme\nvalu