"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following two statements  \n\n*   **Lemma\u202f1.**  \\(k=0\\) is always attainable: for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny, that covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n*   **Lemma\u202f2.**  If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny (i.e. \\(k=n\\)), then the covering condition cannot be satisfied for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nConsequently the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is a non\u2011empty subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) that contains \\(0\\) and does not contain \\(n\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use only vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   Each such line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and it contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\) fixed and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \n   Because the set of admissible points is precisely the union of the vertical strips \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\), the \\(n\\) vertical lines cover all required points.  \n   The lines are distinct, so the construction satisfies the conditions with \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that there exists a covering by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three special directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Hence any two distinct sunny lines intersect in a unique point, and no three of them are concurrent.  \n   The set of admissible lattice points contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points.  \n   Because a line that is not parallel to the three special directions can contain at most two of those lattice points (otherwise the line would have to be parallel to one of the three directions, a simple lattice\u2011geometry observation proved in Lemma\u202f3 below), the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) sunny lines and the admissible points is at most \\(2n\\).  \n   Since every admissible point must lie on at least one line, we would need \\(2n\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), i.e. \\(4\\ge n+1\\), which is false for all \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \n   The case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly: the nine admissible points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by three sunny lines because any sunny line can contain at most two of them.  \n   Hence a covering with \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe missing part of the full solution is to determine, for each \\(n\\ge 3\\), which intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can actually occur. This requires a finer analysis of how many admissible points a sunny line can contain and how non\u2011sunny lines can be combined with sunny ones; such an analysis is not completed here.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1.  Existence of a covering with \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) consider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\;x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_{a}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe admissible lattice points are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFix \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a}\\).  Conversely, every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) has its first coordinate equal to some \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence belongs to the corresponding \\(L_{a}\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}\\subseteq \\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}L_{a}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) are distinct, so we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines covering all required points.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable. \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2.  Non\u2011existence of a covering with \\(k=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal{L}=\\{ \\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct **sunny** lines such that every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\nA sunny line is, by definition, not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}:\\;y=0,\\qquad d_{2}:\\;x=0,\\qquad d_{3}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently no two distinct sunny lines are parallel, and no three of them are concurrent; any two intersect in a unique point.\n\nWe first establish a bound on the number of admissible lattice points that a single sunny line can contain.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3.  A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma\u202f3.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is not parallel to any of \\(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3}\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct lattice points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\) with integer coordinates, then the vector differences \\((a_{2}-a_{1},\\,b_{2}-b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{3}-a_{1},\\,b_{3}-b_{1})\\) are integer multiples of a primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\) of \\(\\ell\\).  Hence \\((p,q)\\) is an integer vector, and the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(q/p\\) with \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  The line \\(\\ell\\) therefore passes through infinitely many lattice points, forming an arithmetic progression in both coordinates.  In particular the set of differences \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\) between any two points on \\(\\ell\\) is a set of integer multiples of \\((p,q)\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, we have \\(q\\neq 0\\); since it is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, \\(p\\neq 0\\); and since it is not parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\), we have \\(p+q\\neq 0\\).  Thus the direction vector \\((p,q)\\) is a non\u2011zero integer solution of none of the equations \\(q=0\\), \\(p=0\\), \\(p+q=0\\).  Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to none of the three forbidden directions, which is consistent with the hypothesis.  \n\nNow consider the restriction that all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  If \\(\\ell\\) contained three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\), then moving along the direction \\((p,q)\\) would increase both coordinates (or decrease both) while preserving the sum \\(a+b\\) modulo \\(p+q\\).  Because \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) is a finite triangular region, the only way three collinear lattice points can all lie inside it is that the direction vector satisfies either \\(p=0\\) (vertical), \\(q=0\\) (horizontal) or \\(p+q=0\\) (diagonal).  Each of these possibilities contradicts the assumption that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\). \u220e\n\nReturning to the proof of Lemma\u202f2, Lemma\u202f3 implies that each line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) covers at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  Therefore the total number of incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) is at most \\(2n\\).\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Since every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) must lie on at least one line, we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le 2n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n+1\\le 4 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(n\\le 3\\).  The case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly: \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  Any sunny line can contain at most two of them, so three sunny lines cannot cover all three points.  Hence no covering with \\(k=n\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we know that \\(k=0\\) is always realizable.  \nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we know that \\(k=n\\) is never realizable for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a non\u2011empty subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) containing \\(0\\) and excluding \\(n\\).  Determining precisely which intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) can occur remains open. \u220e"
