            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### Notation  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Lemma\u202f1 (Two\u2011point bound for a sunny line)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nThe vectors \\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)\\) and \\((a_3-a_1,b_3-b_1)\\) are integer multiples of a primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\) of \\(\\ell\\); hence \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\) and the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(q/p\\) (with \\(p\\neq0\\)).  \n\nBecause all three points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), moving from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by the constant amount \\(p+q\\).  \nIf \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) then the three points cannot all lie inside the triangle defined by \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\); the sum would exceed the bound after at most two steps.  \nThus we must have \\(|p+q|=0\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\).  \n\n* If \\(p+q=0\\) then \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \n* If \\(p=0\\) then \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, again a contradiction.  \n* If \\(q=0\\) then \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal, i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, also a contradiction.  \n\nHence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f2 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the \\(k\\) sunny lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be \\(\\ell_{k+1},\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most two incidences with \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore the total number of incidences supplied by the sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\nFor the non\u2011sunny lines we may assume, without loss of generality, that they are vertical (any non\u2011sunny line can be rotated to a vertical one while preserving the set of covered lattice points).  \nChoose distinct integers \\(a_1,\\dots ,a_{n-k}\\) from \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and let \\(\\ell_{k+i}\\) be the line \\(x=a_i\\).  \nThe line \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); hence it covers  \n\n\\[\nm(a):=n+1-a\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nTo maximise the total number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines we take the smallest possible abscissae, i.e. \\(a_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\).  \nConsequently the maximal number of incidences contributed by the non\u2011sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k} m(i)=\\sum_{i=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-i)\n      = (n-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}\n      =\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding the contributions of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines, the total number of incidences that can possibly be supplied is at most  \n\n\\[\nI_{\\max}=2k+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, we need  \n\n\\[\nI_{\\max}\\ge |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\text{(by (1)).}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (3) and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n2k+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n4k+(n-k)(n+k+1)\\ge n(n+1)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nn^{2}+n+3k-k^{2}\\ge n^{2}+n\n\\Longleftrightarrow\nk(3-k)\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\).  \nMoreover, if \\(k=n\\) then there would be no non\u2011sunny line to provide the large contributions of (2); the inequality above would then read \\(2n\\ge n(n+1)/2\\), which fails for all \\(n\\ge3\\). Hence \\(k\\le n-1\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma\u202f3 (Construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\min\\{3,\\;n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   They are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_a:\\; \\text{through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(a,\\,n+1-a).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_a\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_a=\\frac{n+1-a-1}{a-a}= \\frac{n-a}{1-a}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\ge 2\\), we have \\(m_a\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(m_a\\) is finite (not vertical), and \\(m_a\\neq -1\\) (because \\(n-a\\neq 1-a\\)).  \n   Hence each \\(S_a\\) is sunny.  \n   Moreover, \\(S_a\\) contains exactly the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); no other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on \\(S_a\\) because any other point with first coordinate \\(a\\) would have a different second coordinate, contradicting the line\u2019s equation.\n\n3. **Counting lines.**  \n   The number of sunny lines constructed is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\{a\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\}| = k .\n   \\]\n\n   Together with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n4. **Covering property.**  \n   * If a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has \\(a\\le n-k\\) then it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   * If \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then \\(b\\) must be either \\(1\\) or \\(n+1-a\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)). In the first case the point lies on \\(S_a\\) because \\((a,1)\\) is on that line; in the second case it also lies on \\(S_a\\) because \\((a,n+1-a)\\) is on that line.  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nThus the required family exists for every admissible \\(k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Final conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f2 (which shows that no \\(k\\) outside \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can occur) with Lemma\u202f3 (which exhibits a construction for each \\(k\\) in that set, provided \\(k\\le n-1\\)), we obtain the complete answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\cap\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e.\n\n* for \\(n=3\\): the possible values are \\(k=0,1,2\\);\n* for every \\(n\\ge4\\): the possible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\nAll these values are attainable, and no other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) can be realised. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions 