es work).  \n   - For the remaining points we use the vertical lines \\(x=j\\) for \\(j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) of them).  \n   - Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) is already on the sunny line \\(L_a\\).  \n   - Every point with \\(a\\ge k+1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n   All vertical lines are non\u2011sunny, the \\(k\\) chosen lines are sunny, and the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).  \n   Hence for each \\(k\\) we have produced a valid configuration.\n\nSince we have exhibited explicit configurations for every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), the answer is complete.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Zero sunny lines are possible).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nAll \\(V_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All lines sunny are possible).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose a slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(i\\neq j)\n\\]\n\n(e.g. \\(\\alpha_i=i\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i (x-i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,1)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  \nSince each \\(\\alpha_i\\) avoids the three forbidden directions, every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nConsequently we have a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering the \\(n\\) points \\((i,1)\\); the remaining points of \\(S_n\\) are automatically covered because each of them belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\) (the lines are not required to be disjoint).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (For any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix such a \\(k\\).  \n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y-1=i\\,(x-i) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(i\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n2. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   For every integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j .\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(V_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct because the abscissae differ.\n\n3. **Coverage of \\(S_n\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) (by construction \\(S_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and has a non\u2011zero slope, so it meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) at exactly one point; that point is \\((a,b)\\) for the appropriate choice of \\(b\\)).  \n   * If \\(k+1\\le a\\le n\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   In both cases the point is covered by one of the chosen lines.\n\nThus we have produced \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, that cover every point of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemmas\u202f1,\u202f2 and\u202f3 together show that for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n