ng points, the total number of points in \\(S_{n}\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). The \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most one whole set \\(T_{c}\\) each, i.e. at most \\(\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points. If even a single sunny line is present, at least one of the sets \\(T_{c}\\) would have to be split between a non\u2011sunny line and a sunny line, forcing the sunny line to contain **more than one** point of that \\(T_{c}\\), contradicting Lemma\u202f1. Hence no sunny line can appear; i.e. \\(k\\) must be \\(0\\).\n\nCombining the two parts, the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_{n}\\) is the disjoint union of the sets \\(T_{c}\\) \\((c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_{c}|=c-1\\ge 2\\qquad\\text{for every }c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|T_{c}|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line (i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThen for each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap T_{c}\\) contains **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_{c}\\) satisfy the linear equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) contained two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) of \\(T_{c}\\), then both would satisfy the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx+y=c,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{equation of }\\ell .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points determine a unique line; consequently \\(\\ell\\) would have to coincide with the line \\(x+y=c\\).  \nBut \\(x+y=c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the set \\(T_{c}\\) contains at least two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (2) we have \\(|T_{c}|=c-1\\ge 2\\) because \\(c\\ge 2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{\\,\\ell_{c}: x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Impossibility of any sunny line)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition of the problem.  \nThen every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non\u2011sunny; consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line; denote the set of sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal S\\) and the set of non\u2011sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) with \\(|\\mathcal S|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|\\le n-1\\).\n\nBecause the lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are non\u2011sunny, each of them is either vertical, horizontal, or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nOnly a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain **more than one** point of a given set \\(T_{c}\\); a vertical or horizontal line meets a set \\(T_{c}\\) in at most 