            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_{n}\\) is the disjoint union of the sets \\(T_{c}\\) \\((c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_{c}|=c-1\\ge 2\\qquad\\text{for every }c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|T_{c}|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line (i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThen for each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap T_{c}\\) contains **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_{c}\\) satisfy the linear equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) contained two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) of \\(T_{c}\\), then both would satisfy the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx+y=c,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{equation of }\\ell .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points determine a unique line; consequently \\(\\ell\\) would have to coincide with the line \\(x+y=c\\).  \nBut \\(x+y=c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the set \\(T_{c}\\) contains at least two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (2) we have \\(|T_{c}|=c-1\\ge 2\\) because \\(c\\ge 2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{\\,\\ell_{c}: x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Impossibility of any sunny line)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition of the problem.  \nThen every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non\u2011sunny; consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line; denote the set of sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal S\\) and the set of non\u2011sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) with \\(|\\mathcal S|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|\\le n-1\\).\n\nBecause the lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are non\u2011sunny, each of them is either vertical, horizontal, or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nOnly a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain **more than one** point of a given set \\(T_{c}\\); a vertical or horizontal line meets a set \\(T_{c}\\) in at most one point (since all points of \\(T_{c}\\) have distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates).  \nHence, in order to cover all points of a particular \\(T_{c}\\), the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(c\\) (namely \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nIf any sunny line were present, at most \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) could be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Consequently at least one index \\(c_{0}\\) would have the property that **no** line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) with constant term \\(c_{0}\\).  \n\nConsider the set \\(T_{c_{0}}\\). By Lemma\u202f2 it contains at least two points.  \nSince no line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) with constant term \\(c_{0}\\), each point of \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) must be covered either by a vertical/horizontal line (which can cover at most one point of \\(T_{c_{0}}\\)) or by a sunny line.  \nIf a sunny line covered two distinct points of \\(T_{c_{0}}\\), Lemma\u202f1 would be violated. Therefore at most one point of \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) could be covered by a sunny line, and at most one point could be covered by a vertical/horizontal line, leaving at least one point of \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) uncovered\u2014a contradiction to the covering requirement.  \n\nHence the assumption that \\(\\mathcal S\\neq\\varnothing\\) is impossible; all lines must be non\u2011sunny and \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Complete Answer)  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that any \\(k\\ge 1\\) leads to a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n**Thus the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO g