t two points.\n- **Reasoning:** Directly from \\(|T_{c}|=c-1\\ge2\\) for \\(c\\ge2\\).\n- **Verification:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\n- **Construction:** The family \\(\\mathcal L_{0}=\\{\\ell_{c}:x+y=c\\mid c=2,\\dots,n+1\\}\\).\n- **Verification:** Each \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and clearly covers exactly the points of \\(T_{c}\\). The family has \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers all of \\(S_{n}\\). This lemma is correct.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f4 (Impossibility of any sunny line)\n\n- **Initial assumption:** Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) contains at least one sunny line.\n- **Key claim (critical step):**  \n  > \u201cOnly a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) can contain **more than one** point of a given set \\(T_{c}\\); a vertical or horizontal line meets a set \\(T_{c}\\) in at most one point (since all points of \\(T_{c}\\) have distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates). Hence, in order to cover all points of a particular \\(T_{c}\\), the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\u201d\n\n- **Why this is a Critical Error:**  \n  1. The conclusion that *each* \\(T_{c}\\) must contain a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is false.  \n  2. A vertical line \\(x = a\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y = b\\)) intersects **exactly one point** of each \\(T_{c}\\) (the point \\((a, c-a)\\) or \\((c-b, b)\\) respectively, provided the coordinates stay positive). Thus a collection of vertical (or horizontal) lines can together cover **all** points of many different \\(T_{c}\\) without any line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n  3. Consequently, the argument that the presence of a sunny line forces a missing \u201c\\(c_{0}\\)\u201d for which no line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) exists, and that this leads to an uncovered point, is invalid. The uncovered\u2011point contradiction does not follow because the missing diagonal line can be compensated by vertical/horizontal lines covering the points of that \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) one\u2011by\u2011one.\n\n- **Impact:** This error invalidates the entire Lemma\u202f4, which is the cornerstone of the claim that \\(k\\ge1\\) is impossible. Since Lemma\u202f4 is unsound, the conclusion that the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\) is not justified.\n\n- **Further steps dependent on Lemma\u202f4:**  \n  The final theorem (that the answer set is \\(\\{0\\}\\)) relies directly on Lemma\u202f4. Because Lemma\u202f4 is false, the theorem is unsupported. No further verification of later statements is necessary; the proof collapses at this point.\n\n---\n\n#### Overall Assessment\n\n- The solution correctly establishes the existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\) (Lemma\u202f3).  \n- However, it fails to prove the non\u2011existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge1\\) due to a critical logical flaw in Lemma\u202f4.  \n- Therefore the solution does **not** rigorously determine all possible values of \\(k\\); the answer set \\(\\{0\\}\\) is not proven and is, in fact, likely incorrect.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The solution contains a critical error in Lemma\u202f4, rendering the overall argument invalid."
