            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{c}:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\bigsqcup_{c=2}^{n+1} T_{c}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_{c}| = c-1\\qquad (2\\le c\\le n+1),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nhence \\(|T_{c}|\\ge 2\\) for every \\(c\\). Moreover  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}|T_{c}|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a **sunny** line (i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThen for each \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the intersection \\(\\ell\\cap T_{c}\\) contains **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points of \\(T_{c}\\) satisfy the linear equation \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) contained two distinct points of \\(T_{c}\\), those two points would determine the unique line \\(x+y=c\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor every \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the set \\(T_{c}\\) contains at least two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nEquation (3) gives \\(|T_{c}|=c-1\\ge 2\\) because \\(c\\ge 2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid \\ell_{c}: x+y=c,\\;c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (No sunny line can appear)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition of the problem.  \nThen every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one sunny line; denote the set of sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal S\\) and the set of non\u2011sunny lines by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) with \\(|\\mathcal S|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|\\le n-1\\).\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Observation 1.*  \nA vertical line has equation \\(x = \\alpha\\). Since the points of a fixed \\(T_{c}\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates (they are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\)), such a line can meet \\(T_{c}\\) in **at most one** point.  \nThe same holds for a horizontal line \\(y=\\beta\\).\n\n*Observation 2.*  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=\\gamma\\). It meets \\(T_{c}\\) in all of its points when \\(\\gamma=c\\), and in none otherwise.\n\nConsequently, **the only type of line that can contain more than one point of a given set \\(T_{c}\\) is a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).**  \n\nNow fix a particular value \\(c_{0}\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2, \\(|T_{c_{0}}|\\ge 2\\). To cover all points of \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n1. Use a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) with constant term \\(c_{0}\\); this line covers the whole set \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) at once.\n\n2. Use other lines (vertical, horizontal, or sunny).  \n   By Observations\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f1, each such line can meet \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   Since \\(|T_{c_{0}}|\\ge 2\\), at least two distinct lines would be required to cover \\(T_{c_{0}}\\) if we do not employ a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nBecause we have only \\(n\\) lines in total and there are exactly \\(n\\) different sets \\(T_{c}\\) (one for each \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)), the following counting argument applies:\n\n- Suppose that for some \\(c\\) we do **not** use a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n  Then covering \\(T_{c}\\) would need at least two lines.  \n- For every other value of \\(c\\) we need at least one line (a parallel line) to cover the whole \\(T_{c}\\).\n\nHence the total number of lines required would be at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{for each of the }n-1\\text{ remaining }c\\text{'s})\\;+\\;2\\;(\\text{for the exceptional }c)=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich exceeds the allowed number \\(n\\). This contradiction shows that **for every \\(c\\) a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) with constant term \\(c\\) must be present**.\n\nTherefore all \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); none of them is sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem