tinct lines, each parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny), and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them because \\(a+b\\) determines \\(c\\). This exhibits a valid configuration with \\(k=0\\). Correct.\n\n**Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 No sunny line can appear.**  \nThe lemma assumes a collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and argues by contradiction that a sunny line cannot be present.\n\n* Observation\u202f1: A vertical line \\(x=\\alpha\\) (or horizontal line \\(y=\\beta\\)) meets any fixed \\(T_c\\) in at most one point because the points of \\(T_c\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates (and distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates). This is accurate.\n\n* Observation\u202f2: A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=\\gamma\\); it either coincides with a whole \\(T_c\\) (when \\(\\gamma=c\\)) or misses it entirely. Correct.\n\n* Using Lemma\u202f1, any sunny line also meets each \\(T_c\\) in at most one point.\n\nThus, for a given \\(c\\), covering all points of \\(T_c\\) without using the line \\(x+y=c\\) would require at least two distinct lines (since \\(|T_c|\\ge 2\\) for all \\(c\\ge 3\\); the case \\(c=2\\) is handled similarly). Because there are exactly \\(n\\) values of \\(c\\) and only \\(n\\) lines available, the pigeonhole principle forces the use of the line \\(x+y=c\\) for every \\(c\\). Consequently every line in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and none is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\nAll logical steps are justified; no hidden assumptions are made without proof.\n\n**Theorem \u2013 Final answer.**  \nCombining Lemma\u202f3 (existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)) and Lemma\u202f4 (impossibility of any sunny line) yields the set of admissible \\(k\\) as \\(\\{0\\}\\). This follows directly from the previous lemmas.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nEvery claim in the solution is either proved explicitly or follows from elementary combinatorial or geometric reasoning. No critical errors