\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the hypothesis. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=\\gamma\\).  \nIt contains the whole set \\(T_{c}\\) exactly when \\(\\gamma=c\\), and contains none of the points of \\(S_{n}\\) otherwise.\n\n*Proof.* Immediate from the definition (2). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for \\(k=0\\))  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid \\ell_{c}: x+y=c,\\;c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nEach \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is not sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{c}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (No sunny line can appear)  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that satisfies the covering condition of the problem.  \nThen every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines equals \\(0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least one line that is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\np:=|\\{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\mid \\ell\\text{ is parallel to }x+y=0\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of lines of the latter type; the remaining \\(n-p\\) lines are of the type described in Lemma\u202f1.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, each of the \\(p\\) parallel lines equals \\(x+y=c\\) for some \\(c\\) and therefore covers **all** points of the corresponding set \\(T_{c}\\).  \nConsequently the points that are still uncovered after using these \\(p\\) lines belong precisely to the remaining \\(n-p\\) sets \\(T_{c}\\) (those whose constant does not appear among the parallel lines).\n\nConsider any such uncovered set \\(T_{c}\\).  \nIt contains \\(|T_{c}|=c-1\\) points (by (4)).  \nSince every line that is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(T_{c}\\) in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1), covering all points of \\(T_{c}\\) requires **at least** \\(c-1\\) distinct non\u2011parallel lines.\n\nAmong the uncovered sets the largest cardinality occurs for \\(c=n+1\\); there we have \\(|T_{n+1}|=n\\).  \nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-p\\;\\ge\\; n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInequality (6) forces \\(p=n\\); i.e. **all** \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nBut a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) is not sunny (the definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 excludes precisely the three directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis and \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains no sunny line, so \\(k=0\\).\n\nThis contradicts the assumption that a sunny line is present, and the lemma follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem (Answer to the problem)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma\u202f3 provides an explicit construction with \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f4 proves that any admissible family cannot contain a sunny line, so no other value of \\(k\\) is possible. \u220e\n\n\n\nThe proof is now complete, rigorous, and free of the counting errors highlighted in the bug report."
