\]\n\n   Hence we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion.** Lemma\u202f1 excludes \\(k=0\\); Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable. Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (No configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that none of the lines is sunny.  \nHence each line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* diagonal:   \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsider a line of each type.\n\n*Horizontal.* If \\(L\\) is \\(y=c\\) and contains two distinct points \\((a_1,c),(a_2,c)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\), then both satisfy \\(a_i+c\\le n+1\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\na_2-a_1\\le n+1-2c .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a positive integer, the inequality forces \\(c\\) to be an integer and limits the number of admissible \\(a\\)\u2019s to at most \\(n+1-2c\\). In particular, for a **fixed** value of \\(c\\) the line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most one point of \\(T_n\\) that is not already on another horizontal line (different horizontal lines have different constants \\(c\\)). Thus each horizontal line contributes at most one new point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical.* The same argument with the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) exchanged shows that each vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contribute at most one new point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal.* If \\(L\\) is \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\), the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) are the integer solutions of  \n\n\\[\na+b=c,\\qqua