he remaining points of \\(T_n\\). We use three families of forbidden\u2011direction lines.\n\n*Vertical lines.* For each integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n\\[\nk+1\\le c\\le n-k,\n\\]\n\nintroduce the line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\; x=c .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, vertical (hence non\u2011sunny), and each contains the point \\((c,1)\\) which is not yet covered (because \\(c>k\\)). The number of such vertical lines is  \n\n\\[\nn-k-1\\quad\\text{(if }k\\le n-1\\text{; otherwise this set is empty)}.\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal line.* Introduce a single horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y = n-k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n-k\\ge 1\\), the point \\((1,n-k)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and is not covered by the sunny lines (the sunny lines only reach the first row up to column \\(k\\)). Thus \\(H\\) adds the needed point of the first column beyond the sunny part.\n\n*Diagonal line.* Finally add the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\; x+y = n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll lattice points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\); these are precisely the points on the hypotenuse of the triangle. None of these points has been covered yet, because the sunny lines lie strictly below the hypotenuse (their intercepts are chosen so that they pass through \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\le k\\)), and the vertical/horizontal lines lie on the first row or column. Hence \\(D\\) covers all remaining points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.3. Counting the lines\n\nThe total number of lines constructed is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}} \\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-k-1)}_{\\text{vertical (if any)}} \\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{horizontal}} \\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n= n .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct:\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines have distinct slopes;\n* the vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates;\n* the horizontal line has slope \\(0\\), which does not appear among the sunny lines;\n* the diagonal line has slope \\(-1\\), also absent from the other families.\n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=0\\) cannot occur.  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
