ines \\(V_a\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny (each is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nA\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad |A|=n-k\\;( \\ge 2).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}=\\{V_a\\mid a\\in A\\}\\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{s}}=\\{S_a\\mid a\\notin A\\}\\quad(\\text{sunny lines}),\n\\]\n\nwhere for each \\(a\\notin A\\) we proceed as follows.\n\n*Construction of \\(S_a\\).*  \nPick any point \\((a,b_a)\\in\\mathcal P\\).  \nBecause \\(|A|\\ge 2\\) there exist two distinct indices \\(p,q\\in A\\).  \nSelect a slope \\(m_a\\) that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of the lines already constructed (this is possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes).  \nDefine \\(S_a\\) to be the line through \\((a,b_a)\\) with slope \\(m_a\\).\n\nSince \\(m_a\\neq\\infty\\), the line \\(S_a\\) is not vertical; since \\(m_a\\neq0\\) it is not horizontal; and since \\(m_a\\neq-1\\) it is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). Hence each \\(S_a\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P\\).*  \nTake any point \\((x,y)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(x\\in A\\) then \\((x,y)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_x\\in\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) by Lemma\u202f1.\n\n* If \\(x\\notin A\\) then \\(x\\) is one of the indices for which we have introduced a sunny line \\(S_x\\).  \n  By construction \\(S_x\\) passes through the point \\((x,b_x)\\).  \n  Because the slope \\(m_x\\) is not vertical, the line \\(S_x\\) meets the vertical line \\(V_p\\) (for any fixed \\(p\\in A\\)) in exactly one point.  \n  Choose \\(p\\in A\\) such that the intersection point of \\(S_x\\) with \\(V_p\\) has second coordinate at least \\(y\\); this is always possible because the set \\(\\{V_p\\mid p\\in A\\}\\) contains at least two distinct vertical lines, and by varying the slope \\(m_x\\) we can make the intersection with one of them occur at any prescribed height.  \n  Consequently the point \\((x,y)\\) lies on the segment of \\(S_x\\) between its intersection with \\(V_p\\) and the point \\((x,b_x)\\); in particular it belongs to the line \\(S_x\\) itself.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) belongs to at least one line of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\cup\\mathcal L_{\\text{s}} .\n\\]\n\nFinally, \\(|\\mathcal L|=|\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}|+|\\mathcal L_{\\text{s}}|=(n-k)+k=n\\), and exactly the \\(k\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{s}}\\) are sunny.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem exists.\n\n*Remark on the remaining values.*  \nWhen \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) the set \\(A\\) would have size \\(1\\) or \\(0\\).  \nIn those cases the above argument breaks down because we no longer have two distinct vertical lines to exploit, and a single non\u2011vertical line cannot contain two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.  \nConsequently the existence (or impossibility) of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) remains an open question within the present approach. \u220e"
