 total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most\n\\[\n(n-1)n+1=n^{2}-n+1.\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-n+1,\n\\]\nso some point of \\(T_n\\) would remain uncovered \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Construction for \\(k=0\\))\n\n*For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration with \\(k=0\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny.  For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for \\(2\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n*Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  Then there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(k=n-m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Use the \\(m\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=m .\n   \\]\n   These are non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(m+1\\le t\\le n\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = x-(t-1) .\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L_t\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_t\\) is sunny.  \n   The indices run over exactly \\(n-(m)=k\\) values, so we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nWe claim that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on one of the above lines.\n\n*If \\(b\\le m\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\n*If \\(b>m\\)*, set \\(t=a+b-1\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), we have\n\\[\nm+1\\le t = a+b-1 \\le n .\n\\]\nThus \\(t\\) is one of the indices used for the sunny lines.  Moreover,\n\\[\nb = a-(t-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y = x-(t-1),\n\\]\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, the total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1, the value \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  \nLemmas\u202f2 and\u202f3 provide explicit constructions for all remaining non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\): \\(k=0\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
