gh \\((t,1)\\), i.e. \\(L_t:\\;y=x-(t-1)\\).  These \\(k\\) lines are sunny.  \n   One checks that a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to a horizontal line when \\(b\\le m\\); otherwise \\(b>m\\) and setting \\(t=a+b-1\\) (which lies in \\([m+1,n]\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, the total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThese two parts together give the full description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (No configuration with exactly one sunny line)\n\n*If exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines is sunny, then the points of \\(T_n\\) cannot be covered.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume there is precisely one sunny line \\(L\\) and the other \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).  \n  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=c\\) gives \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on it.\n\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous and also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* Line of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\).  \n  From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(d\\le n+1\\).  For a fixed \\(d\\) the admissible pairs are \\((a,b)=(1,d-1),(2,d-2),\\dots ,(d-1,1)\\); thus at most \\(d-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the line.\n\nConsequently each of the \\(n-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines covers at most \\(n\\) points, for a total of at most \\((n-1)n\\) points.\n\n*Sunny line.*  \nLet the slope of \\(L\\) be a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms with \\(|p|,|q|\\ge 1\\) and \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-b'}{a-a'}=\\frac{p}{q},\n\\qquad\\text{so }|a-a'|\\ge |q|,\\;|b-b'|\\ge |p|.\n\\]\n\nHence the integer points of \\(L\\) inside the triangle \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) are spaced at least \\(|q|\\) units apart horizontally and at least \\(|p|\\) units apart vertically.  Therefore the number of such points is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\) (the excluded slopes correspond to \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=1\\)).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor < n\\).\n\nThus the sunny line can contribute at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) new points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nPutting the two estimates together, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)n+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n< n^2-n+n = n^2 .\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^2-n\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nso \\(|T_n|\\) exceeds the maximal possible coverage, a contradiction.  Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line does not exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\n*For every \\(n\\ge3\\) there is a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny, that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Hence the whole set \\(T_n\\) is covered. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for every \\(2\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n*Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  Then there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite \\(k=n-m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n   Use the \\(m\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=m .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(m+1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y = x-(t-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of each \\(L_t\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all \\(L_t\\) are sunny.  The index \\(t\\) runs over exactly \\(n-m=k\\) values, so we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le m\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\n*If \\(b>m\\)*, set \\(t=a+b-1\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nm+1\\le a+b-1 = t \\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\) is one of the indices used for the sunny lines.  Moreover  \n\n\\[\nb = a-(t-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y = x-(t-1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_t\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, the total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=1\\) is impossible.  \nLemmas\u202f2 and\u202f3 provide explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}\\cup\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
