            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three forbidden\ndirections:\n\\[\n\\text{vertical }(x=c),\\qquad\n\\text{horizontal }(y=c),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=c).\n\\]\nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\nThus the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.\nTherefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\n\\]\n\nand add a single line \\(L\\) through the point \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is different\nfrom \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nA convenient choice is the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\):\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1=\\frac{2-1}{1-n}\\,(x-n).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{1-n}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is\nsunny.  All points \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lie on the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\); the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).  Consequently the \\(n\\)\nlines cover \\(T_n\\) and exactly one of them is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is\nattainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non\u2011existence for \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\n#### Lemma  \nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,2)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise joined only by non\u2011sunny lines:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_1P_2 &: y=1 \\text{ (horizontal)},\\\\\nP_1P_3 &: \\text{slope } \\frac{2-1}{(n-1)-n}=-1 \\text{ (diagonal)},\\\\\nP_2P_3 &: x=n-1 \\text{ (vertical)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Computing the slopes of the three segments gives respectively\n\\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); each is one of the forbidden directions, so the\ncorresponding lines are non\u2011sunny. \u220e\n\nBecause every point of \\(T_n\\) must belong to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines,\nthe three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) require three distinct lines.  \nBy the Lemma, no sunny line can contain two of these points; therefore at most\none of the three required lines can be sunny (the line covering \\(P_1\\) may be\nchosen sunny, while the lines covering \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) must be non\u2011sunny).\nHence any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains **at most one sunny line**,\ni.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\), and we have\nproved that no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines.  Consequently,\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solu