All steps are explicit and correct.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Non\u2011existence for \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\n**Lemma.** The three points  \n\\(P_1=(n,1),\\;P_2=(n-1,1),\\;P_3=(n-1,2)\\) are pairwise joined only by non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*Verification of Lemma:*  \n- \\(P_1P_2\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (slope \\(0\\)).  \n- \\(P_1P_3\\) has slope \\(\\frac{2-1}{(n-1)-n}=-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n- \\(P_2P_3\\) is the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (undefined slope).  \n\nEach is parallel to a forbidden direction, so the lemma is correct.\n\n**Argument after the Lemma.**  \nThe solution states:\n\n> \u201cBecause every point of \\(T_n\\) must belong to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) require three distinct lines. By the Lemma, no sunny line can contain two of these points; therefore at most one of the three required lines can be sunny \u2026 Hence any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains **at most one sunny line** \u2026\u201d\n\n*Analysis of the reasoning:*\n\n1. **Correct observation:** The three points are distinct, so they indeed need at least three distinct lines in any covering family (a single line cannot contain two of them unless it is non\u2011sunny, as shown by the Lemma).\n\n2. **Missing justification:** From the fact that *among the three lines that cover \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) at most one can be sunny* the solution jumps to the global statement that *the whole family of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most one sunny line*. This inference is not justified.\n\n   - It is perfectly possible that the three required lines are all non\u2011sunny, while **other** lines in the family (which do not pass through any of \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\)) are sunny.  \n   - The argument does not show that any sunny line must pass through at least one of the three special points, nor does it limit the number of sunny lines that could be used to cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Consequently, the conclusion \\(k\\le 1\\) is not logically derived from the Lemma; a gap remains in the proof.\n\n*Classification:* This is a **Justification Gap**. The step\u2019s conclusion may be true, but the provided reasoning is insufficient to establish it.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion\n\nThe solution correctly demonstrates the realizability of \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). However, the proof that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) is incomplete, as explained above. Because the central claim that \\(k\\le 1\\) is not rigorously justified, the overall solution is not valid."
