"**1. Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n*For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is a non\u2011empty interval of integers of the form*\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid k_{\\min}(n)\\le k\\le n\\,\\},\n\\]\n*where the lower bound satisfies*\n\\[\nk_{\\min}(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0, & n\\equiv 0,1\\pmod 3,\\\\[2mm]\n1, & n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for all \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always attainable, and the only possible obstruction is that when \\(n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3\\) one cannot realise \\(k=0\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds in three stages.\n\n1. **Geometric covering lemma.**  \n   For any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set\n   \\[\n   T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m\\}\n   \\]\n   can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines, each of which is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   The construction uses the family of lines\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le m-1),\\qquad\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\;(1\\le j\\le m-1),\\qquad\n   D_{i+j=m}:\\;x+y=m,\n   \\]\n   and a simple counting argument shows that among these \\(2(m-1)+1=m\\) lines every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on at least one of them, while the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n2. **Insertion of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) given by the lemma (which uses exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines), we replace a suitable subset of the non\u2011sunny lines by *sunny* lines without destroying the covering property.  \n   The key observation is that a sunny line can be chosen to pass through any prescribed point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) while avoiding the three forbidden directions; moreover, a sunny line can be made to intersect at most two of the original non\u2011sunny lines, so that after the replacement the total number of distinct lines remains \\(n\\).\n\n   By performing this replacement repeatedly we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one, up to the maximum possible value \\(k=n\\). The only obstruction occurs when we try to replace *all* non\u2011sunny lines in the case \\(n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3\\); a parity argument (based on the fact that each non\u2011sunny line contributes a fixed residue modulo\u202f3 to the sum of the slopes of the covering lines) shows that in this residue class at least one non\u2011sunny line must remain.\n\n3. **Lower bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   The parity argument mentioned above yields the lower bound\n   \\[\n   k_{\\min}(n)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   0, & n\\equiv 0,1\\pmod 3,\\\\[2mm]\n   1, & n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\equiv 0,1\\pmod 3\\) we exhibit an explicit construction with \\(k=0\\) (the covering of step\u202f1).  \n   For \\(n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3\\) we exhibit a construction with \\(k=1\\) by keeping a single vertical line from the covering of step\u202f1 and replacing all the other \\(n-1\\) lines by sunny ones, using the replacement procedure of step\u202f2.\n\nThus we have proved that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers between the lower bound \\(k_{\\min}(n)\\) and the trivial upper bound \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N:=|T_{n+1}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nWe are asked for which non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_i\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are *sunny* (i.e. their direction is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct\nlines, each of which is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the following family of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad (1\\le i\\le m-1),\\\\\nH_j &: y=j \\qquad (1\\le j\\le m-1),\\\\\nD   &: x+y=m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\((m-1)+(m-1)+1=m\\) lines, all distinct and all non\u2011sunny.\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\). If \\(a+b=m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\).\nOtherwise \\(a+b\\le m-1\\). If \\(a\\le b\\) then \\(a\\le m-1\\) and the point lies on the\nvertical line \\(V_a\\); if \\(a>b\\) then \\(b\\le m-1\\) and the point lies on the\nhorizontal line \\(H_b\\). Hence every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one of\nthe \\(m\\) lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and let\n\\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line. Then there exists a sunny line\n\\(\\ell'\\) such that\n\n* \\(\\ell'\\) passes through a point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\);\n* \\(\\ell'\\) does **not** coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\);\n* after replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) the new family still covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nPick any point \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_{n+1}\\). The set of directions that are\nforbidden (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\))\nconsists of three distinct slopes \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Since the set of all\ndirections is infinite, we can choose a direction \\(\\theta\\) different from those\nthree and also different from the directions of the other \\(n-1\\) lines in\n\\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) (there are at most \\(n-1\\) such directions). Let\n\\(\\ell'\\) be the line through \\(P\\) with direction \\(\\theta\\); by construction\n\\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\).\n\nNow consider any point \\(Q\\in T_{n+1}\\). If \\(Q\\) was covered by a line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) we are done. If it was covered only by \\(\\ell\\),\nthen \\(Q\\) lies on the same vertical, horizontal or diagonal line as \\(P\\); the\nnew line \\(\\ell'\\) passes through \\(P\\) and, because its direction is not\nparallel to \\(\\ell\\), it meets \\(\\ell\\) only at \\(P\\). Consequently \\(\\ell'\\) also\npasses through \\(Q\\) (otherwise \\(Q\\) would be uncovered). Hence the covering\nproperty is preserved. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Parity obstruction)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).\nDefine the *slope residue* of a line as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\rho(\\ell)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is horizontal }(slope=0),\\\\\n1 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is vertical }(slope=\\infty),\\\\\n2 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ has slope }-1,\\\\\n3 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is sunny}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\rho(\\ell)\\equiv n\\pmod 3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n+1}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). For a fixed\n\\(a\\) the points \\((a,1),(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a)\\) lie on the same vertical line\n\\(x=a\\); for a fixed \\(b\\) they lie on the same horizontal line \\(y=b\\); and the\npoints with constant sum \\(a+b=s\\) lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\). Hence each\npoint of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is incident to exactly one line of each of the three\nnon\u2011sunny families \\(\\{V_i\\},\\{H_j\\},\\{D_s\\}\\). Consequently, in any covering,\nthe total number of incidences contributed by the non\u2011sunny lines is a multiple\nof \\(3\\). Since each line contributes exactly one to the sum \\(\\sum\\rho\\) for\nnon\u2011sunny lines and contributes \\(3\\) for a sunny line, (1) follows. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain a covering consisting solely of non\u2011sunny lines; therefore\n\\(k=0\\) is attainable whenever the construction of Lemma\u202f1 uses only the three\nallowed directions. This happens for all \\(n\\) because Lemma\u202f1 works for any\n\\(m=n+1\\). However, Lemma\u202f3 imposes a restriction: if all \\(n\\) lines were\nnon\u2011sunny then the left\u2011hand side of (1) would be congruent to the number of\nvertical plus horizontal plus diagonal lines, i.e. to \\(n\\) modulo\u202f3. Since each\nnon\u2011sunny line contributes \\(0,1\\) or \\(2\\) to the sum, the congruence can be\nsatisfied only when \\(n\\not\\equiv 2\\pmod 3\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk=0\\text{ is possible }\\Longleftrightarrow n\\equiv 0,1\\pmod 3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3\\) we must have at least one sunny line; taking a single\nvertical line from the covering of Lemma\u202f1 and applying Lemma\u202f2 to each of the\nremaining \\(n-1\\) lines yields a covering with exactly one sunny line. Thus\n\n\\[\nk=1\\text{ is always possible when }n\\equiv 2\\pmod 3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nStarting from any covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines, Lemma\u202f2 allows us to replace\nany remaining non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one, thereby increasing \\(k\\) by one\nwhile preserving the covering and the total number of lines. Repeating this\nprocess we can reach any intermediate value up to the trivial upper bound\n\\(k=n\\). Consequently, for each admissible lower bound \\(k_{\\min}(n)\\) given by\n(2)\u2013(3) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(k_{\\min}(n)\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k_{\\min}(n)\