            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N:=|T_{n+1}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nWe must decide, for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\), which non\u2011negative integers\n\\(k\\) admit a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n   the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Covering by non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\)\ndistinct lines, each of which is either vertical, horizontal or has slope\n\\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the following family of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad (1\\le i\\le m-1),\\\\\nH_j &: y=j \\qquad (1\\le j\\le m-1),\\\\\nD   &: x+y=m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\((m-1)+(m-1)+1=m\\) lines, all distinct and all non\u2011sunny.\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\).  \n\n* If \\(a+b=m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\).  \n* If \\(a+b\\le m-1\\) then either \\(a\\le b\\) or \\(a>b\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a\\le b\\) then \\(a\\le m-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line\n    \\(V_a\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(a>b\\) then \\(b\\le m-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line\n    \\(H_b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(m\\) lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and let\n\\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line. Then there exists a sunny line\n\\(\\ell'\\) such that\n\n* \\(\\ell'\\) passes through a point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that lies on \\(\\ell\\);\n* \\(\\ell'\\) is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\);\n* after replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) the new family still covers \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose any point \\(P\\in\\ell\\cap T_{n+1}\\) (such a point exists because \\(\\ell\\)\ncovers at least one point of the set).  \n\nThe set of forbidden directions consists of the three slopes\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). In addition, each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\) has\nits own direction; there are at most \\(n-1\\) of them. Since the set of all\ndirections in the plane is infinite, we can select a direction \\(\\theta\\) that\nis different from all these \\(n+2\\) forbidden slopes.  \n\nLet \\(\\ell'\\) be the line through \\(P\\) with direction \\(\\theta\\). By construction\n\\(\\ell'\\) is sunny and does not coincide with any line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\).\n\nNow consider any point \\(Q\\in T_{n+1}\\).\n\n* If \\(Q\\) was covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\), it remains\n  covered after the replacement.\n* If \\(Q\\) was covered **only** by \\(\\ell\\), then \\(Q\\) lies on the same\n  vertical, horizontal or diagonal line as \\(P\\). Because \\(\\ell'\\) shares the\n  point \\(P\\) with \\(\\ell\\) but has a different direction, the two lines meet\n  only at \\(P\\); consequently \\(\\ell'\\) also passes through \\(Q\\). Hence \\(Q\\)\n  stays covered.\n\nTherefore the family \\((\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\\) is a covering\nof \\(T_{n+1}\\) with one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Parity obstruction)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).\nDefine the *slope residue* of a line by  \n\n\\[\n\\rho(\\ell)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is horizontal }(slope=0),\\\\\n1 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is vertical }(slope=\\infty),\\\\\n2 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ has slope }-1,\\\\\n3 &\\text{if }\\ell\\text{ is sunny}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in\\mathcal L}\\rho(\\ell)\\equiv n\\pmod 3. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) satisfying\n\\(a+b=s\\) lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\); there are exactly \\(s-1\\) such\npoints. Similarly, for each \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) the points with\n\\(a=i\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=i\\), and those with \\(b=i\\) lie on the\nhorizontal line \\(y=i\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is incident to **exactly one** line of\neach of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=s\\mid2\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the total number of incidences contributed by the non\u2011sunny lines in any\ncovering is a multiple of \\(3\\).  \n\nEach horizontal line contributes \\(0\\) to the sum \\(\\sum\\rho\\), each vertical\nline contributes \\(1\\), each diagonal line contributes \\(2\\), and each sunny\nline contributes \\(3\\equiv0\\pmod3\\). Therefore the left\u2011hand side of (1) is\ncongruent modulo \\(3\\) to the number of lines in the covering, i.e. to \\(n\\).\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*Existence of a covering with no sunny lines.*  \nApplying Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=n+1\\) yields a covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) by exactly\n\\(n\\) lines, all of which are vertical, horizontal or diagonal; thus \\(k=0\\) is\nrealised whenever such a covering does not contradict Lemma\u202f3.\n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, the left\u2011hand side of (1) is a sum of numbers\nfrom \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\). Modulo \\(3\\) this sum can equal \\(n\\) only when\n\\(n\\not\\equiv2\\pmod3\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk=0\\text{ is possible }\\Longleftrightarrow n\\equiv0,1\\pmod3. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Existence of a covering with exactly one sunny line when \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\).*  \nStart from the covering given by Lemma\u202f1 (which uses \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines).\nChoose any one of its vertical lines and keep it unchanged.  \nApply Lemma\u202f2 to each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines; each application replaces\na non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one while preserving the covering property.\nAfter the \\(n-1\\) replacements we obtain a covering with precisely one sunny\nline. Thus\n\n\\[\nk=1\\text{ is always attainable when }n\\equiv2\\pmod3. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Increasing the number of sunny lines.*  \nSuppose we have a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k<n\\)).  \nIf a non\u2011sunny line remains, Lemma\u202f2 allows us to replace it by a sunny line,\nthereby obtaining a covering with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines. Repeating this step we\ncan reach any integer \\(k'\\) with \\(k\\le k'\\le n\\). Consequently, for each\nadmissible lower bound \\(k_{\\min}(n)\\) given by (2)\u2013(3) all integers between\n\\(k_{\\min}(n)\\) and \\(n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k_{\\min }(n)\\le k\\le n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min }(n)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0, & n\\equiv0\\text{ or }1\\pmod3,\\\\[2mm]\n1, & n\\equiv2\\pmod3 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn words:\n\n* If \\(n\\equiv0\\) or \\(1\\pmod3\\) then every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n  can be realised.\n* If \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) then the possible values are \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n\\); the\n  value \\(k=0\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the problem is completely solved up to the unavoidable parity\nrestriction described above. \u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
