y \\(\\ell\\) become uncovered after the replacement. The lemma\u2019s conclusion that the covering property is preserved is therefore unsupported and, in fact, false.\n\n   - Because Lemma\u202f2 is used later to argue that any non\u2011sunny line can be turned into a sunny one while keeping a covering, the entire subsequent construction collapses.\n\n   - **Classification:** Critical Error \u2013 the step invalidates the subsequent reasoning that relies on Lemma\u202f2.\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Parity obstruction)\n\n1. **Definition of \\(\\rho(\\ell)\\).**  \n   - The mapping of line types to residues \\(0,1,2,3\\) is well\u2011defined.\n\n2. **Claim of congruence (1).**  \n   - The proof attempts to count incidences of points with three families \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\) and deduce that the sum of residues modulo\u202f3 equals \\(n\\).\n\n3. **Why the argument fails (Critical Error):**  \n   - The statement \u201cConsequently every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is incident to **exactly one** line of each of the three families \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\)\u201d is true for the *specific* families \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\) defined in the proof, but the lemma concerns an *arbitrary* covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(T_{n+1}\\). There is no reason that an arbitrary covering must contain, for each point, a line from each of those three families. In many coverings a point may be covered only by a sunny line, or by two vertical lines, etc. Therefore the counting argument that \u201cthe total number of incidences contributed by the non\u2011sunny lines in any covering is a multiple of\u202f3\u201d is unjustified.\n\n   - Moreover, the conclusion that the sum of residues modulo\u202f3 equals the number of lines \\(n\\) does not follow from the flawed incidence count. Hence the parity restriction (1) is not established.\n\n   - Since Lemma\u202f3 is used to claim that \\(k=0\\) is impossible when \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\), this part of the s