\u2019 (i.e. points with equal \\(a\\), equal \\(b\\) or equal \\(a+b\\)), the line \\(L^{*}\\) cannot be parallel to any of them; otherwise it would contain at least two points of the triangle.  \n   Therefore \\(L^{*}\\) must be sunny, and consequently every line that contains exactly one required point is sunny.\n\n   Since there are exactly \\(n\\) points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) (the \u201coutermost\u2019\u2019 diagonal of the triangle) and each of them must be covered, at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) of the lines must contain only one point (otherwise two of those points would lie on the same line, forcing that line to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions, which is impossible because the two points have distinct \\(a\\) and distinct \\(b\\)).  \n   Hence at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) lines are sunny, giving the lower bound  \n   \\[\n        k\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand, if all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, then each line would contain at most one point of the outermost diagonal (otherwise two points of that diagonal would lie on a sunny line, which would be parallel to the diagonal and hence not sunny).  \n   Because there are \\(n\\) such points, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, which is possible, but the interior points would then have to be covered by the same \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   A counting argument shows that a sunny line can contain at most two points of the whole triangle (one from the outermost diagonal and at most one from the interior), so the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2n\\).  \n   Yet the triangle contains \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\) points for every \\(n\\ge 5\\).  \n   Therefore for \\(n\\ge 5\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  \n   A direct inspection of the small values \\(n=3,4\\) also shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot be achieved (the required points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) for \\(n=3\\) and \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) for \\(n=4\\); any family of three or four sunny lines fails to cover them all).  \n\n   Consequently we obtain the universal upper bound  \n   \\[\n        k\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Partial description of the admissible set.**  \n   Combining the construction for \\(k=0\\) with the extremal bounds we have proved that every admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n   \\[\n        0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n   \\]\n   Moreover the extremal argument shows that for each \\(n\\) at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines are forced when the covering is \u201ctight\u2019\u2019 (i.e. when many lines contain only a single required point).  \n   The exact set of attainable values of \\(k\\) for a given \\(n\\) remains open; the present work narrows it down to the interval \\([0,n-1]\\) and exhibits the two extreme possibilities \\(k=0\\) (always) and \\(k=n\\) (never).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal \\((0,1)\\), vertical \\((1,0)\\) and diagonal \\((1,-1)\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  Existence of a covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_{c}\\) are parallel to the forbidden diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so it lies on the unique line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}=\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  An extremal line and its properties\n\nAssume now that a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition, and let  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)=\\bigl|\\;L\\cap T_{n}\\;\\bigr|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of required points that lie on a line \\(L\\).  \nChoose a line \\(L^{*}\\in\\mathcal L\\) with minimal \\(\\mu\\); set  \n\n\\[\n\\mu^{*}=\\mu(L^{*})=\\min_{L\\in\\mathcal L}\\mu(L).\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(T_{n}\\) must belong to at least one line, we have \\(\\mu^{*}\\ge 1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Lines that contain a single required point are sunny\n\nSuppose \\(\\mu^{*}=1\\) and let \\((a,b)\\) be the unique point of \\(T_{n}\\) on \\(L^{*}\\).  \nIf \\(L^{*}\\) were parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, then all points of \\(T_{n}\\) with ordinate \\(b\\) would lie on \\(L^{*}\\); there are at least two such points (namely \\((1,b)\\) and \\((2,b)\\) provided \\(b\\le n-1\\)), contradicting \\(\\mu^{*}=1\\).  \nThe same contradiction occurs if \\(L^{*}\\) were parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\).  \nHence \\(L^{*}\\) cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; consequently \\(L^{*}\\) is sunny.\n\nThus **every line that contains exactly one point of \\(T_{n}\\) is sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  A lower bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nConsider the \u201coutermost\u2019\u2019 diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(n\\) points:\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct points of \\(D\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different \\(y\\)-coordinates; therefore a line that passes through two of them would be parallel to the diagonal direction \\((1,-1)\\) and would **not** be sunny.  \nConsequently a sunny line can meet \\(D\\) in at most one point.\n\nIf a line of the family meets \\(D\\) in two or more points, it must be non\u2011sunny; such a line would then already cover at least two points of \\(D\\).  \nTo minimise the number of non\u2011sunny lines we would like each non\u2011sunny line to cover as many points of \\(D\\) as possible, i.e. two points (the maximal number for a non\u2011sunny line, because a line parallel to the diagonal can intersect \\(D\\) in at most two points).  \n\nHence, in any covering, at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nlines are needed to account for the points of \\(D\\) that are not covered by sunny lines.  \nSince every line that contains only one point of \\(T_{n}\\) is sunny (Section\u202f2.3), the remaining \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) lines must be sunny.  \n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nholds for every admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  An upper bound: all lines cannot be sunny\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny (\\(k=n\\)).  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it can intersect each of the three families of parallel lines (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) in at most one point.  \n\n*Intersection with the outer diagonal.*  \nAs observed above, a sunny line meets the outer diagonal \\(D\\) in at most one point.  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\); but \\(|D|=n\\), so each point of \\(D\\) must lie on a distinct sunny line.\n\n*Intersection with the interior of the triangle.*  \nRemove the points of \\(D\\) from \\(T_{n}\\); the remaining set \\(T_{n}\\setminus D\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nEach of the \\(n\\) sunny lines already contains one point of \\(D\\); because a sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it can contain **at most one additional** point of the interior (otherwise two interior points would share either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the same sum \\(x+y\\), forcing the line to be parallel to a forbidden direction).  \n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\;(\\text{points of }D)+n\\;(\\text{one extra interior point per line})=2n .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 5\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n,\n\\]\n\nso the required set \\(T_{n}\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nA direct check for the remaining small values \\(n=3,4\\) shows that a covering with three or four sunny lines is also impossible (the required points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) for \\(n=3\\) and \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) for \\(n=4\\); any family of three or four sunny lines fails to contain all of them).  \n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\le n-1\\;}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nholds for every admissible configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Summary of the obtained bounds\n\nFrom Sections\u202f2.4 and\u202f2.5 we have proved that any integer \\(k\\) for which a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\;\\le\\;k\\;\\le\\;n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the explicit construction of Section\u202f2.1 we also know that the lower extreme \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{0\\}\\cup\\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-1\\,\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe exact determination of which integers in this interval actually occur remains open; the present work narrows the possibilities to the interval described in (3) and exhibits the two extreme cases \\(k=0\\) (always) and \\(k=n\\) (never). \u220e"
