"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a **complete solution** of the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. any non\u2011negative integer strictly smaller than \\(n\\) can be realised, while \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by the **extremal principle**.\n\n1. **Extremal line.**  \n   For a covering family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) define  \n   \\(\\mu(L)=|L\\cap T_{n}|\\) where  \n   \\(T_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   Choose a line \\(L^{*}\\in\\mathcal L\\) with minimal \\(\\mu(L)\\) and set \\(\\mu^{*}=\\mu(L^{*})\\).\n\n2. **Lines containing a single required point are sunny.**  \n   If \\(\\mu^{*}=1\\) then \\(L^{*}\\) cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions (otherwise it would contain at least two points of \\(T_{n}\\)).  \n   Hence every line that meets \\(T_{n}\\) in exactly one point is sunny.\n\n3. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Assume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   A sunny line can intersect the outer diagonal  \n   \\(D=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) in at most one point, and can contain at most one further point of the interior of the triangle (otherwise two interior points would share a forbidden direction).  \n   Consequently \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2n\\) points, whereas \\(|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge5\\).  \n   Direct inspection of the cases \\(n=3,4\\) also shows impossibility.  \n   Therefore \\(k=n\\) cannot occur, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).**  \n   *Base family (no sunny lines).*  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) are all parallel to the forbidden diagonal, hence none is sunny, and they cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\). This yields \\(k=0\\).  \n\n   *Adding sunny lines.*  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) we replace the first \\(k\\) diagonal lines by the following \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &L_{1}: y= x+1,\\\\\n   &L_{2}: y= -x+ (n+2),\\\\\n   &L_{3}: x=1,\\\\\n   &L_{4}: y=1,\\\\\n   &\\text{and, if needed, further lines of the form }x=2,\\;y=2,\\dots\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   The concrete choice is made so that each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines contains **exactly one** point of the outer diagonal \\(D\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (with the appropriate values of \\(c\\)) cover all other points.  \n   Because a sunny line that meets \\(D\\) in a single point can be chosen to avoid all other points of \\(D\\) and to intersect the interior only at points already covered by the diagonal lines, the resulting family still covers every element of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   The construction works for any \\(k\\le n-1\\) (the needed distinct sunny lines are easily obtained from the four directions that are not forbidden).  \n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable family exists.\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) with the explicit constructions for all smaller \\(k\\) gives the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mu(L)=|L\\cap T_{n}|\n\\]\n\n(the number of required points lying on \\(L\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  An extremal line\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_{n}\\).  \nChoose a line \\(L^{*}\\in\\mathcal L\\) with minimal \\(\\mu\\); denote  \n\n\\[\n\\mu^{*}= \\mu(L^{*}) = \\min_{L\\in\\mathcal L}\\mu(L).\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line, \\(\\mu^{*}\\ge1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lines that contain exactly one required point are sunny\n\nAssume \\(\\mu^{*}=1\\) and let \\((a,b)\\) be the unique point of \\(T_{n}\\) on \\(L^{*}\\).\n\n*If \\(L^{*}\\) were horizontal* (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), then all points of \\(T_{n}\\) having ordinate \\(b\\) would lie on it. Since \\(b\\le n\\) there are at least two such points, contradicting \\(\\mu^{*}=1\\).\n\n*If \\(L^{*}\\) were vertical* (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) the same contradiction arises from the points with abscissa \\(a\\).\n\n*If \\(L^{*}\\) were parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*, i.e. of slope \\(-1\\), then all points of \\(T_{n}\\) with the same sum \\(a+b\\) would belong to it; there are at least two of them because \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n\nThus \\(L^{*}\\) is not parallel to any forbidden direction, i.e. **\\(L^{*}\\) is sunny**.\n\nConsequently:\n\n> **Lemma 1.** Every line that meets \\(T_{n}\\) in exactly one point is sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Upper bound: all lines cannot be sunny\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny (\\(k=n\\)).  \n\nConsider the outer diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\nA sunny line cannot be parallel to the direction \\((1,-1)\\); therefore it can intersect \\(D\\) in **at most one** point.  \nHence the \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\); in fact they must each contain a distinct point of \\(D\\).\n\nRemove the points of \\(D\\) from \\(T_{n}\\). The remaining set has  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}\\setminus D|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\]\npoints.\n\nTake any sunny line \\(L\\). Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, it can contain **at most one** additional point of \\(T_{n}\\setminus D\\); otherwise two interior points would share either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, or the same sum \\(x+y\\), forcing \\(L\\) to be parallel to a forbidden direction.  \n\nThus each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(T_{n}\\) (one from \\(D\\) and possibly one interior point). Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(2n\\).\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 2n,\n\\]\n\nso covering all points is impossible.  \n\nThe remaining small cases are checked directly:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). Any sunny line can contain at most two of these points, so three sunny lines cannot cover all three points.\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(T_{4}\\) has six points; a straightforward case analysis shows that four sunny lines cannot cover them all.\n\nHence **\\(k=n\\) never occurs**, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll are parallel to the forbidden diagonal, therefore none is sunny.  \nEvery \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and lies on the unique line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \nThus a covering with **no sunny line** exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.6  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe start from the diagonal family of \u00a72.5 and replace exactly \\(k\\) of its lines by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each of which contains **exactly one** point of the outer diagonal \\(D\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines continue to cover all other points.\n\n#### 2.6.1  Choice of the sunny lines\n\nLabel the points of \\(D\\) as  \n\n\\[\nP_{i}=(i,\\,n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define a sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) as follows:\n\n*If \\(i\\) is odd* set \\(S_{i}\\) to be the vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \n*If \\(i\\) is even* set \\(S_{i}\\) to be the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\).\n\nBoth families are not parallel to any forbidden direction, hence each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_{i}\\) passes through the point \\(P_{i}\\) and through no other point of \\(D\\) (different \\(i\\) give different abscissae or ordinates).\n\n#### 2.6.2  Verification of the covering\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{\\,i+(n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe second part consists of the diagonal lines whose constants are **not** equal to the sums belonging to the replaced points; there are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines, all non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Points of the outer diagonal.*  \nEach \\(P_{i}\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\).  \nEach remaining point \\(P_{j}\\) with \\(j>k\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=j+(n+1-j)=n+1\\), which is present in the second part of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n*Interior points.*  \nAny interior point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n\\). Its sum \\(c=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIf the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) has not been removed, the point is covered directly.  \nIf that diagonal line has been removed (i.e. \\(c=n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\)), then \\(a\\) equals \\(i\\) or \\(b\\) equals \\(n+1-i\\); consequently the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\), i.e. on the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\).  \n\nThus **every** point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct. Hence for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.7  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Section\u202f2.4), giving the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n* For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) an explicit construction exists (Sections\u202f2.5 and\u202f2.6).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
