ny.  \n\nAll points \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\nthe remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\). Hence the \\(n\\) lines cover\n\\(T_n\\) and exactly one of them is sunny. Thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\), each column \\(C_a\\) is non\u2011empty; indeed  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line intersects a fixed column \\(C_a\\) in **at most one** point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not vertical, i.e. its equation can be written as\n\\(y=mx+q\\) with finite slope \\(m\\neq 0\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\)\ngives a unique value of \\(y\\); therefore the line meets the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\) (and hence the column \\(C_a\\)) in at most one point. \u220e\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a line is non\u2011sunny, then it can contain **at most two** points of the set\n\\(\\{C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\}\\) belonging to distinct columns.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\). It meets exactly one column, namely \\(C_c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\). It meets each column at most once, but because the\n  points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), a horizontal line can intersect at\n  most two columns (the columns whose maximal \\(b\\)-value equals the constant\n  \\(c\\));\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\). Again, the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces such a\n  line to intersect at most two columns.\n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line can cover points from at most two different columns. \u220e\n\n---\n\nNow consider any admissible family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nEach column \\(C