t most two columns (Lemma\u202f2), so\nthe total number of columns that can be covered by the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines\nis at most \\(2(n-2)\\). Adding the two columns covered by the sunny lines gives at\nmost \\(2(n-2)+2=2n-2\\) columns. Since there are exactly \\(n\\) columns, this bound\ndoes not yield a contradiction directly; we need a sharper observation.\n\nObserve that the column \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\) consists of a **single** point.\nA sunny line that passes through \\((n,1)\\) cannot pass through any other point of\n\\(T_n\\) with the same abscissa (there is none), but it could pass through points\nof other columns. However, any other column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a<n\\) contains at\nleast two points, namely \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,2)\\) (provided \\(a\\le n-1\\)).\nIf a sunny line contains a point of such a column, it can contain **only one**\npoint of that column (Lemma\u202f1). Consequently, to cover the second point of the\nsame column we would need a second line intersecting that column.\n\nThus, for every column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) (which contains at least two\npoints), at least **two** distinct lines are required: one line for each of the\ntwo points, unless a non\u2011sunny line covers both points simultaneously. The only\nnon\u2011sunny lines capable of covering two points of the same column are the\nhorizontal lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\). The line \\(y=1\\) covers the points\n\\((1,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); the line \\(y=2\\) covers \\((1,2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\). Any\nother column would then be left with a single uncovered point, forcing a\nsunny line for each such column, which would exceed the total of \\(n\\) lines.\n\nA concrete counting argument is simpler: consider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,2).\n\\]\n\nThe three lines determined by each pair are respectively horizontal,\nvertical, and of slope \\(-1\\); all three are non\u2011sunny. Hence **no sunny line\ncan contain two of these three poi