            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{vertical }(x=c),\\qquad\n\\text{horizontal }(y=c),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=c),\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(L_a\\). Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and contain no sunny\nline. Therefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\n\\]\n\nand add the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1=\\frac{2-1}{1-n}\\,(x-n)\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through the points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{1-n}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nAll points \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=a\\);\nthe remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\). Hence the \\(n\\) lines cover\n\\(T_n\\) and exactly one of them is sunny. Thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\}=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\); in particular \\(|C_n|=1\\) and \\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\) for\n\\(a\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \nAny line that is **not vertical** (whether sunny, horizontal, or diagonal) meets\na fixed column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line can be written as \\(y=mx+q\\) with finite slope \\(m\\). Substituting\n\\(x=a\\) yields a unique value of \\(y\\); therefore the line intersects the vertical\nline \\(x=a\\) \u2013 and hence the column \\(C_a\\) \u2013 in at most one point. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2  \nIf a column \\(C_a\\) contains at least two points (i.e. \\(a\\le n-1\\)), then any\nline that contains two distinct points of \\(C_a\\) must be the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\), which is non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(C_a\\) have the same abscissa \\(x=a\\). The unique line\nthrough them is therefore the vertical line \\(x=a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Denote  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nv=n-k=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\n(The remaining non\u2011sunny lines, if any, are horizontal or diagonal; they are not\nvertical and therefore, by Lemma\u202f1, can contribute at most one point to each\ncolumn.)\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) can cover **all** points of the single column \\(C_c\\) and\nno points of any other column. Hence the \\(v\\) vertical lines can cover at most\n\\(v\\) whole columns.\n\nConsider a column that is **not** assigned a vertical line.  \nIf the column is \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\). By Lemma\u202f2 a line\nthat contains two points of such a column would have to be vertical, which is\nimpossible because the column has no vertical line assigned to it. Consequently\neach non\u2011vertical line (whether sunny, horizontal, or diagonal) can contribute at\nmost one point to that column (Lemma\u202f1). Therefore a column with at least two\npoints cannot be completely covered unless it possesses a vertical line.\n\nThe only column that may lack a vertical line is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\), which\nconsists of a single point. That single point can be covered by at most one\nnon\u2011vertical line, i.e. by at most one sunny line (using a horizontal or diagonal\nnon\u2011sunny line would increase the total number of lines without helping to cover\nany other column).\n\nHence the number of columns that are not assigned a vertical line is at most one,\nand each such column can be covered by at most one sunny line. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\), and we have\nproved that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le 1\\). Therefore the\nset of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
