a\u202f1\n> \u201cAny line that is **not vertical** can be written as \\(y=mx+q\\) with finite slope \\(m\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields a unique value of \\(y\\); therefore the line intersects the vertical line \\(x=a\\) \u2013 and hence the column \\(C_a\\) \u2013 in at most one point.\u201d\n\n*Correct.* The argument holds for all non\u2011vertical lines, including horizontal (\\(m=0\\)) and diagonal (\\(m=-1\\)) cases. Hence each such line contributes at most one point to any fixed column \\(C_a\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma\u202f2\n> \u201cIf a column \\(C_a\\) contains at least two points, then any line that contains two distinct points of \\(C_a\\) must be the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is non\u2011sunny.\u201d\n\n*Correct.* Two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate determine the vertical line \\(x=a\\). No other line can pass through both, so any line covering two points of a column with \\(|C_a|\\ge2\\) must be vertical.\n\n---\n\n#### Counting argument for the upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\n1. **Notation:**  \n   > \u201cLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\). Denote \\(k\\) = number of sunny lines, \\(v=n-k\\) = number of vertical lines.\u201d\n\n   *Correct.* The remaining non\u2011vertical lines are either horizontal or diagonal (both non\u2011sunny).\n\n2. **Capability of vertical lines:**  \n   > \u201cA vertical line \\(x=c\\) can cover all points of the single column \\(C_c\\) and no points of any other column. Hence the \\(v\\) vertical lines can cover at most \\(v\\) whole columns.\u201d\n\n   *Correct.* By definition a vertical line intersects only the column with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate.\n\n3. **Columns without a vertical line:**  \n   > \u201cConsider a column that is **not** assigned a vertical line. If the column is \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\(|C_a|\\ge2\\). By Lemma\u202f2 a line that contains two points of such a column would have to be vertical, which is impossible becau