(|C_a|\\ge2\\) for \\(a\\le n-1\\). This is a correct partition of \\(T_n\\).\n\n##### Lemma\u202f1  \n*A non\u2011vertical line meets a fixed column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point.*  \nProof: a non\u2011vertical line can be written \\(y=mx+q\\) with finite slope \\(m\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields a unique \\(y\\). Hence at most one intersection with the vertical line \\(x=a\\), i.e. with \\(C_a\\). The argument is rigorous.\n\n##### Lemma\u202f2  \n*If a column \\(C_a\\) contains at least two points, any line containing two of them must be the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is non\u2011sunny.*  \nProof: two points of \\(C_a\\) share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\); the unique line through them is \\(x=a\\). This is correct.\n\n##### Counting argument  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nDefine  \n\\[\nk=\\#\\{\\text{sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad v=n-k=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]  \nThe remaining \\(k\\) lines are non\u2011vertical (they may be horizontal, diagonal, or sunny). By Lemma\u202f1 each such line contributes at most one point to any column.\n\n- A vertical line \\(x=c\\) can cover **all** points of column \\(C_c\\) and no points of any other column. Hence the \\(v\\) vertical lines can fully cover at most \\(v\\) columns.\n\n- Consider a column that is **not** assigned a vertical line.  \n  *If the column is \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\), then \\(|C_a|\\ge2\\). By Lemma\u202f2 a line covering two points of this column would have to be vertical, contradicting the assumption that the column has no vertical line. Therefore each non\u2011vertical line can contribute at most one point to such a column, and the column cannot be completely covered.*  \n\n  Consequently every column with at least two points must have a vertical line assigned to it.\n\n- The only column that may lack a vertical line is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\), which consists of a single point. Th