ic reasoning; holds for all non\u2011vertical lines (including horizontal and diagonal).\n\n##### Lemma\u202f2\n- Claims that if a column contains at least two points, any line through two of them must be the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which is non\u2011sunny.  \n  *Justified*: two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate determine the vertical line uniquely.\n\n##### Counting argument\n- Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \n- Denote \\(k\\) = number of sunny lines, \\(v=n-k\\) = number of vertical lines.  \n- Observes that any non\u2011vertical line can contribute at most one point to each column (Lemma\u202f1).  \n- Notes that a vertical line can cover an entire column, and each vertical line can cover only its own column. Hence the \\(v\\) vertical lines can fully cover at most \\(v\\) columns.  \n\n- Considers a column without a vertical line.  \n  * If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\). By Lemma\u202f2 a line covering two points of such a column would have to be vertical, contradicting the assumption that the column lacks a vertical line. Therefore each non\u2011vertical line can contribute at most one point, so the column cannot be completely covered.  \n  * The only column that can be left without a vertical line is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\), which has a single point. This point can be covered by at most one non\u2011vertical line, i.e. at most one sunny line.\n\n- Concludes that at most one column can lack a vertical line, and that column can be covered by at most one sunny line, giving \\(k\\le 1\\).  \n  *Justified*: the reasoning follows directly from Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f2 and the counting of columns versus lines; no hidden assumptions are made.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion\n- Summarises that configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) exist (shown in Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2) and that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le 1\\) (proved in Section\u202f3).  \n- Hence the set of possible \\(k\\) is